2026-04-03 04:07:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] case "tool": {
2026-04-03 04:07:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// item.content is now an object, use it directly
2026-04-03 04:07:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	const functionResponse = {
2026-04-03 04:07:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		name: item.name,
2026-04-03 04:07:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		response: item.content
2026-04-03 04:07:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	};
2026-04-03 04:07:44 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	if (debugging) echo("[GEMINI] functionResponse", functionResponse);
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	contents.push({ role: "function", parts: [{ functionResponse }] });
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] break;
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this is better:
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Cleaner State: Your `rohaHistory` remains a structured, searchable object array rather
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] than a mix of objects and serialized strings.
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  Performance: You avoid unnecessary CPU cycles parsing and stringifying JSON every time
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] a tool is used.
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  API Compatibility: You pass the native object directly to the SDK, which is exactly
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] what the `google.protobuf.Struct` expects, preventing the `400 Bad Request` error.
2026-04-03 04:07:45 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:48 [nitro@ryzen5] what is normal in json world for an object called content be, string or object?
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the "JSON world" (and specifically in the context of LLM API schemas like OpenAI, Anthropic, or
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] Gemini), the standard convention is:
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. `content` as a String (The Default)
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] In almost all chat completion APIs, `content` is a string. This is the standard for
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] text-based messages:
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] {
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite]   "role": "assistant",
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite]   "content": "Hello, how can I help you?"
2026-04-03 04:08:54 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. `content` as an Object (The Multi-modal/Tool Case)
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] When an API needs to support multi-modal input (images, files) or tool
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] interactions, `content` is defined as an Array of Objects:
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] {
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite]   "role": "user",
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite]   "content": [
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite]     { "type": "text", "text": "What is in this image?" },
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite]     { "type": "image_url", "image_url": { "url": "..." } }
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite]   ]
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. The "Tool Result" Exception
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] When you are dealing specifically with Tool Results (your current issue), the field is
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] almost never called `content` in the API schema. It is usually called `result` or `response`.
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • OpenAI: Uses `tool_outputs` which is an object.
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Anthropic: Uses `content` as an array of objects.
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Gemini: Uses `function_response` which contains a `response` field that must be an
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] object.
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] Summary Recommendation for your `rohaHistory`
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since your `rohaHistory` is a custom internal structure, you should maintain semantic
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] naming to avoid confusion:
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • For Chat Messages (Text): Keep `content` as a string.
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • For Tool Results: Rename the field in your internal `rohaHistory` objects from `content`
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] to `result`.
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Why?*
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you keep naming everything `content`, your code will eventually crash because you will try to
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] call `.replace()` or `.substring()` on a tool result object, or you will try to parse a text string
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] as an object.
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • The "Clean" JSON World approach:*
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `message.content` = `string` (The text)
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `message.tool_result` = `object` (The structured data)
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you strictly follow this, you won't need to guess whether to `JSON.parse()` or not—you will know
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] exactly what the data type is based on the key name.
2026-04-03 04:08:55 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:16 [nitro@ryzen5] I would like to support array and plain string in history result content
2026-04-03 04:10:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] To support both a `string` (for text-only logs) and an `array` (for multi-modal or structured tool
2026-04-03 04:10:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] outputs) in your `rohaHistory` content, you should use a type-safe approach in your
2026-04-03 04:10:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] processing logic.
2026-04-03 04:10:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since `rohaHistory` is your internal source of truth, you can store whatever the tool returns. The
2026-04-03 04:10:34 [gemini-3.1-lite] trick is to handle the logic when you read that history to build the API payload.
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. Update the `tool` message structure
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] In `relay()` and `processToolCalls()`, store the data as-is.
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] // If the tool returns a string, store a string.
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] // If it returns an object/array, store that directly.
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] rohaHistory.push({
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     role: "tool",
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     title: "ToolCall:" + id,
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     tool_call_id: id,
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     content: result // This is now dynamic: string | object | array
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] });
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. Update `prepareGeminiPrompt` (The "Smart" Dispatcher)
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] When building the payload for Gemini, check the type of `content`. This prevents the `400 Bad
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] Request` error by ensuring the API gets exactly what it expects.
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] case "tool": {
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     let responseData = item.content;
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 1. If it's a string, try to parse it (in case it's valid JSON)
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     if (typeof responseData === 'string') {
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]         try {
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]             responseData = JSON.parse(responseData);
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]         } catch (e) {
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]             // If it's just plain text, wrap it in a standard object
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]             responseData = { text: responseData };
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]         }
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // 2. Now responseData is guaranteed to be an Object or Array
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // Gemini's 'response' field accepts either an Object or an Array
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const functionResponse = {
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]         name: item.name,
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]         response: responseData
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     };
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite]     contents.push({ role: "function", parts: [{ functionResponse }] });
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] break;
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Why this works for the "JSON World"
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Flexibility: You no longer need to decide if `content` is a string or object at the time
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] of creation. You decide at the time of consumption (the API call).
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Robustness: By adding the `try/catch` inside the dispatch, you handle "legacy" string
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] results (from old history) and "new" object results (from updated tools) seamlessly.
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] • API Compliance: You satisfy the `google.protobuf.Struct` requirement by ensuring that
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] even if a tool returns a simple string, you treat it as an object property (`{text: "..."}`) before
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] sending it to the API.
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] A Note on "Normal"
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the JSON world, avoiding ambiguity is king. If you have a field that is sometimes a
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] `string` and sometimes an `array`, you are creating a "Union Type."
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you find this becomes a headache, the most "JSON-idiomatic" way is to always store an object:
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Text: `{ "type": "text", "value": "the string" }`
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Structured: `{ "type": "data", "value": [...] }`
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] However, the approach in the code block above is the fastest way to support your existing history
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] without a massive refactor.
2026-04-03 04:10:35 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
