2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] If Windows opens the file without complaint but VS Code flags an error, it is almost certainly
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] because VS Code’s internal image previewer is more sensitive to trailing bits or
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] incomplete byte-alignment at the end of the LZW stream.
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the GIF specification, the LZW stream must be properly flushed. Your current `BitPacker` leaves
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] a partial byte in the `buffer` which is then pushed to `bytes` in `finish()`. This is correct, but
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] there is a nuance: the decoder expects the LZW data to be a multiple of the current `bitWidth`
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] or byte-aligned.
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Fix: Force Byte Alignment
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] After writing the `EOI` code, you should force the `BitPacker` to finish the current byte, and
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] potentially pad with zeros if the stream ends in the middle of a byte.
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] Update your `BitPacker.finish()` to this:
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] finish(): Uint8Array {
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// If there are bits remaining in the buffer,
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// they are pushed to bytes. This is technically sufficient.
