2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Real Culprit: The "Initial" Code Size
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] There is one specific detail in your `writeLzw` that VS Code might be rejecting:
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] this.file!.writeSync(new Uint8Array([4])); // Min code size
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] While 4 is correct for a 16-color palette, some stricter decoders expect the Logical Screen
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] Descriptor (in the `begin()` method) to perfectly match the color depth.
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] In `begin()`:
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] buf[4] = 0xF3; // 11110011 -> 4 bits per pixel (size 16)
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] `0xF3` is `11110011`.
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `1` (bit 7): Global Color Table present.
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `111` (bits 6-4): Color resolution (3 = 8 bits). This should be 3 for 16 colors.
2026-04-06 12:01:57 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `0` (bit 3): Sort flag.
2026-04-06 12:01:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `011` (bits 2-0): Size of global color table (3 = 2^(3+1) = 16 colors).
2026-04-06 12:01:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:01:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your `0xF3` is actually correct. However, try changing it to `0x73` (setting the resolution
2026-04-06 12:01:58 [gemini-3.1-lite] to 4-bit/16-color depth).
