2026-04-06 12:04:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] Yes, exactly. Let's look at the byte structure for `0xB3`.
2026-04-06 12:04:29 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] In the `begin()` method, the byte `buf[4]` is the Global Color Table Flag and Parameters.
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Breakdown of `0xB3` (1011 0011):
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Bit 7 (1): Global Color Table Flag (Present)
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Bits 6-4 (011): Color Resolution (3 = 4 bits per primary color, i.e., 8 bits total).
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is correct for 16-color/256-color palettes.
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Bit 3 (0): Sort Flag (Not sorted)
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Bits 2-0 (011): Size of Global Color Table (3 = 2^(3+1) = 16 colors). This is correct
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] for your 16-entry palette.
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why `0xB3` (or `0x73`) instead of `0xF3`?
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `0xF3` (1111 0011): The "Color Resolution" (bits 6-4) is `111` (decimal 7). This tells
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] the decoder that the original image had 8 bits per color channel (256-color depth), even though you
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] only provide a 16-color table. Some strict decoders (like VS Code's) see this mismatch and flag it
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] as a potential corruption.
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `0xB3` (1011 0011): The "Color Resolution" is `101` (decimal 5).
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `0x73` (0111 0011): The "Color Resolution" is `011` (decimal 3).
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `0x73` is the most "correct" value* for a 16-color image because it tells the decoder to
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] expect 4 bits of color resolution, which perfectly matches your 16-entry palette.
2026-04-06 12:04:30 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
