2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] Understood. Let's look at the bit-field for `buf[4]` again.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] The byte is defined as: `[M | cr2 | cr1 | cr0 | s | pixel2 | pixel1 | pixel0]`
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • M: Global Color Table Flag (1 = Present)
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • cr2, cr1, cr0: Color Resolution (number of bits per primary color - 1)
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • s: Sort flag
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • pixel2, pixel1, pixel0: Size of Global Color Table (size = 2^(N+1))
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want 16 colors, `size` must be 3 (since 2^(3+1) = 16).
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] So the last 3 bits are `011`.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want 4 bits per primary color (which is standard for 16-color palettes), then `cr`
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] is `4 - 1 = 3`.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] So bits 6-4 are `011`.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] Combined: `1 | 011 | 0 | 011` = `10110011` = `0xB3`.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] If `0xB3` (the "correct" value) fails, let's look at the only other possibility:
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Logical Screen Descriptor might be expecting the "Color Resolution" to be set to the
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] maximum (7), which is `111`.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] `1 | 111 | 0 | 011` = `11110011` = `0xF3`.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] If `0xF3` works on Windows but not VS Code, and `0x73` fails on both, the issue is likely not the
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] header, but the LZW stream itself.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] The "VS Code/Windows" difference:
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] VS Code uses a Chromium-based image decoder (which is very strict). Windows uses a GDI+ based
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] decoder (which is very permissive).
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] The most common reason a GIF is "valid" but rejected by strict decoders is trailing bits in the
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] final byte.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] Try this modification to your `BitPacker.finish()`:
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] We will force the final byte to be fully padded with `0` bits to ensure the decoder doesn't read
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] garbage at the very end of the stream.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] finish(): Uint8Array {
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // If there are bits remaining in the buffer,
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // the current byte is pushed.
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     if (this.bitCount > 0) {
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]         this.bytes.push(this.buffer & 0xFF);
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     }
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:02 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // IMPORTANT: Some decoders require the stream to be padded
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // to a full byte if it ends mid-byte.
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // The LZW EOI code is 17 (0x11).
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite]     // If the buffer ends here, we are good.
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite]     return new Uint8Array(this.bytes);
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] One last test:
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] Look at your `writeLzw` method again:
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] this.file!.writeSync(new Uint8Array([4])); // Min code size
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] Try changing this to `2`. Even though your palette has 16 colors (which usually requires 4
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] bits), sometimes the "Minimum Code Size" in the GIF header is interpreted as the bit-depth of the
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] data (which is 4), but some decoders calculate it as `log2(numberofcolors)`. If you
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] have 16 colors, `log2(16) = 4`.
2026-04-06 12:06:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
