2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] This is a classic "VS Code/Electron" quirk. The VS Code image previewer uses a very strict standard
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] that checks for specific GIF89a compliance. If the previewer works but flags an error, it
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] is almost certainly complaining about the Logical Screen Descriptor or Global Color
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] Table fields being inconsistent with the Image Descriptor.
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since `B3` works on Windows and displays in VS Code, your LZW stream is correct. The "Invalid"
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] error in VS Code is likely due to the Logical Screen Height/Width vs the Image
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] Descriptor Height/Width.
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] The "VS Code" Fix
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] VS Code's image component often checks that the `Image Descriptor` (which starts with `0x2C`)
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] perfectly matches the `Logical Screen Descriptor` (which starts with `GIF89a`).
2026-04-06 12:07:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] In your `writeImageDescriptor`, you have:
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] buf[5] = this.width & 0xFF; // Left
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] buf[6] = (this.width >> 8) & 0xFF; // Top
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] buf[7] = this.height & 0xFF; // Width
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] buf[8] = (height >> 8) & 0xFF; // Height
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Wait!* Look closely at your `writeImageDescriptor` logic:
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `buf[1]` and `buf[2]` are usually Left and Top offsets.
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `buf[3]` and `buf[4]` are Width and Height.
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your current code:
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] buf[5] = this.width & 0xFF; // This is actually the Width field
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] buf[6] = (this.width >> 8) & 0xFF; // This is actually the Height field
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] The Image Descriptor structure is:
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `0x2C` (Separator)
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `[2 bytes]` Left Offset
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `[2 bytes]` Top Offset
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `[2 bytes]` Image Width
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `[2 bytes]` Image Height
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • `[1 byte]` Packed Fields
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Correct your `writeImageDescriptor` to this:*
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] private writeImageDescriptor(): void {
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const buf = new Uint8Array(10);
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buf[0] = 0x2C; // Image Separator
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buf[1] = 0;    // Left Offset (low)
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buf[2] = 0;    // Left Offset (high)
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buf[3] = 0;    // Top Offset (low)
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buf[4] = 0;    // Top Offset (high)
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buf[5] = this.width & 0xFF;      // Width (low)
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buf[6] = (this.width >> 8) & 0xFF; // Width (high)
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buf[7] = this.height & 0xFF;     // Height (low)
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buf[8] = (this.height >> 8) & 0xFF; // Height (high)
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     buf[9] = 0x00; // No local color table
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite]     this.file!.writeSync(buf);
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] Why this fixes the VS Code error:
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] Your previous code was putting the `width` into the `Left Offset` field and the `height` into the
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] `Top Offset` field. Strict decoders see an image that is technically "outside" the logical screen
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] or has a 0x0 dimension, which triggers the "invalid" flag, even if the renderer manages to "guess"
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] the intended size and display it anyway.
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] Try this change; it is highly likely to clear the error in VS Code.
2026-04-06 12:07:24 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
