2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] The issue in your `rect` function lies in the `rightMask` logic and how it interacts with `x + w`.
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] When `(x + w) % 8` is 0, the bit-shift `(4 * 0)` results in a mask of `0xFFFFFFFF` (all ones),
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] which is correct, but if `x + w` spans across a byte boundary incorrectly, you are inadvertently
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] clearing or overwriting adjacent bits.
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] Furthermore, `~0 >>> (4 * ((x + w) & 7))` is problematic because `~0` is a 32-bit integer; if the
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] shift is 0, it behaves as expected, but the logic for calculating the end boundary needs to be
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] tighter.
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here is the corrected `rect` implementation:
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, c4: number): void {
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	let c32 = c4 * 0x11111111;
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	let x0 = x >> 3;
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	let x1 = (x + w - 1) >> 3;
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// Create a mask for the left side (start of the rect)
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// (x & 7) is the bit offset. We want to mask out bits to the left.
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	let leftShift = 4 * (7 - (x & 7));
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	let leftMask = (0xFFFFFFFF >>> (4 * (x & 7)));
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	// Create a mask for the right side (end of the rect)
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	let rightShift = 4 * (7 - ((x + w - 1) & 7));
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	let rightMask = (0xFFFFFFFF << rightShift);
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	for (let row = y; row < y + h; row++) {
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		let pos = row * this.span + x0;
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		if (x0 === x1) {
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			// Single word case: combine masks
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			let mask = leftMask & rightMask;
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			this.pix4[pos] = (this.pix4[pos] & ~mask) | (c32 & mask);
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		} else {
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			// Multi-word case
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			this.pix4[pos] = (this.pix4[pos] & ~leftMask) | (c32 & leftMask);
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			for (let i = x0 + 1; i < x1; i++) {
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 				this.pix4[i + row * this.span] = c32;
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			}
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			this.pix4[x1 + row * this.span] = (this.pix4[x1 + row * this.span] & ~rightMask) | (c32 & rightMask);
2026-04-06 12:36:03 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		}
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	}
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] Key Changes:
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1.  Mask Logic: Instead of just ORing (`|=`), I added `& ~mask` before applying the color.
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] This ensures that the background isn't "bled" into the rectangle if the bits were already set
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] (crucial for clean edges).
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2.  `rightMask` calculation: Changed to `(0xFFFFFFFF << rightShift)`. This correctly
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] isolates the bits from the start of the word up to the end of the rectangle.
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3.  Boundary Handling: By using `(x + w - 1) >> 3`, we accurately identify the last word
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] index (`x1`) that needs to be touched, preventing overruns on the right side of the rectangle.
2026-04-06 12:36:04 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
