2026-04-07 15:47:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:47:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] System Logic
2026-04-07 15:47:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Sync/Pedestal (GP2): Driven by `vidblit.pio`.
2026-04-07 15:47:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Luma+ (GP3): Active only during `irq` signal from GP2.
2026-04-07 15:47:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Pb/Pr (GP4/GP5): Active only during `irq` signal from GP2.
2026-04-07 15:47:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Negative Rail (GP6): Continuous 50% square wave (SM4).
2026-04-07 15:47:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Attenuation: Switching GP(n) to Low (0V) through dedicated resistors provides the
2026-04-07 15:47:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] "Half-Bright" effect.
2026-04-07 15:47:19 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:47:59 [nitro@ryzen5] how many pins for the trim attenuation?
2026-04-07 15:48:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] To implement the "Half-Bright" effect without crosstalk, you need 3 pins:
2026-04-07 15:48:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:48:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Pin 1: Attenuates Y (Luma).
2026-04-07 15:48:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Pin 2: Attenuates Pb.
2026-04-07 15:48:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Pin 3: Attenuates Pr.
2026-04-07 15:48:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:48:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] When these pins are High-Z (Input), the video signal is at full brightness. When set to
2026-04-07 15:48:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] Low (Output 0V), they pull the signal down through the attenuation resistors to create the
2026-04-07 15:48:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] "half-bright" effect.
2026-04-07 15:48:02 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:49:14 [nitro@ryzen5] which could apply 3 levels of trim or none to the signal? could we apply it to the common ground instead?
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] Yes. You can connect a single resistor from each of the three video lines (Y, Pb, Pr) to a
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] common node, then connect that node to a single trim-pin.
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Logic:
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   High-Z (Input): Trim pin is disconnected; full brightness.
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   Low (Output 0V): Trim pin pulls the node to ground; all three lines attenuate
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] simultaneously.
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Constraint: You cannot apply "3 levels of trim" to this common ground node without adding
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] a resistor ladder (e.g., 2 pins for 4 levels of trim). If you use only one pin, you only get two
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] states (Full vs. Attenuated).
2026-04-07 15:49:18 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:49:52 [nitro@ryzen5] how about pwm?
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] Yes. If you connect the trim resistors to a GPIO running PWM, you can achieve variable
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] attenuation.
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Logic:
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   PWM 100% (High): Pin acts as a reference; no current flows through trim resistors
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] (Full brightness).
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   PWM 0% (Low): Pin acts as a ground; maximum attenuation.
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite]     *   PWM 0-100%: The effective resistance to ground changes based on the duty cycle,
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] allowing you to smoothly fade the brightness or create distinct "trim levels" in software.
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Constraint: You must use a low-pass filter (RC) on the PWM pin to stabilize the voltage,
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] otherwise the video signal will flicker at the PWM frequency.
2026-04-07 15:49:56 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
