2026-04-07 15:54:06 [nitro@ryzen5] would a schematic diagram be appropriate or do electronic designs typically have a design section first
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] A design section is standard practice. It provides the context and constraints before jumping into
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] the schematic, ensuring the implementation meets the requirements.
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] Design Principles
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Impedance Matching: Outputs are designed to drive a 75Ω load. The series resistors (240Ω)
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] and shunt resistors (330Ω) create a source impedance that approximates the standard, minimizing
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] reflections.
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Chroma Symmetry: Pb and Pr use identical networks to ensure hue accuracy.
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Luma Pedestal: The combination of the `vidblit` tristate pin and the Luma+ PDM pin
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] ensures the 0.3V blanking pedestal is maintained even when Luma+ is at 0V.
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Attenuation: The trim node acts as a variable shunt to ground. Changing the DC voltage at
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] the RC-filtered GP6 node shifts the effective bias of the entire DAC, allowing global
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] brightness/contrast control.
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Charge Pump: A standard inverting voltage doubler configuration is used on GP7 to provide
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] a negative reference for the chroma DACs.
2026-04-07 15:54:12 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
