2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The 5-Bit Symmetry Problem*
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Repeating a 5-bit pattern twice (`in x, 5` then `in x, 5`) can only generate an even number of high
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] bits in the 10-bit output (0, 2, 4, 6, 8, or 10). That yields exactly 6 unique duty cycles.
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because we need 8 unique levels for our 3-bit color depth, we must generate
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] asymmetric patterns (e.g., 3/10 or 5/10) where the first 5 bits and the second 5 bits are
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] different.
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] To do this while staying under the strict 32-instruction limit of the PIO, we have to use advanced
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction sharing and fallthrough optimization.
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the mathematically perfect, 31-instruction Expander code. It uses `X` for the first 5 bits
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] and `Y` for the second 5 bits, achieving 8 distinct, monotonically increasing voltage levels.
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Perfected 8-Level Expander (31 Instructions)
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] .program expander
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; --- 0-7: Computed Jump Table ---
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp p0
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp p1
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp p2
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp p3
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp p4
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp p5
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp p6
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp p7
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; --- 8: Main Entry Point ---
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] public start:
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     ; (C code configures 'out null, N' here to strip bits)
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     out pc, 3            ; Pull 3 bits and jump to address 0-7
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; ==========================================
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; Asymmetric Levels (30%, 40%, 50%, 60%)
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; ==========================================
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] p2: set y, 0b00001
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp set_x_5          ; Level 2 (3/10 = 30%)
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] p3: set y, 0b01001       ; Level 3 (4/10 = 40%) - Falls through!
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] set_x_5:
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     set x, 0b00101
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp emit_xy
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] p4: set y, 0b01001
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp set_x_21         ; Level 4 (5/10 = 50%)
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] p5: set y, 0b11001       ; Level 5 (6/10 = 60%) - Falls through!
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] set_x_21:
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     set x, 0b10101       ; Falls through to emit_xy!
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; --- Push Asymmetric Pattern ---
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] emit_xy:
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     in x, 5              ; Shift first 5 bits
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     in y, 5              ; Shift second 5 bits
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     push                 ; Send 10 bits to Output SM
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp start
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; ==========================================
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; Symmetric Levels (0%, 20%, 80%, 100%)
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; ==========================================
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] p0: set x, 0b00000
2026-04-07 16:33:15 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp emit_sym         ; Level 0 (0/10 = 0%)
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] p1: set x, 0b00001
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp emit_sym         ; Level 1 (2/10 = 20%)
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] p6: set x, 0b10111
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp emit_sym         ; Level 6 (8/10 = 80%)
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] p7: set x, 0b11111       ; Level 7 (10/10 = 100%) - Falls through!
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; --- Push Symmetric Pattern ---
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] emit_sym:
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro]     mov y, x             ; Copy X to Y to make them identical
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp emit_xy          ; Jump up to the emit routine
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] How the Optimization Works
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Instruction Sharing: Notice how `p2` and `p3` both need `X` to be `0b00101`. By jumping
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] to a shared `setx5` block, we save instructions.
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Fallthroughs: `p3`, `p5`, and `p7` do not use `jmp` instructions. They simply set their
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] values and fall vertically into the next required instruction, saving 3 precious instruction slots.
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] This gives you exactly 8 distinct duty cycles (0%, 20%, 30%, 40%, 50%, 60%, 80%, 100%) perfectly
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] balanced for video, utilizing 31 out of the 32 available PIO instructions.
2026-04-07 16:33:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:36:11 [nitro@ryzen5] so set is limited to a 5 bit pattern, but we could set x with the other 5 bits, giving us a 10 bit pattern?
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, exactly. Because the `set` instruction is hard-limited by the hardware to 5 bits, you must
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] build the 10-bit pattern in two halves:
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. `set x, [first 5 bits]`
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. `set y, [second 5 bits]`
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Shift both into the Output Shift Register (`in x, 5` then `in y, 5`).
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Why we can't just do that for all 8 levels:*
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] If we explicitly wrote `set x` and `set y` for all 8 levels, the math looks like this:
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 8 Computed Jumps at the top = 8 instructions
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 8 Levels × 2 `set` instructions = 16 instructions
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 7 `jmp emit` instructions = 7 instructions
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Main loop (`out`, `in`, `in`, `push`, `jmp`) = 5 instructions
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total = 36 instructions. (Fails the 32-instruction limit).
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] That is why the code in the previous response uses "fallthroughs" and the `mov y, x` trick. It
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] allows us to build those exact 10-bit `X` + `Y` patterns while mathematically compressing the code
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] down to 31 instructions so it fits on the RP2350!
2026-04-07 16:36:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
