2026-04-07 16:59:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Sweet Spot Discussion
2026-04-07 16:59:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] To get the cleanest analog voltage, PDM relies on spreading the `1`s and `0`s as evenly as possible
2026-04-07 16:59:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] across the 10 cycles.
2026-04-07 16:59:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 16:59:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Minimizing Ripple: Clumping bits (e.g., `1111100000`) creates a low-frequency 1.4 MHz
2026-04-07 16:59:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] wave that is hard to filter. Alternating bits (`1010101010`) creates a 7.4 MHz wave that the RC
2026-04-07 16:59:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] network smooths effortlessly.
2026-04-07 16:59:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Mappings: The values above map to 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, and 9 high bits out of 10. They
2026-04-07 16:59:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] are arranged to maximize state transitions, keeping the charge pump load dynamic and the analog
2026-04-07 16:59:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] output flat.
