2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the timing breakdown of that exact code, which reveals a critical hardware distinction
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] between serial and parallel output.
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Blanking/Sync Timing
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `wait 1 irq 0`: While the Luma SM is in a porch or sync pulse, this instruction blocks
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] execution completely. It consumes 0 active cycles while waiting, perfectly halting the PDM
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] output.
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `set pins, 0`: When the line ends and the IRQ drops, the loop exits and this executes in
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly 1 cycle, forcing the pin to 0V for the duration of the blanking period.
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Active Video Timing (The Trap)
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] As written, your `active_loop` takes exactly 3 clock cycles, not 10:
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `pull block` = 1 cycle
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `out pins, 10` = 1 cycle
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `jmp pin, ...` = 1 cycle
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Hardware Trap:*
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] The instruction `out pins, 10` does not shift 10 bits serially over 10 cycles to a single
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] pin. It takes exactly 1 clock cycle and outputs 10 bits in parallel to 10 adjacent GPIO
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] pins simultaneously.
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] How to fix it for 10-cycle Serial PDM
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] To output your 10-bit pattern serially to a single pin at exactly 10 cycles per pixel, you
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] must shift 1 bit at a time.
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you enable autopull (threshold = 10) in your C configuration, you can drop the `pull`
2026-04-07 17:25:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction entirely. The perfectly timed 10-cycle loop looks like this:
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro] active_loop:
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro]     out pins, 1          ; Cycle 1
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro]     out pins, 1          ; Cycle 2
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro]     out pins, 1          ; Cycle 3
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro]     out pins, 1          ; Cycle 4
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro]     out pins, 1          ; Cycle 5
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro]     out pins, 1          ; Cycle 6
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro]     out pins, 1          ; Cycle 7
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro]     out pins, 1          ; Cycle 8
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro]     out pins, 1          ; Cycle 9
2026-04-07 17:25:28 [gemini-3.1-pro]     jmp pin, active_loop ; Cycle 10 (Checks IRQ, loops, and autopulls next 10 bits)
