2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] How to hit exactly 10 cycles:
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your Expander SM guarantees the 10th bit is always a `0` (`in null, 1`), you can use a
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] hardware trick called "Stall on Zero" to eliminate the `pull`, the `jmp`, and the `set`
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] entirely.
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  Enable Autopull: Set the OSR threshold to 10 in your C code. This removes the need for
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] `pull block`.
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  Rely on DMA Pacing: Program your DMA to send exactly the number of pixels in your
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] active line, then stop.
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3.  The Stall: After the 10th bit of the last pixel is shifted out (which is a `0`), the
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] State Machine wraps and hits the first `out pins, 1` for the next pixel. Because the DMA stopped,
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] the FIFO is empty. The State Machine stalls.
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4.  The Result: The pin remains frozen at that final `0` (0V) for the entire blanking
2026-04-07 17:29:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] period until the DMA sends the next line.
