2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] To transition this hardware architecture into C++, we need to orchestrate the RP2350's hardware
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] blocks to run autonomously. The CPU's only job during active video should be filling the
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] framebuffer for the next frame.
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the primary software components we need to configure:
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. System & Pin Initialization
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Clocking: Lock the system clock to exactly 148.5 MHz so the PIO dividers can simply be
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] `1.0`.
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] • GPIO Muxing: Assign GP2-GP5 to PIO0. Assign GP7 (Negative Rail) to the hardware PWM
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] block.
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] • PWM Config: Set GP7 to a continuous 50% duty cycle at a high frequency (e.g., 14.85 MHz)
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] to drive the charge pump.
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. PIO Configuration
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] We need to load three distinct PIO programs into the instruction memory and configure the State
2026-04-07 17:33:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] Machines (SMs):
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • SM0 (Y Base): Load `vidblit.pio`.
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • SM1-SM3 (Expanders): Load `expand.pio`. Configure `OUT` shift (right) and `IN` shift
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] (left). Set `autopush` threshold to 9. Set the `out null, N` shift masks to strip the correct bits
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] for Y Detail, Pb, and Pr.
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • SM4-SM6 (Outputs): Load `pwm10.pio`. Map both `OUT` and `SET` to the respective GPIO
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] pins. Set `autopull` threshold to 9.
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. The DMA Routing Topology
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is the most complex C++ task. We need two layers of DMA:
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Layer 1 (The Broadcast): Dedicated DMA channels reading from the 16-bit framebuffer and
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] writing to the TX FIFOs of the Expander SMs.
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Layer 2 (The Bridge): Dedicated memory-to-memory DMA channels reading from the Expander
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] RX FIFOs and writing to the `pwm10` TX FIFOs, paced by the Output SM's `DREQ`.
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Scanline Dispatch (The Trigger)
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because `vidblit` handles the horizontal blanking internally, the DMA channels feeding the pixel
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] data must be carefully triggered so they only send data during the active video window.
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Discussion Point:*
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] How would you prefer to handle the start of each scanline?
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  CPU Interrupt (IRQ): `vidblit` fires an interrupt at the start of active video, and the
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] CPU quickly resets and triggers the DMA channels.
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  Hardware Chaining (Control Blocks): We use a master DMA channel to automatically
2026-04-07 17:33:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] reconfigure and trigger the pixel DMAs without any CPU intervention.
