2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] Based on your architecture, here are a few structural reasons why Gemini might be getting stuck in
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] that loop:
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Recursive Relay Circuit Breaker: Your relay function handles tool calls via recursion.
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] If Gemini doesn't recognize the tool response as fulfilling its request, it will simply fire the
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] tool call again, leading to an endless loop. Adding a strict depth limit to the relay function is
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] usually the safest fallback to prevent this.
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Function Response Formatting: In your Gemini prompt preparation, the tool result is
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] parsed and passed as a `functionResponse`. If Gemini expects a highly specific nested JSON schema
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] for the return value and instead receives a flat string or a slightly different object structure,
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] it often treats the response as a failure and tries calling the tool again.
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Completion Triggers: Gemini models sometimes need explicit system instructions telling
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] them to synthesize a final user response immediately after receiving a tool's output. Without this,
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] it might continuously poll the tool (like a clock) thinking it needs to monitor the state.
2026-04-07 20:19:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
