2026-04-08 15:54:09 [nitro@ryzen5] more details about it's PIO feature
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] RP2350 PIO (Programmable I/O)
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] 
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] Overview
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] PIO is a unique hardware feature — tiny, deterministic state machines that run custom microcode to
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] implement I/O protocols in hardware, freeing up the CPU.
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] 
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] Block Diagram
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - 2 PIO blocks (PIO0 and PIO1)
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - 4 state machines per block = 8 total
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - 32-bit instruction memory per block (4× the RP2040's 9-bit width... actually same 32
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] instructions × 9-bit)
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] 
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] Instruction Set
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] 9-bit instructions, 9 opcodes:
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] 
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] ╭──────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] │  Opcode  │  Description                                 │
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] ├──────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] │  `MOV`   │  Move data between registers                 │
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] │  `SET`   │  Set pins/registers immediately              │
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] │  `OUT`   │  Shift data out from OSR to pins/registers   │
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] │  `IN`    │  Shift data in from pins/registers into ISR  │
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] │  `PULL`  │  Load from TX FIFO → OSR                     │
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] │  `PUSH`  │  Store ISR → RX FIFO                         │
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] │  `JMP`   │  Conditional jump                            │
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] │  `WAIT`  │  Wait on pin/IRQ condition                   │
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] │  `IRQ`   │  Set/clear interrupt flags                   │
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] ╰──────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] 
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] Registers Per State Machine
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - OSR — Output Shift Register
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - ISR — Input Shift Register
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - X / Y — General-purpose scratch registers
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - PC — Program Counter
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] 
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] FIFOs
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - 4-entry TX FIFO (CPU → state machine)
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - 4-entry RX FIFO (state machine → CPU)
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - Can be joined into a single 8-entry FIFO in one direction
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] 
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] What's New vs RP2040
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - Optional 32-bit instruction width (RP2350 adds a second wider instruction format for more
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] flexibility)
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - Execution from XIP — state machines can fetch instructions from external flash (not just
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] the 32-slot instruction memory)
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - GPIO output enable control is more granular
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - Improved clock divider range
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] 
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] Key Capabilities
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - Cycle-accurate timing — every instruction takes 1 clock cycle
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - Independent clock divider per state machine (down to ~2 kHz from 150 MHz)
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - Can drive and sample any GPIO
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - State machines can signal each other via IRQ
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - Side-set pins can change alongside any instruction (no extra cycle)
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - Auto-push / auto-pull — automatically transfer data when shift registers fill/empty
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] 
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] Common Use Cases
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - WS2812 / NeoPixel LEDs
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - VGA output
2026-04-08 15:54:35 [glm-5.1] - I2S audio
2026-04-08 15:54:36 [glm-5.1] - SD card bit-banging
2026-04-08 15:54:36 [glm-5.1] - Custom serial protocols (1-Wire, Manchester, etc.)
2026-04-08 15:54:36 [glm-5.1] - Stepper motor timing
2026-04-08 15:54:36 [glm-5.1] - PS/2 keyboard/mouse
2026-04-08 15:54:36 [glm-5.1] 
2026-04-08 15:54:36 [glm-5.1] The killer feature is that PIO runs independently of the CPU — once programmed, it handles I/O with
2026-04-08 15:54:36 [glm-5.1] zero jitter and no CPU overhead. Want me to write a PIO example?
2026-04-08 15:54:36 [glm-5.1] 
