2026-04-09 09:40:18 [nitro@ryzen5] this history is shared with various models, what are my options?
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] Because your `rohaHistory` is shared across different model providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google,
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] Mistral, Cohere), the primary issue is that each provider has different expectations for
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] history formatting (especially regarding tool calls and multi-modal blobs).
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] Here are your options to manage this shared history:
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 1. The "Canonical" History Pattern (Recommended)
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] Instead of forcing every model to accept the same `rohaHistory` structure, move the conversion
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] logic into a Strategy Pattern. Your current `plainHistory`, `strictHistory`, and
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] `multiHistory` functions are a good start, but they should be formalized:
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Normalize on input: Keep `rohaHistory` as a clean, provider-agnostic array of objects.
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Transform on export: Create a `getPayload(modelName)` function that maps your
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] `rohaHistory` specifically for that model's API requirements (e.g., Anthropic needs `tool_use`
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] blocks, Gemini needs `functionResponse` parts).
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] 2. The "Squash & Strip" Strategy (Compatibility)
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you want to keep the history universal, you must strip features that cause crashes:
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Tool Call Stripping: If you switch from a "tool-capable" model (like `gemini-3.1`) to one
2026-04-09 09:40:26 [gemini-3.1-lite] that isn't, use a function to strip `tool_calls` from the history before passing it to the new
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] model.
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Blob/Image Normalization: If a model doesn't support the `blob` format, filter those
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] messages out of the history array before the `relay` call.
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 3. Session Branching
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] Since you already have `sessionStack` in your `slopfountain.ts`, use it to enforce model-specific
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] boundaries:
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] • When switching from a "reasoning" model (like `gemini-3.1-flash-lite`) to a "standard" model,
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] push the current history to the stack and start a fresh, sanitized history for the new
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] model.
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] • This prevents the "polluted" history (which might contain the problematic `thought_signature` or
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] tool-call formats) from being sent to models that don't recognize them.
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 4. Implementation Suggestion
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] Modify your `relay` function to validate the history against the model's capabilities before
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] sending:
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] // Add a validator in relay()
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] function sanitizeHistory(history: Plop[], model: string): Plop[] {
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite]     const isGoogle = model.includes("@google");
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite]     return history.filter(item => {
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite]         // Drop tool calls if the model is not tool-capable
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite]         if (item.tool_calls && !modelSupportsTools(model)) return false;
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite]         return true;
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite]     });
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] Which option to choose?
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If you want to keep using `gemini-3.1-flash-lite`: You must use Option 1. You
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] need to ensure the `thought_signature` is passed through if it exists in the history, but filter it
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] out when sending that same history to models like GPT-4o or Claude.
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] • If you want stability: Use Option 3. Create a "Bridge" function that clears the
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] history when switching between fundamentally different model types (e.g., Reasoning vs. Standard).
2026-04-09 09:40:27 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
