2026-04-09 17:17:06 [nitro@ryzen5] explain what exactly return id.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9_-]/g, "_"); does
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] The line `return id.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9-]/g, "");` scans the string and replaces any
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] invalid character with an underscore.
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the breakdown of the regular expression `/[^a-zA-Z0-9_-]/g`:
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `[...]` defines a character class or set.
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `^` at the beginning of the set acts as a negation operator. It means "match any character that
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] is not in this set."
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `a-zA-Z0-9` includes all uppercase letters, lowercase letters, and digits.
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `_` includes the underscore.
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `-` includes the hyphen.
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • `g` is the global flag, meaning the replacement applies to all matches in the string, rather than
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] stopping after the first one.
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] For example, a tool ID like `call:time.tool id!` would be converted to
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] `calltimetoolid`, ensuring it safely passes Claude's validation pattern.
2026-04-09 17:17:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-09 17:22:25 [nitro@ryzen5] in ts how to check of var is an object?
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] To reliably check if a variable is a plain object in TypeScript, you need to account for
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] JavaScript's quirks where `null` and arrays are also evaluated as objects.
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a named reusable function using a type predicate:
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] function isPlainObject(value: unknown): value is Record<string, unknown> {
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	return typeof value === "object" && value !== null && !Array.isArray(value);
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] This ensures the value is actually an object, excluding `null` and arrays, while helping TypeScript
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] narrow down the type for safe property access.
2026-04-09 17:22:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-09 17:23:06 [nitro@ryzen5] standard string rep for null
2026-04-09 17:23:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] The standard string representation for `null` in TypeScript and JavaScript is the string `"null"`.
2026-04-09 17:23:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is the exact output generated when using `String(null)` or `JSON.stringify(null)`.
2026-04-09 17:23:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
