2026-04-09 18:26:39 [nitro@ryzen5] the use of the colon means gemini barfs when seeing it
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] Gemini enforces stricter validation on `toolcallid` than OpenAI's spec suggests—it
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] typically only accepts alphanumeric, hyphens and underscores. When Kimi (or any OpenAI-compatible
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] endpoint) generates IDs containing colons (e.g., `callabc:123` or your `readtime:2`
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] format), Gemini's API returns a 400 error.
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] • The fix:* When proxying between Kimi and Gemini, sanitize the tool call IDs:
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] // Map to store: sanitizedId -> originalId
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] const idMap = new Map();
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] function sanitizeToolCallId(originalId) {
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] 	// Replace colons and other invalid chars with underscores
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] 	const clean = originalId.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9_-]/g, '_');
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] 	idMap.set(clean, originalId);
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] 	return clean;
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-04-09 18:27:14 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] function restoreToolCallId(sanitizedId) {
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] 	return idMap.get(sanitizedId) || sanitizedId;
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] Apply `sanitizeToolCallId` to the `id` field in each `tool_calls` object when forwarding Kimi's
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] request to Gemini, and use `restoreToolCallId` when sending the tool results back to match Kimi's
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] expected IDs.
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] If you're controlling the ID generation (manually constructing tool calls instead of relaying
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] them), simply use `crypto.randomUUID().replace(/-/g, '')` or similar colon-free format instead of
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] `functionName:index` schemas.
2026-04-09 18:27:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
