2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] The `slopfountain.ts` research tool is a complex CLI application designed to interface with various
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] LLM providers, manage file shares, and track conversation history.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Based on the provided source code, here is a summary of the core functionality:
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Key Features
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Multi-Model Support: Connects to OpenAI, DeepSeek, Google (Gemini), Cohere, and Anthropic
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] (Claude) using environment variables for API keys.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • State Management: Uses a `roha` object to track configuration, history, file shares, and
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] usage counters, persisted in `forge/forge.json`.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • File Handling: Supports sharing local files/directories with hashing (SHA-256) and basic
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] MIME-type detection. It can "commit" files as blobs or content into the conversation history.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Tool Interface: Implements a function-calling mechanism (`onCall`) allowing models to
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] interact with the local environment (e.g., `readtime`, `submitfile`, `fetchfile`,
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `tagslop`).
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Output Formatting: Includes custom markdown-to-ANSI rendering, table box-drawing, and
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] word-wrapping for terminal display.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Experimental Features:
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     *   TTS Integration: Hooks for Google Cloud TTS and OpenAI speech endpoints.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     *   Slop Workers: Capability to load and run TypeScript worker scripts (`.slop.ts`)
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] from a `../slop` directory.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     *   Session Management: Allows saving and loading full conversation histories as JSON
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] files.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Critical Components
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `relay()`: The primary function for orchestrating API requests, calculating costs based
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] on token usage, and handling tool execution recursively.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `chat()`: The main loop that handles user input, command parsing (commands starting with
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `/`), and triggers the relay logic.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • `flush()`: Manages output buffering and logging to `forge/forge.log`.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Observations for Testing
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Platform Dependencies: The code relies on platform-specific binaries for clipboard
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] operations (`pbcopy`, `clip`, `xclip`) and file opening (`open`, `xdg-open`, `start`).
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Configuration: Behavior is highly dependent on `modelspecs.json` and `accounts.json`.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Safety: The `eval`-like nature of processing `toolcalls` and the potential for file
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] system interaction via `submitfile`/`fetch_file` suggests it is intended for controlled
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] research environments rather than public deployment.
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] If you are currently debugging or testing specific model behavior, I can provide focused breakdowns
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] of the `relay` logic or the prompt preparation methods for a specific provider. What would you like
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] to examine first?
2026-04-16 11:29:15 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:31:12 [nitro@ryzen5] i am considering integrating tts models, but need to consider style of interaction they provide, output only?
2026-04-16 11:31:19 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Based on the `slopfountain.ts` architecture, you have a few distinct ways to handle TTS
2026-04-16 11:31:19 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] integration. The "style" of interaction depends heavily on whether you treat the TTS as a
2026-04-16 11:31:19 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] passive sink (output only) or an active participant (part of the tool-use loop).
2026-04-16 11:31:19 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:31:19 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Here is how the current implementation approaches it and the trade-offs for your integration:
2026-04-16 11:31:19 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1. The Output-Only Model (Passive Sink)
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] This is how `gptSay()` and `geminiSay()` are currently implemented.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Workflow: The model generates text \rightarrow `relay()` finishes \rightarrow `flush()`
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] triggers the TTS function \rightarrow Audio file is saved/opened.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Interaction Style: "Read-aloud." The user reads the text on the terminal, and the audio
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] follows as a secondary, non-blocking stream.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Pros: Low latency, doesn't interfere with the LLM's context window, easy to implement.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Cons: Disconnected from the LLM's "intent." The LLM cannot choose how or
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] when to speak; it just happens at the end of a turn.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2. The Tool-Use Model (Active Participant)
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] If you integrate TTS as a function in the `rohaTools` array, you change the interaction style
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] significantly.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Workflow: The model identifies a turn where it should speak \rightarrow It emits a
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `tool_call` (e.g., `speak({text: "..."})`) \rightarrow The `onCall()` handler invokes the TTS
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] engine.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Interaction Style: "Conversational Agent." The model can interject, read specific parts
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] of a long response, or choose not to speak at all.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Pros: Highly interactive. The LLM can handle multi-modal responses (e.g., "I've generated
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] the file, let me explain it to you..." [triggers TTS]).
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Cons: Complexity. You have to handle the `tool_result` returned to the model so it knows
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] the audio was successfully queued or played.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 3. The "Real-time" Model (Streaming)
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] This is not currently fully implemented in your code but is the industry standard for modern TTS.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Workflow: Instead of waiting for the full completion, you stream the audio chunks as the
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] LLM generates tokens.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Interaction Style: "Human-like." The latency is reduced from "seconds" to "milliseconds,"
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] allowing for interruption and natural pauses.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Pros: Best user experience.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Cons: Extremely high complexity. Requires handling audio buffers, sample rates, and
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] potentially using WebSocket protocols (like Gemini's Multimodal Live API).
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Recommendation for Research
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Since you are using this as a "dunking" tool for LLMs, I suggest Option 2 (Tool-Use).
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Why: It allows you to test how well different models handle multi-modal tool-calling.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Does the model know to use the `speak` tool only when appropriate? Does it hallucinate the
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] parameters?
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Implementation Strategy:
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     1.  Add a `speak` function to your `rohaTools` definition.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     2.  Update `onCall()` to handle the `speak` tool-call, which triggers the TTS client.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]     3.  If the model triggers the tool, do not echo the same text to the terminal (or echo it in a
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] different style) to avoid redundancy.
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Would you like to see how to define the `speak` tool schema in `rohaTools` to get the models
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] to start calling it?*
2026-04-16 11:31:20 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:20 [nitro@ryzen5] how do i enumerate voices available for models like gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview?
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] To enumerate available voices for models like `gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview` (which uses the Gemini
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Multimodal Live API or standard generation with audio modalities), you cannot rely on a static
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] list. Because Google frequently updates the model and voice registry, the most reliable way is to
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] query the model's capabilities dynamically.
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Since you are using `GoogleGenerativeAI` in your `connectGoogle` function, you can use the
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `getGenerativeModel` method to access the model's metadata or use the `ListVoices` endpoint if you
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] were using the Google Cloud Text-to-Speech API.
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] However, for the Gemini model-specific voices (like `Kore`, `Fenrir`, etc.), the following
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] approach is the standard for your `slopfountain.ts` integration:
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1. The Direct Query Method
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] You can add a function to your `connectGoogle` block to fetch the latest supported voices directly
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] from the API.
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] // Add this inside connectGoogle() or as a standalone helper
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] async function getGeminiVoices(genAI) {
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	// The Gemini API doesn't have a direct 'listVoices' method
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	// for the generative model itself.
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	// The standard way is to use the Google Cloud Text-to-Speech API
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	// if you are using the same underlying engine.
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	// If you are using the Gemini Multimodal Live API,
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	// the list is often found in the model's documentation or
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	// by sending a dummy request to check the 'supported modalities'.
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	echo("[GEMINI] Fetching available prebuilt voices...");
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	// Use the Google Cloud TTS client (already initialized in your connectGoogleVoice)
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	if (geminiSpeechClient) {
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 		const [result] = await geminiSpeechClient.listVoices();
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 		const voices = result.voices.map(v => v.name);
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 		echo("[GEMINI] Available voices:", voices.join(", "));
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 		return voices;
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	}
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	return [];
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] }
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2. The "Discovery" Pattern (Best for your tool)
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Since your research tool is model-agnostic, you should implement a `discover` command that models
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] can call, or that you run on startup.
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Add this as a command in your `callCommand` switch:
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] case "voices":
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	const modelProvider = grokModel.split("@")[1];
2026-04-16 11:32:27 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	if (modelProvider === "google") {
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 		await getGeminiVoices(rohaEndpoint["google"].genAI);
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	} else {
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 		echo("[SAY] Voice discovery not implemented for", modelProvider);
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	}
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	break;
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 3. Important Distinction: Model-Native vs. Cloud TTS
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] It is critical to distinguish between the two:
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Google Cloud TTS: These voices (like `en-US-Journey-F`) are accessed via the
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `TextToSpeechClient`. They are not the same as the "Gemini-native" voices (like `Charon` or
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `Puck`).
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Gemini-Native Voices: These are currently hardcoded in the Gemini API
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] documentation and experimental SDKs. They are not discoverable via an API call in the same way
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] standard TTS voices are.
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Recommendation:*
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] If you want to test the `gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview` specifically, you should create a
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `voices.json` file in your `forge/` directory that acts as a local cache of the voices you
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] have confirmed work with that specific model version.
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] {
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 	"gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview": ["Kore", "Fenrir", "Charon", "Puck"]
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] }
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Then, in your `geminiSay` function, add a validation step:
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1. Check if the requested voice is in the `voices.json` list for that model.
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2. If the model is new, let the user trigger a `/discover-voices` command that hits the Google
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Cloud TTS endpoint to see if there is overlap.
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Would you like me to write a small helper function to manage that `voices.json` registry
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] within your existing `forge` logic?*
2026-04-16 11:32:28 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:01 [nitro@ryzen5] what is jsonc?
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • JSONC stands for JSON with Comments*.
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] It is a superset of the standard JSON format that allows the use of single-line (`//`) and
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] multi-line (`/ ... /`) comments.
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Why it exists
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Standard JSON (as defined by RFC 8259) is strictly a data-interchange format. It does not support
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] comments, primarily to maintain strict interoperability and avoid ambiguity. However, this makes it
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] frustrating for developers who want to:
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Document configuration files.
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Comment out sections of a file during debugging.
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Provide human-readable explanations within a settings file.
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Key differences
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] ╭───────────────────┬───────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] │  Feature          │  JSON                     │  JSONC                                    │
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] ├───────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] │  :---             │  :---                     │  :---                                     │
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] │  Comments         │  Not allowed              │  Supported (`//` and `/* */`)             │
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] │  Trailing Commas  │  Not allowed              │  Often allowed (depending on the parser)  │
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] │  Intended Use     │  Machine-to-machine data  │  Human-readable configurations            │
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] ╰───────────────────┴───────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Example
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] In a standard `config.json`, you cannot explain your settings:
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] {
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]   "temperature": 0.7,
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]   "tools": true
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] }
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] In `config.jsonc`, you can:
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] {
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]   // Set the creativity of the model
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]   "temperature": 0.7,
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]   /*
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]      Enable or disable
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]      the function-calling tools
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]   */
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite]   "tools": true
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] }
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:07 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] Usage in your project
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] If you decide to use JSONC for your `modelspecs.json` or `accounts.json` files in
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `slopfountain.ts`:
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 1.  Parsing: You cannot use `JSON.parse()` directly on a `.jsonc` file, as it will throw a
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] syntax error when it encounters the `//`.
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 2.  Deno/TypeScript: You would need to use a library like `jsonc-parser` (the one used by
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] VS Code).
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] import { parse } from "https://deno.land/std/encoding/jsonc.ts";
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const content = await Deno.readTextFile("modelspecs.jsonc");
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] const config = parse(content); // This handles the comments for you
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] • Would you like me to show you how to swap your current `JSON.parse` logic in
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] `slopfountain.ts` for a JSONC-compatible version?*
2026-04-16 11:37:08 [gemini-3.1-flash-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:19 [nitro@ryzen5] can i list voices that can be used with google GenerativeModel
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] The `google-generativeai` SDK does not provide a direct method to list available voices, as voice
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] selection is typically handled by the Text-to-Speech (TTS) service rather than the
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] `GenerativeModel` itself.
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you are using Gemini's Multimodal Live API (which supports real-time audio), you can
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] typically choose from a set of predefined voices. As of the current documentation, the supported
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] voices for the Multimodal Live API are:
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Puck
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Charon
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Kore
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Fenrir
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Aoede
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] How to use them
2026-04-16 17:33:22 [gemini-3.1-lite] When configuring your session, you specify the voice in the `speech_config`:
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] import google.generativeai as genai
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] # Example configuration for a live session
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] config = {
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	"generation_config": {
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		"speech_config": {
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			"voice_config": {
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 				"prebuilt_voice_config": {
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 					"voice_name": "Puck"
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 				}
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 			}
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		}
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	}
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] }
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] If you are using the standard `generate_content` method, it only returns text. You would need to
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] pass that text to the Google Cloud Text-to-Speech API to generate audio. That API has its
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] own method to list voices:
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] from google.cloud import texttospeech
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] def list_tts_voices():
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	client = texttospeech.TextToSpeechClient()
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	voices = client.list_voices()
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 	for voice in voices.voices:
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 		print(f"Name: {voice.name}, Language: {voice.language_codes[0]}")
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] # list_tts_voices()
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] 
2026-04-16 17:33:23 [gemini-3.1-lite] • Note: Ensure you have the `google-cloud-texttospeech` library installed if you intend to use the
