2026-04-17 15:31:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] Thank you for sharing the PIO source. The approach of using `pindirs` modulation to generate three
2026-04-17 15:31:44 [gemini-3.1-pro] distinct video levels (Sync, Black, White) from a single pin (or minimal pin configuration) is an
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] elegant way to handle monochrome composite video. It effectively leverages the high-Z state for the
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] black level, driven-low for sync, and driven-high for white.
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] Reviewing the cycle timing against your target of 9504 cycles per line at 148.5 MHz, I noticed a
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] couple of minor details regarding the cycle counting that you may want to verify:
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Pixel Counter Discrepancy:
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] The header comment mentions `[2] pixelcounter = 703`, but the inline comment under the `line:`
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] label calculates active video as `768 * 10 = 7680 cycles`. To achieve the 51.71 µs active time and
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] the total line time, the pixel counter provided via the FIFO should indeed be 767 (giving 768
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] iterations), rather than 703.
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Total Cycle Count Verification:
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] If we sum the cycles for a line using 768 active pixels, it comes out to 9502 cycles, which is
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly 2 cycles shy of your 9504 target:
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Active loop: 1 setup + (768 iterations * 10 cycles) = 7681 cycles
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Front porch: 2 setup + 1 setup + (8 iterations * 30 cycles) = 243 cycles
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Hsync: 1 setup + 1 setup + (23 iterations * 30 cycles) = 692 cycles
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Back porch: 1 setup + 1 setup + (29 iterations * 30 cycles) = 872 cycles
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Line loop overhead: `jmp x--, line [13]` = 14 cycles
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total: 9502 cycles
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] To hit exactly 9504 cycles and maintain perfect 15625 Hz line timing, you can simply increase the
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] delay on the final `jmp x--, line` instruction by 2 (changing `[13]` to `[15]`).
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] The structure of pulling the frame parameters (vsync, line count, pixel count) at the start of the
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] `.wrap` cycle before streaming the bitmap is a very clean use of the FIFO. Let me know if you would
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] like me to review the corresponding C/C++ DMA setup or any other parts of the system.
2026-04-17 15:31:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:33:26 [nitro@ryzen5] after half way across my lcd tv is skipping every 40 pixels, not sure a porch adjustment could help
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] The symptom you are describing—an image that renders fine for the first half of the line but then
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] begins skipping or tearing at regular intervals—is a classic artifact with a couple of highly
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] specific potential causes on the RP2040/Pico2.
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are correct to be skeptical about a porch adjustment. Porch timings generally dictate the
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] horizontal centering of the image and the TV's black-level clamping. They rarely cause repeating
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] artifacts that only begin midway through the active video.
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the two most likely culprits for this behavior:
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. DMA Starvation / Bus Contention (The "32-Pixel" Threshold)
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] You mentioned the skipping happens roughly every 40 pixels. In the Pico's architecture, a 32-bit
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] word provides exactly 32 pixels of data. If your skipping is actually happening exactly every 32
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixels, this strongly points to the PIO's TX FIFO running dry.
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Mechanism:*
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] The PIO TX FIFO is 4 words deep (or 8 if you joined them). At the start of the line, the FIFO is
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] full, acting as a buffer. If your DMA channel is fetching pixel data slightly slower than the PIO
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] is consuming it, the FIFO will gradually drain. Midway across the screen, the FIFO empties
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] completely. From that point on, every time the PIO's `autopull` triggers (every 32nd pixel), the
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machine stalls for a few cycles waiting for the DMA to provide the next word. This stall
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] stretches that specific pixel, pushing the rest of the line out of sync and appearing as a "skip"
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] on the TV.
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How to fix/verify:*
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Memory Location: Ensure your video bitmap array is stored in SRAM, not Flash memory.
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] Flash memory (XIP) cache misses will cause massive DMA delays. You can force an array into RAM
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] using compiler attributes (e.g., `attribute((section(".time_critical.data")))` or
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] just omitting `const` depending on your setup).
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Bus Contention: If your CPU is aggressively accessing the same SRAM bank that the DMA is
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] reading the framebuffer from, it will stall the DMA. The Pico's SRAM is divided into banks (usually
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 64KB each). Try placing your framebuffer in a dedicated SRAM bank that the CPU isn't actively
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] working in during the active video line.
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] • DMA Priority: If you have multiple DMA channels running, ensure the video DMA channel has
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] high priority on the bus.
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. LCD ADC Phase Drift (Beat Frequency)
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] Unlike old CRT monitors that continuously sweep an electron beam, LCD TVs digitize the analog
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] composite signal using an internal Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) running at a fixed sampling
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] clock.
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Mechanism:*
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because monochrome composite video lacks a colorburst signal (which TVs normally use to lock their
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] internal PLLs), the TV has to guess the pixel clock based entirely on the HSYNC pulse.
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your Pico is outputting pixels at exactly 14.85 MHz (148.5 MHz / 10 cycles). Standard
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] Definition TVs typically sample analog video at 13.5 MHz (ITU-R BT.601) or 14.75
2026-04-17 15:34:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] MHz (Square pixel PAL).
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your pixel clock doesn't perfectly match the TV's internal sampling clock, a phase error
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] accumulates as it scans left to right. At the start of the line, the TV samples the center of your
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixels. Midway across the screen, the phase error reaches 180 degrees, meaning the TV's ADC is now
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] sampling exactly on the transition edges between your pixels. This causes aliasing, where the TV
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] scaler drops or duplicates pixels at a regular repeating frequency (a "beat frequency").
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How to fix/verify:*
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Test a different pixel width: Try changing the PIO cycle delay to stretch the pixels
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] slightly to see if the artifact changes frequency or disappears. For example, changing the active
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] loop to take 11 cycles instead of 10 (which yields a 13.5 MHz pixel clock at 148.5 MHz sysclock)
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] and reducing the total pixel count per line.
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If changing the PIO timing changes the distance between the "skips", it is definitely an LCD
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] scaling/phase issue. If the skip remains stubbornly at 32 pixels regardless of timing, it is DMA
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] starvation.
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] I would recommend double-checking the exact pixel distance of the skip. If it is exactly 32 pixels,
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] focus entirely on your DMA and memory layout.
2026-04-17 15:34:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:32 [nitro@ryzen5] is there a method to detect an fifo starving
2026-04-17 15:38:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, the RP2040/Pico 2 hardware has a built-in mechanism designed exactly for this.
2026-04-17 15:38:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] The PIO peripheral contains a dedicated FIFO Debug register (`FDEBUG`) which includes a
2026-04-17 15:38:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] `TXSTALL` flag for each state machine.
2026-04-17 15:38:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] This flag is a "sticky" bit. If your state machine ever attempts to execute a `pull` (or an `out`
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] with `autopull` enabled) while the TX FIFO is empty, the hardware will pause the state machine and
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] set this `TXSTALL` bit to 1. It will remain at 1 until you explicitly clear it, making it perfect
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] for detecting transient starvation that happens midway through a scanline.
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a simple C function you can use to check for this starvation.
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include "hardware/pio.h"
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include <stdio.h>
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] // Pass your PIO instance (e.g., pio0 or pio1) and the State Machine index (0-3)
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] void check_fifo_starvation(PIO pio, uint sm) {
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Calculate the bitmask for this specific state machine's TXSTALL flag
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint32_t stall_mask = 1u << (PIO_FDEBUG_TXSTALL_LSB + sm);
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Check if the stall flag is currently set
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	if (pio->fdebug & stall_mask) {
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		printf("WARNING: PIO SM %u TX FIFO Starvation Detected!\n", sm);
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// The bit is "write 1 to clear" (W1C).
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// We write the mask back to clear the flag for the next test.
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		pio->fdebug = stall_mask;
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	} else {
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		printf("FIFO OK: No starvation detected.\n");
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] How to use this:
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Clear the flag before testing: Before you start your video DMA transfer for a new frame,
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] call `pio->fdebug = (1u << (PIOFDEBUGTXSTALL_LSB + sm));` to ensure you are starting with a
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] clean slate.
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Run your video output: Let the DMA and PIO render a frame (or at least wait long enough
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] for the artifact to appear on the screen).
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Check the flag: Call the `checkfifostarvation(pio, sm)` function.
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] If it prints the warning, your DMA is definitely falling behind the PIO consumption rate.
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] If Starvation is Confirmed
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] If the `TXSTALL` bit is tripping, here are the most effective ways to resolve the DMA bottleneck:
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] • SRAM Striping: The Pico's SRAM is divided into banks. If your CPU is executing code or
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] accessing variables in the same SRAM bank where your framebuffer resides, the bus arbiter will
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] pause the DMA to let the CPU through. Move your framebuffer to a dedicated SRAM bank.
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] • DMA Priority: You can give the DMA read channel high priority on the bus. In the RP2040,
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] this is done by setting the `BUS_PRIORITY` register in the DMA block.
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Avoid Flash (XIP): Ensure absolutely no part of your active video buffer is being read
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] from Flash memory during the active line. A single XIP cache miss takes dozens of cycles and will
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] instantly starve the FIFO.
2026-04-17 15:38:55 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:09 [nitro@ryzen5] what does bus_priority default to?
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] By default, the bus priority for all masters (CPU0, CPU1, DMA Read, and DMA Write) defaults to
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 0 (Normal/Equal Priority).
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] When all bus masters have the same priority, the bus arbiter uses Round-Robin arbitration.
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] This means if your CPU and your DMA controller both try to access the same SRAM bank at the exact
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] same time, they will take turns.
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] For standard applications, this is perfectly fine. However, for high-speed video generation, this
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] "taking turns" is a primary cause of FIFO starvation. If the CPU is doing a heavy block of data
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] processing, variable crunching, or loop unrolling in the same memory bank where your framebuffer
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] lives, the DMA will be forced to wait for its turn, draining the PIO FIFO and causing the skipping
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] artifact you are seeing.
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] How to Elevate DMA Priority
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can give the DMA read channel absolute priority over the CPUs. This ensures that if there is a
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] collision, the CPU stalls and the DMA gets the data immediately.
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] The priority settings are not located in the DMA peripheral itself, but rather in the Bus Fabric
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] controller (`bus_ctrl`). Here is how you can elevate the DMA Read priority:
2026-04-17 15:40:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include "hardware/structs/bus_ctrl.h"
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] void elevate_dma_priority(void) {
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Elevate DMA Read priority to 1.
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// CPUs and DMA Write remain at default priority 0.
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	bus_ctrl_hw->priority = BUSCTRL_BUS_PRIORITY_DMA_R_BITS;
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] A Note on Memory Architecture
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] While elevating the DMA priority is a great quick fix and often solves the problem entirely, the
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] most robust solution is to avoid the bus collision in the first place using SRAM Striping.
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Pico's SRAM is divided into distinct, independent banks. The bus crossbar allows multiple
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] masters to access different banks simultaneously with zero stall cycles.
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you place your framebuffer array in an SRAM bank that the CPU is not actively using during the
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] active video line (for example, keeping the CPU stack and main variables in SRAM bank 0, and
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] forcing the framebuffer into SRAM bank 2 or 3), the DMA and CPU will never collide, regardless of
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] priority settings.
2026-04-17 15:40:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:46:02 [nitro@ryzen5] led is turning on
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] I see you applied the `[15]` cycle delay to the final `jmp`—that perfectly rounds out the timing to
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly 9504 cycles per line for a rock-solid 15625 Hz horizontal frequency.
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regarding the "led is turning on" observation, there are two possibilities here depending
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] on how you set things up:
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. If you mapped the TXSTALL check to the LED (Diagnostic Success)
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you took the `TXSTALL` FIFO starvation check we discussed and wired it to turn on the LED when
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] triggered, this is a fantastic diagnostic result. It confirms 100% that DMA starvation is the
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] cause of your image skipping.
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your PIO consumes one 32-bit word every 320 system cycles (10 cycles per pixel × 32 bits). At 148.5
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] MHz, 320 cycles is an eternity for the DMA. If the FIFO is running dry, it means the bus is being
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] heavily blocked by something else.
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] To fix this, check these three things in your C/C++ code:
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Move Framebuffer to RAM: If your `bitmap` array is `const` and stored in Flash, a cache
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] miss will easily stall the bus for more than 320 cycles. Force it into RAM (e.g., using
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] `uint32t attribute((section(".timecritical.data"))) bitmap[...]` or by
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] removing `const`).
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Elevate DMA Priority: Apply the `busctrlhw->priority =
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] BUSCTRLBUSPRIORITYDMAR_BITS;` setting to give the DMA right-of-way over the CPU.
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] • CPU Contention: Ensure the CPU isn't executing a tight, heavy loop in the exact same SRAM
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] bank where your video buffer resides during the active frame.
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. If you did NOT map the LED intentionally (Pin Overlap)
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] If the LED is just turning on by itself when you run this PIO program, it means your PIO pin
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] mappings are accidentally overlapping with the board's LED pin.
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your PIO code, you use:
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] `set pins, 1`
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] `set pins, 0`
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] If the `set` base pin in your state machine configuration happens to be pointing at or overlapping
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] with the LED GPIO (GPIO 25 on a standard Pico, or the specific LED pin on your Pico 2 board), the
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] PIO will rapidly toggle the LED during the active video line, making it appear solidly lit.
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] Double-check your `smconfigsetsetpins()` setup in your initialization code to
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] ensure it is only targeting your intended video output pin.
2026-04-17 15:46:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Aha! Looking at your C++ code, the mystery of the midway screen tearing is completely solved.
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] The LED turning on confirms your PIO TX FIFO is starving, and your `main()` loop reveals exactly
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] why: You are using the CPU to feed the video PIO instead of DMA.
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] The CPU Bottleneck
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your `main()` loop, you are pushing pixels to the PIO using this block:
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		while (!pio_sm_is_tx_fifo_full(pio0, VideoStateMachine)) {
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			uint32_t pix=frame[p++];
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			// ...
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			pio_sm_put(pio0, VideoStateMachine, pix);
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because you joined the TX FIFO (`smconfigsetfifojoin`), it can hold 8 words (256
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixels). At the start of the line, the CPU fills this buffer. However, the PIO consumes one word
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] every 320 cycles (10 cycles × 32 bits).
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Once the FIFO is full, the CPU exits that `while` loop and continues down `main()`, where it does
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] three very expensive things:
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. It checks the `TXSTALL` flag.
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. It loops back to the top and calls `ReadBootSel()`.
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. `ReadBootSel()` disables interrupts and runs a `for (volatile int i = 0; i < 100; ++i);` delay
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] loop to let the CS pin settle.
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] That volatile delay loop in `ReadBootSel()` alone takes hundreds of cycles. By the time the CPU
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] finishes checking the buttons and audio and gets back to `piosmput`, the PIO has completely
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] drained the 8-word FIFO and stalled, causing your skip.
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Solution: Hardware DMA
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] To generate stable video, the CPU should almost never touch the `piosmput` registers
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] directly. You need to hand the `frame` array over to the hardware DMA controller. The DMA will
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] automatically push a word to the PIO the exact nanosecond it asks for one, completely independent
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] of whatever the CPU is doing.
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is how you can replace your CPU push with a DMA channel.
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] First, include the DMA hardware library at the top of your file:
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] #include <hardware/dma.h>
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Next, add a reusable function to initialize the DMA:
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] int video_dma_chan;
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] void video_dma_init() {
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	video_dma_chan = dma_claim_unused_channel(true);
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_config c = dma_channel_get_default_config(video_dma_chan);
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_transfer_data_size(&c, DMA_SIZE_32);
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_read_increment(&c, true);
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_write_increment(&c, false);
2026-04-17 15:47:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Pace the DMA transfers based on the PIO TX FIFO demand
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_dreq(&c, pio_get_dreq(pio0, VideoStateMachine, true));
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_configure(
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		video_dma_chan,
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		&c,
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		&pio0->txf[VideoStateMachine],	// Write address (PIO TX FIFO)
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		NULL,				// Read address (set later)
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		wordCount,			// Number of transfers
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		false				// Do not start yet
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	);
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Call `videodmainit();` in your `main()` function right after `vid_init()`.
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Finally, remove the `while (!piosmistxfifo_full(...))` block from your `main()`
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] loop and replace it with a check to restart the DMA whenever it finishes the frame:
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// If DMA has finished sending the frame, reset the read address and restart it
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (!dma_channel_is_busy(video_dma_chan)) {
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			f++;
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, frame, true);
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] One Minor Note on the LED Diagnostic
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your diagnostic code:
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (pio0->fdebug & stall_mask) {
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led=true;
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		} else {
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led=false;
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] The `fdebug` register bits are "Write 1 to Clear" (W1C). Once the state machine stalls a single
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] time, that bit stays `1` forever until you explicitly write a `1` back to it. So as currently
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] written, the LED will turn on at the first glitch and never turn off.
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] To make it flash only when actively starving, you would clear it after reading:
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (pio0->fdebug & stall_mask) {
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led=true;
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			pio0->fdebug = stall_mask; // Clear the sticky flag
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		} else {
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led=false;
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Switching to DMA will completely eliminate this starvation, allowing you to run audio, input
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] processing, and game logic on the CPU without glitching the screen.
2026-04-17 15:47:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] I see the changes you made in this iteration! By wrapping the `ReadBootSel()` check in
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] `if((count&0xfffff)==0)` and disabling the audio/PS2 streams, you drastically reduced the execution
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] time of your `main` `while(true)` loop.
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the CPU is no longer getting bogged down by that 100-cycle volatile delay in the boot
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] button check every single iteration, it can get back to `piosmput` fast enough to keep the
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 8-word TX FIFO topped up. I suspect the skipping artifact has either disappeared or been
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] significantly reduced.
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, I highly recommend treating this as a temporary diagnostic step rather than the final
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] architecture.
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Fragility of CPU-Driven Video
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Right now, your video stability is entirely dependent on the execution time of your main loop. The
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] PIO needs a new 32-bit word exactly every 320 system cycles. Because your FIFO holds 8 words, the
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] absolute maximum time your CPU can spend doing anything else before returning to the `while
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] (!piosmistxfifo_full)` loop is 2,560 cycles (~17 microseconds).
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] The moment you re-enable the audio synthesis, add game logic, or try to process PS2 keyboard
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] interrupts, you will likely exceed this 17µs budget, and the screen will start tearing again.
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Fixing the LED Diagnostic
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] As mentioned in the previous message, the `TXSTALL` bit in `fdebug` is a sticky bit. If
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] your screen glitches even for a single microsecond, that bit gets set to `1` and stays `1` forever.
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] To make your LED accurately reflect real-time starvation, you must clear the bit after reading it
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] by writing a `1` back to it:
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t stall_mask = 1u << (PIO_FDEBUG_TXSTALL_LSB + VideoStateMachine);
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (pio0->fdebug & stall_mask) {
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led=true;
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			pio0->fdebug = stall_mask;	// Write 1 to clear the sticky flag
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		} else {
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led=false;
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		gpio_put(LED_PIN,led);
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] Moving Forward
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] When you are ready to start building out the rest of the system (audio, input, game state), you
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] will definitely want to offload the `frame` array pushing to the DMA controller.
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] By letting the DMA handle the `piosmput` operations in the background, your CPU becomes
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 100% free. Your `main` loop will no longer need to worry about timing, and you can run heavy tasks
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] without ever dropping a single pixel on the TV. Let me know if you'd like to implement the DMA
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] approach and need a hand hooking it up!
2026-04-17 15:55:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] This looks excellent! Moving the video frame pushing to the hardware DMA is the perfect
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] architectural step.
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] By setting up the DMA with `dreq` paced by the PIO TX FIFO, the DMA controller will perfectly
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] trickle-feed your `frame` array into the PIO at the exact rate of the pixel clock, completely
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] independently of the CPU. The logic you used to start/restart the DMA in the main loop is also
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] spot-on:
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (!dma_channel_is_busy(video_dma_chan)) {
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			f++;
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, frame, true);
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because you initialized the DMA with `false` (don't start yet), `dmachannelis_busy` will
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] correctly evaluate to `false` on the very first loop iteration, cleanly kicking off the first frame
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] and seamlessly reloading subsequent frames.
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] One Last Tweak for the Diagnostic LED
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you notice the LED turning on and never turning off, remember that the `TXSTALL` bit is
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] "sticky" (Write-1-to-Clear). If the DMA is late by even a single clock cycle right at startup, the
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] flag will trip and stay high forever.
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] To make the LED accurately show live, ongoing starvation, you just need to clear the flag when you
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] read it by writing the mask back to the register:
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t stall_mask = 1u << (PIO_FDEBUG_TXSTALL_LSB + VideoStateMachine);
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (pio0->fdebug & stall_mask) {
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led=true;
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			pio0->fdebug = stall_mask;	// Clear the sticky flag
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		} else {
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led=false;
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		gpio_put(LED_PIN,led);
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:01:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
