2026-04-17 16:02:45 [nitro@ryzen5] still same issue
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] If the issue is still happening exactly as before, we have successfully ruled out CPU execution
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] delays. The DMA is doing its job, which means the root cause is one of three highly specific
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] hardware interactions.
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] To narrow it down immediately, is your diagnostic LED turning on?
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the three remaining culprits, in order of likelihood:
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Flash Memory (XIP) Starvation
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your LED is still turning on, your DMA is still starving. Because the DMA is now
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] handling the transfer, the only thing that can starve it is Flash memory cache misses.
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your `frame` array in `monobit.h` is declared as `const uint32_t frame[]`, the compiler stores
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] it in Flash memory. As the DMA sweeps across the scanline, it constantly triggers XIP
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] (Execute-In-Place) cache misses. A cache miss halts the bus for up to 100 cycles, easily draining
2026-04-17 16:03:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] the PIO FIFO and causing a skip.
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Fix: Open `monobit.h` and remove the `const` keyword, or force it into RAM by
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] declaring it as:
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro]   `uint32t attribute((section(".timecritical.data"))) frame[...] = { ...
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] };`
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. LCD Beat Frequency (10 vs 11 cycles)
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your LED is not turning on, your DMA is perfectly healthy, and the artifact is being
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] created by the TV itself.
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your C++ header, you wrote: `pal - 64us line - 9504 cycles - 11 cycles per pixel`.
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, your PIO code is actually executing 10 cycles per pixel:
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 1 [8]   ; 1 + 8 = 9 cycles
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--,active       ; 1 cycle (Total = 10 cycles)
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 148.5 MHz ÷ 10 = 14.85 MHz pixel clock.
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Most LCD TVs expect standard Rec.601 digital video, which has a pixel clock of exactly 13.5
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] MHz. Because 14.85 MHz is exactly 10% faster than the TV's internal ADC clock, the TV scaler
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] gets out of phase. Halfway across the screen, the phase error hits 180 degrees, and the TV's scaler
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] drops or duplicates a pixel to resync, causing a repeating skip.
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Fix: Change the PIO to 11 cycles per pixel (which perfectly yields 13.5 MHz). Note
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] that this requires changing your active pixels from 768 (24 words) to 704 (22 words) to fit the
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 52µs active window.
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; active = 52.14 µs = 7,744 cycles (704 * 11)
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	mov	y,isr
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] active:
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out	pindirs, 1 [9]   ; 1 + 9 = 10
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp	y--,active       ; + 1 = 11 cycles
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (You would also need to adjust your back porch `set y, 26` to balance the 9504 total line cycles,
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] and update your frame array to 22 words per line).*
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Bit Shift Direction (The "32-Pixel Scramble")
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] If the skipping happens exactly every 32 pixels, it might be a bit-order mismatch.
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your C++ code, you configured the PIO to shift RIGHT:
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] `smconfigsetoutshift(&config, true, true, 32);`
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] This means the Least Significant Bit (LSB) of each word is drawn first. If your `monobit.h` image
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] was generated assuming MSB-first, every single block of 32 pixels is being drawn backwards on the
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] screen. On text or diagonal lines, this looks exactly like a jagged skipping artifact.
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Fix: Change the shift direction to LEFT:
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro]   `smconfigsetoutshift(&config, false, true, 32);`
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know what the LED is doing, or if changing the shift direction clears it up!
2026-04-17 16:03:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
