2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Thank you for sharing this RP2350 project. Generating a stable PAL video signal alongside I2S audio
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] and PS/2 input using the programmable I/O (PIO) state machines is a fantastic showcase of the
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] microcontroller's hardware capabilities.
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are a few observations on your implementation:
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Architecture and Timing*
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your PIO implementation for the video blitter is tightly optimized. Calculating the exact
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction delays for the active video, front porch, hsync, and back porch to perfectly fit the
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 9504 cycles per line at 148.5 MHz makes excellent use of the PIO's deterministic timing.
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] Furthermore, pacing the DMA channel via the PIO's TX FIFO data request (DREQ) is the ideal approach
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] to keep memory bandwidth usage efficient without starving the video signal.
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Graphics and Font Handling*
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] The inclusion of the C64 font data and the PetAscii conversion routine is a very nice touch. The
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] bit-flipping function to align the font data's endianness with the PIO shift register expectations
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] is a highly practical solution. Building out reusable named functions for plotting, unplotting,
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] text rendering, and rectangle drawing keeps the main frame initialization exceptionally clean.
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:33:30 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Hardware Specifics*
