2026-04-17 18:34:26 [nitro@ryzen5] to increase fifo bandwidth what are my options?
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] To increase the effective bandwidth and resilience of your PIO TX FIFO, you are already off to a
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] great start by using `PIOFIFOJOIN_TX` (which doubles your FIFO depth to 8 words) and
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] elevating the DMA read priority on the bus matrix.
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you are looking to push more data through, prevent underflows, or free up bus cycles, here are
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] the primary hardware and architectural options available on the RP2350:
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. SRAM Bank Striping (Bus Matrix Optimization)*
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] The RP-series microcontrollers use a crossbar bus matrix. If your CPU (or another DMA channel, like
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] your I2S audio) tries to access the same SRAM bank that your video DMA is currently reading from,
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] arbitration occurs, and one of them stalls.
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] By explicitly placing your `frame` array into a dedicated SRAM bank (using linker section
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] attributes), and ensuring your CPU stack and audio buffers are in different banks, you grant the
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] video DMA channel uninterrupted, zero-wait-state access to memory. This maximizes the speed at
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] which the DMA can refill the FIFO.
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. DMA Chaining (Zero-CPU Reload)*
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Currently, your main `while(true)` loop checks if the DMA is busy and manually reloads the read
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] address. This introduces CPU jitter; if the CPU is delayed by an interrupt or a flash read, the
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] FIFO might drain before the CPU triggers the next frame.
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can configure a second, hidden DMA channel to act as a control block. When the video DMA
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] finishes sending a frame, it automatically chains to the control DMA. The control DMA writes the
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] start address of `frame` back into the video DMA's read address register, restarting the process
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] instantly. This guarantees continuous FIFO feeding without CPU intervention.
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. Utilizing the RP2350 HSTX (High-Speed Serial Transmit)*
2026-04-17 18:34:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since you are targeting the RP2350, you have access to the new HSTX peripheral. While PIO is
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] fantastic, HSTX was designed specifically for continuous, high-bandwidth streaming (like DVI/HDMI
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] or high-res analog video). HSTX has its own dedicated FIFO, shift registers, and clock dividers,
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] and it sits directly on the bus matrix. Moving the video generation to HSTX would provide the
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] highest possible data bandwidth on this silicon, completely immune to PIO instruction cycle limits,
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] while freeing up an entire PIO block for other tasks.
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 4. PIO-Level Run-Length Encoding (RLE)*
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you want to stick with PIO but need more pixel bandwidth per FIFO word, you can change
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] the data format. Right now, every bit in the 32-bit word equals one pixel. For a UI or text-heavy
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] display, many consecutive pixels are identical.
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] You could modify your PIO program to interpret a 32-bit word not as a raw bitmap, but as an
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction (e.g., 16 bits for pixel color/state, 16 bits for a loop counter). The PIO would then
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] hold that state and output it for the specified number of cycles. This drastically reduces the
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] number of words the DMA needs to fetch from memory, effectively multiplying your FIFO bandwidth for
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] solid areas.
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 5. 64-bit Memory Access (RP2350 Specific)*
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] The RP2350 features a double-precision FPU and some widened internal bus paths compared to the
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] RP2040. While the PIO FIFO is still 32-bit, ensuring your DMA is reading from memory in the most
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] efficient burst sizes supported by the RP2350's updated bus fabric can slightly reduce the overhead
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] per byte transferred.
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please let me know if you would like to explore the implementation details for any of these
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] approaches, and I can provide the relevant configuration steps or architectural outlines.
2026-04-17 18:34:54 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:37:05 [nitro@ryzen5] i want to clear the fifo error once i toggle the led
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] To clear the TX stall flag, you need to write a `1` back to the specific bit in the `fdebug`
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] register. The RP-series microcontrollers use a "write-1-to-clear" (WC) mechanism for these hardware
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] debug flags, meaning writing a `1` clears the flag, and writing a `0` leaves the other flags
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] unaffected.
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Following your preference for named reusable functions, you can define a dedicated function to
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] handle this:
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] void clear_pio_tx_stall(PIO pio, int sm) {
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint32_t stall_mask = 1u << (PIO_FDEBUG_TXSTALL_LSB + sm);
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pio->fdebug = stall_mask;
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can then integrate this into your main loop where you check for the stall. Here is how your
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] updated debug check would look:
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t stall_mask = 1u << (PIO_FDEBUG_TXSTALL_LSB + VideoStateMachine);
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (pio0->fdebug & stall_mask) {
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led = true;
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			clear_pio_tx_stall(pio0, VideoStateMachine);
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		} else {
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			led = false;
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		gpio_put(LED_PIN, led);
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] By clearing the flag immediately after detecting it, the LED will only remain lit if the DMA is
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] continuously failing to keep up with the PIO's TX FIFO demand. If it was just a transient stall,
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] the LED will turn off on the next loop iteration.
2026-04-17 18:37:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:41:38 [nitro@ryzen5] cool, the stall is only happening once at the start, i think the skipping every 32 pixels half way across each scanline is due to combfilter getting out of sync with pixel clock
2026-04-17 18:43:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] That is excellent debugging. If the TX stall flag is only tripping once at the very start, it
2026-04-17 18:43:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] simply means the PIO state machine starts executing slightly before the DMA has completely filled
2026-04-17 18:43:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] the initial 8-word FIFO buffer. Once that initial buffering catches up, your DMA is perfectly
2026-04-17 18:43:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] keeping pace with the pixel clock.
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your hypothesis about the TV's comb filter struggling to lock onto a 14.85 MHz monochrome signal
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] without a color burst is a very logical guess for analog video artifacts. However, the specific
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] symptom you mentioned—"skipping every 32 pixels half way across each scanline"—actually points to a
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] much more precise digital culprit hidden in your C++ rendering logic.
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] Take a look at your `initFrame` function. You are rendering the C64 text starting at `x = 420`
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] (which is exactly halfway across your 768-pixel scanline):
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] drawC64Char(cc, 420 + x * 8, 60 + y * 10);
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] Now, let's trace the math inside your `drawC64Char` function for the first few characters:
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Char 1 (x=420): `420 & 31 = 4`. The 8-bit character is shifted left by 4. It occupies
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] bits 4–11 of the 32-bit word. (Perfect)
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Char 2 (x=428): `428 & 31 = 12`. Occupies bits 12–19. (Perfect)
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Char 3 (x=436): `436 & 31 = 20`. Occupies bits 20–27. (Perfect)
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Char 4 (x=444): `444 & 31 = 28`. The 8-bit character is shifted left by 28.
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is where the skip happens: shifting an 8-bit value left by 28 means the bottom 4 bits of the
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] character occupy bits 28–31 of the integer. The top 4 bits are shifted into bits 32–35, which do
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] not exist in a 32-bit integer. They are truncated and lost.
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the font is exactly 8 pixels wide, this word-boundary overflow will happen exactly every 4
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] characters (which is exactly every 32 pixels). The right half of every 4th character is being
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] erased, creating a visual gap that looks like a sync skip!
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] To fix this, you just need to catch the bits that overflow the 32-bit boundary and push them into
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] the next word in the array. Here is the updated function:
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] void drawC64Char(char c, int x, int y) {
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint64_t m = fontMem[c];
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int offset = 3 + (x >> 5) + y * words;
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int shift1 = (x & 31);
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		int shift0 = (7 - i) * 8;
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		int bits = (m >> shift0) & 0xff;
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t stib = flipBits(bits);
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// Write the bits that fit into the current 32-bit word
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		frame[offset] |= stib << shift1;
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// If the 8-bit character crosses the 32-bit boundary,
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// shift the remaining bits into the next word.
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (shift1 > 24) {
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			frame[offset + 1] |= stib >> (32 - shift1);
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		offset += words;
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] Once you apply this, the text halfway across the screen should render perfectly solid without any
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 32-pixel gaps.
2026-04-17 18:43:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
