2026-04-17 18:47:35 [nitro@ryzen5] i would like to discuss steps to introduce interlace, starting with sending the same field for odd and even
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is a fantastic next step. Moving from 288p to 576i is a classic rite of passage in video
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] generation.
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] To get a CRT or upscaler to interlace the image, you do not actually need to rewrite your PIO to
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] understand "half-lines" right away. You can achieve interlace by exploiting how analog sync
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] separators work.
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the theory and the steps to implement it using your current architecture:
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Step 1: The Magic of 625 Lines*
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Currently, your PIO generates 3 lines of VSYNC + 309 lines of video (because `frame[1] = 308`,
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] which loops 309 times). This equals 312 lines per field.
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because 312 + 312 = 624 (an even number), the CRT beam resets to the exact same vertical position
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] every field. This creates a progressive 288p image.
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Standard PAL is 625 lines. Because 625 is an odd number, dividing it by 2 fields gives
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 312.5 lines per field. That 0.5 line remainder perfectly shifts the CRT beam down by half a
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] scanline on the second field, physically weaving the lines together.
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] To trick the display into interlacing without changing your PIO timing, you simply alternate your
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] field lengths:
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Even Field: 312 lines (VSYNC + 309 lines)
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Odd Field: 313 lines (VSYNC + 310 lines)
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 312 + 313 = 625. The hardware will naturally interlace!
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Step 2: Pad the Frame Buffer*
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your PIO pulls pixel data from the FIFO during the `active` loop for every line it
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] draws, asking it to draw an extra line on the odd field means it will pull an extra line's worth of
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] words from the FIFO.
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] You must ensure your `frame` array is large enough to hold this extra line, otherwise, the DMA will
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] read past the end of the array. If your current `wordCount` is exact, add the width of one line in
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] words to its definition:
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] // Add one extra line of words to accommodate the 313-line field
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] const int maxWordCount = wordCount + (704 / 32);
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t frame[maxWordCount];
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Step 3: Toggle the Field Configuration*
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Instead of manually reloading the DMA in your main loop, we can extract this into a clean, reusable
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] function that handles the alternating field logic and adjusts the DMA transfer count accordingly.
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] void queue_next_field(int frame_counter) {
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Determine if this is the 313-line field
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	bool is_odd_field = (frame_counter & 1);
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// frame[1] is the line counter. 308 = 309 lines, 309 = 310 lines.
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frame[1] = 308 + is_odd_field;
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Calculate how many words the PIO will consume this field
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Assuming 'words' is your global variable for words-per-line
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint32_t transfer_count = wordCount + (is_odd_field ? words : 0);
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Reconfigure and restart the DMA
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_set_trans_count(video_dma_chan, transfer_count, false);
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, frame, true);
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Step 4: Update the Main Loop*
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Now, replace the DMA reload block in your `main()` loop with this new function:
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		if (!dma_channel_is_busy(video_dma_chan)) {
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			f++;
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			queue_next_field(f);
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • What to expect:*
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] When you run this, the total frame rate will drop slightly from 50.08Hz to exactly 50.00Hz, and the
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] image should appear to vibrate slightly vertically. This is the classic "interlace flicker."
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because you are sending the exact same pixel data for both fields, the TV is drawing the same image
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] twice, but offset by half a scanline.
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Once you have this vertical shift working, the next step would be splitting your rendering logic to
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] draw the even rows of your C64 font on Field 0, and the odd rows on Field 1!
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know how this tests on your display.
2026-04-17 18:48:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:01 [nitro@ryzen5] looks good, on lcd monitor, no flicker
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] That is an excellent result! The fact that it is rock solid on an LCD monitor confirms your
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 625-line timing is absolutely perfect.
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] The reason you are not seeing the classic "interlace flicker" is due to how modern LCD monitors
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] handle analog signals. LCDs cannot physically draw interlaced lines like a CRT beam; instead, they
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] pass the signal through a digital de-interlacer. Because you are currently sending the exact same
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixel data for both the Even and Odd fields, the monitor's de-interlacer weaves them together
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly, resulting in a completely stable progressive image. On a real CRT, you would likely
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] notice a very subtle 25Hz vertical shimmer on horizontal edges.
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Now that the hardware sync is successfully generating a 576i signal, the next step to achieve true
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] interlaced resolution is to separate your pixel data so you can send different image data for the
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Even and Odd fields.
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is how you can architect that transition:
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Dual Field Buffers*
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Instead of a single `frame` array, you will want two separate arrays. This allows you to render
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] your progressive 576-line logical image split across the two 288-line physical fields.
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] // Even field (312 lines total)
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t frameEven[wordCount];
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] // Odd field (313 lines total - needs the extra line capacity)
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t frameOdd[wordCount + words];
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Hardcoded Headers*
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because you now have two dedicated buffers, you no longer need to dynamically update the line
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] counter in your main loop. You can set them once during initialization, which saves a few CPU
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycles:
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] void initHeaders() {
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Even Field Header
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frameEven[0] = 28511;
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frameEven[1] = 308;
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frameEven[2] = 767;
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Odd Field Header
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frameOdd[0] = 28511;
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frameOdd[1] = 309; // One extra line for the 625-line offset
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	frameOdd[2] = 767;
2026-04-17 19:01:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. Update the DMA Dispatcher*
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your `vid_field` function can now simply alternate the read address and transfer count between the
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] two buffers based on the field counter:
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] void vid_field(int field) {
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	if (field & 1) {
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// Dispatch Odd Field
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		dma_channel_set_trans_count(video_dma_chan, wordCount + words, false);
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, frameOdd, true);
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	} else {
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		// Dispatch Even Field
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		dma_channel_set_trans_count(video_dma_chan, wordCount, false);
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, frameEven, true);
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 4. Adapting the Rendering Logic (The fun part)*
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Once the memory is split, your drawing functions (`plot`, `rect`, `drawC64Char`) will need to know
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] which buffer to write to.
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] For a true 576i coordinate system, a Y-coordinate of `0` goes to line 0 of `frameEven`. A
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Y-coordinate of `1` goes to line 0 of `frameOdd`. A Y-coordinate of `2` goes to line 1 of
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] `frameEven`, and so forth.
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can update your offset calculation in your rendering functions to automatically route the
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] pixels. For example, a basic `plot` function for a 576-line canvas would look like this:
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] void plot_576i(int x, int y) {
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint32_t bit = 1u << (x & 31);
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Divide Y by 2 to get the physical line within the field
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int physical_y = y >> 1;
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int offset = 3 + (x >> 5) + (physical_y * words);
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Check the lowest bit of Y to determine the field buffer
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	if (y & 1) {
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		frameOdd[offset] |= bit;
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	} else {
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		frameEven[offset] |= bit;
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] By transitioning to this dual-buffer setup, you effectively double your vertical resolution. You
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] could then draw 16-pixel high fonts, or render perfectly smooth diagonal lines that utilize the
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] half-scanline offset!
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let me know if you would like to proceed with splitting the buffers, or if you want to test the
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] current setup on a CRT first.
2026-04-17 19:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
