2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] That is a brilliant result! Seeing those artifacts actually means you have successfully achieved
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] true 576i hardware interlacing.
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is exactly what is happening with both of the visual changes you noticed:
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. The "Invisible" Top Lines (The Coordinate Shift)*
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] When you were rendering in progressive 288p, your logical Y-axis went from `0` to `287`. Now that
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] you are rendering in 576i, your logical Y-axis goes from `0` to `575`.
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your rendering functions correctly divide `y` by 2 to find the physical scanline, a
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] coordinate like `y = 40` (where your top text was) now maps to physical line `20`.
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] On an analog signal, the first ~30 to 40 lines are actually part of the Vertical Blanking Interval
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] (VBI) and overscan area—they are physically drawn off the top edge of the screen! By halving the
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] physical line number, you accidentally pushed your top graphics up into the invisible overscan
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] area.
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] To fix this and restore your original layout, you just need to double all the Y-coordinates and
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] heights in your `initFrame` function:
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] int initFrame(){
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	evenFrame[0]=28511;
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	evenFrame[1]=308;
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	evenFrame[2]=767;
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	oddFrame[0]=28511;
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	oddFrame[1]=309;
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	oddFrame[2]=767;
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int x=0;x<width;x++){
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		for(int i=1;i<10;i++){
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			// Doubled base Y (128) and amplitude (40)
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			int y=128+40*(sinf(x*i/50.0)+i);
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			plot(x,y);
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-04-17 19:19:24 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i=0;i<12;i++){
