2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. The Flickering Sine Waves (Interline Twitter)*
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] The flickering you see on the sine waves is the ultimate proof that your interlacing is working
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly!
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your `plot()` function draws a pixel exactly 1 line high, that pixel only exists in
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] one of the two fields (either Even or Odd). This means that specific pixel is only flashing
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] at 25Hz (half the frame rate). On high-contrast horizontal lines, this creates a strobe effect
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] known in broadcast television as "interline twitter."
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] To resolve this, classic game consoles and broadcast graphics systems use an "anti-flicker filter,"
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] which is essentially a slight vertical blur. For your generator, the easiest way to eliminate the
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] flicker is to ensure horizontal lines are always at least 2 pixels thick, so they appear in both
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] the Even and Odd fields. You can achieve this by modifying your plot function to draw a 2-pixel
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] high dot:
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] void plot_thick(int x, int y) {
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	plot(x, y);
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	plot(x, y + 1); // Ensures the pixel exists in both fields
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] • A note on your pointer math:*
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] I want to commend your implementation of the pointer arithmetic in `rect` and `drawC64Char`.
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t *frame=((y&1)?oddFrame:evenFrame)+(y>>1)*words;
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] frame[offset]|= ...
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] Adding the Y-offset directly to the base pointer while keeping the 3-word header and X-offset
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] inside the array index `[offset]` is an incredibly elegant and fast way to route the pixels without
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] calculating the full offset every single time.
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 19:19:25 [gemini-3.1-pro] You now have a fully functional, DMA-driven 576i video generator!
