2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] Everything looks incredibly well integrated! The code is clean, the state machines are properly
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] sharing `pio0`, and the clock math is perfectly aligned.
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] Before you flash this to the RP2350, there is one subtle but critical timing detail we need to
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] address regarding the Interlace Drift.
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Interlace Drift Problem
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your main loop continuously feeds the 15-word `wstFrame` into the Teletext FIFO.
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] If we add up the lines in `initTeletext()`:
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Skip 6 lines
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Transmit 1 line
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Skip 305 lines
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total = 312 lines.*
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, because you successfully implemented 576i interlacing, your Video state machine alternates
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] between 312 lines (Even field) and 313 lines (Odd field).
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the Teletext state machine is blindly looping a 312-line pattern, when the Video SM draws
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] the 313-line Odd field, the Teletext SM will finish early. It will immediately start its next
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] frame, causing the Teletext data to physically drift down the screen by one scanline every single
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] frame!
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Solution: DMA-Driven Teletext
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] To perfectly lock the Teletext data to the alternating fields, we can create an Even and Odd
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] command array, and use a second DMA channel to dispatch them at the exact same moment we dispatch
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] the video fields. This also completely removes the need to poll the Teletext FIFO in your
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] `while(true)` loop!
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the specific updates to implement this:
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Create Even and Odd WST Arrays*
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t wstEven[15];
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] uint32_t wstOdd[15];
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] void initTeletext() {
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int p = 0;
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// --- EVEN FIELD (312 Lines) ---
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = (5 << 1) | 0;     // Skip 6 lines
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = (11 << 1) | 1;    // Transmit 1 line (12 words)
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = 0x15E45555;       // CRI, Framing, Mag/Row
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for (int i = 0; i < 11; i++) wstEven[p++] = 0xA0A0A0A0; // Payload
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = (304 << 1) | 0;   // Skip 305 lines (Total: 312)
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// --- ODD FIELD (313 Lines) ---
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	p = 0;
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstOdd[p++] = (5 << 1) | 0;      // Skip 6 lines
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstOdd[p++] = (11 << 1) | 1;     // Transmit 1 line (12 words)
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstOdd[p++] = 0x15E45555;        // CRI, Framing, Mag/Row
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for (int i = 0; i < 11; i++) wstOdd[p++] = 0xA0A0A0A0; // Payload
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstOdd[p++] = (305 << 1) | 0;    // Skip 306 lines (Total: 313)
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Setup a Teletext DMA Channel*
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Add this right below your `viddmainit` function:
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] int wst_dma_chan;
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] void wst_dma_init(int sm) {
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wst_dma_chan = dma_claim_unused_channel(true);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_config c = dma_channel_get_default_config(wst_dma_chan);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_transfer_data_size(&c, DMA_SIZE_32);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_read_increment(&c, true);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_write_increment(&c, false);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Pace the DMA based on the Teletext PIO TX FIFO
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	channel_config_set_dreq(&c, pio_get_dreq(pio0, sm, true));
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	dma_channel_configure(wst_dma_chan, &c, &pio0->txf[sm], NULL, 15, false);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. Dispatch Both DMAs Together*
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Update your `vid_field` function to trigger both the Video and Teletext DMAs simultaneously:
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] void vid_field(int field) {
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	if (field & 1) { // Odd Field
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		dma_channel_set_trans_count(video_dma_chan, wordCount + words, false);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, oddFrame, true);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		dma_channel_set_read_addr(wst_dma_chan, wstOdd, true);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	} else {         // Even Field
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		dma_channel_set_trans_count(video_dma_chan, wordCount, false);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, evenFrame, true);
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:22:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		dma_channel_set_read_addr(wst_dma_chan, wstEven, true);
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 4. Clean up `main()`*
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can now completely remove the `while (!piosmistxfifofull(pio0,
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] TeletextStateMachine))` block from your main loop, keeping your CPU free. Just remember to call
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] `wstdma_init(TeletextStateMachine);` during your setup phase.
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] With this architecture, your Teletext generator is perfectly frame-locked, DMA-driven, and
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] completely immune to CPU jitter. You are ready to flash!
2026-04-17 20:23:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:27:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] The code is looking incredibly solid! You have successfully built a dual-DMA, frame-locked video
2026-04-17 20:27:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] and data generator.
2026-04-17 20:27:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:27:04 [gemini-3.1-pro] There is just one tiny remnant of the old code left in your `main()` loop that will cause a
