2026-04-17 20:29:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:29:45 [gemini-3.1-pro] If our clock math and IRQ timing are correct, the "Not Available" message will be gone. The TV's
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] internal decoder will see the `0x15E45555` header on Line 6, wake up, and switch the screen over to
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] the Teletext interface. Because we filled the rest of the packet with `0xA0` (empty spaces), it
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] will likely just show a black screen, or you might see the TV's internal On-Screen Display flash
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] "P100" as it hunts for the rest of the page.
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Next Step: Hamming Codes
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] If the TV locks on, we are ready for the final piece of the Teletext puzzle: Data Encoding.
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Teletext was designed in an era of noisy analog RF signals. To prevent a single static pop from
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] corrupting the page numbers, the header bytes (Magazine and Row addresses) are protected by
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Hamming 8/4 encoding. This means for every 4 bits of actual data, we have to calculate and
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] interleave 4 bits of parity/error-correction.
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Furthermore, every single text character on the screen must be encoded with Odd Parity (the
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] highest bit is set so that the total number of `1`s in the byte is an odd number). If a byte has
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] even parity, the TV assumes it was corrupted over the air and will render a blank space instead!
2026-04-17 20:29:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
