2026-04-17 20:31:58 [nitro@ryzen5] No Signal!
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] I know exactly what happened, and it is a fantastic lesson in how the RP2350 handles hardware
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] multiplexing and analog video timing.
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] The "No Signal" means the TV completely lost the horizontal sync pulses.
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Culprit: The HSYNC Overlap
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] If we do the math on the Teletext packet:
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • We are sending 12 words (384 bits).
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • At 6.9375 Mbps (2 cycles per bit at 13.875 MHz), 384 bits takes exactly 55.35
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] microseconds.
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Your active video line is only 51.71 microseconds, and the Front Porch is 1.64 µs.
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] This means the Teletext state machine is still transmitting its final padding bytes when the Video
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machine triggers the HSYNC pulse!
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] In your `vidblit.pio`, you generate the video signal using an Open-Drain technique. You
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] leave `pins` set to `1` (White), and you draw pixels by toggling `pindirs` (Input = Black, Output =
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] White). During HSYNC, `vidblit` forces `pindirs = 1` and `pins = 0` to pull the line to 0V.
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, in the `wst.pio` code I gave you, I used `out pins, 1`.
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the Teletext padding (`0xA0`) contains `1`s, the `wst` state machine was writing `1`s to
2026-04-17 20:33:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] the `pins` register during the HSYNC pulse. This forced the sync pulse to 3.3V (White)
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] instead of 0V, completely destroying the video sync!
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Fix: Drive `pindirs`, not `pins`
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] The RP2350 has a brilliant feature: when multiple state machines drive the same pin, their Output
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] Enables (`pindirs`) and Output Levels (`pins`) are logically OR'd together.
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] If we change `wst.pio` to manipulate `pindirs` exactly like `vidblit.pio` does, the sync crush
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] becomes impossible. During HSYNC, `vidblit` forces `pins = 0`. Even if `wst` forces `pindirs = 1`,
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] it will just OR with `vidblit`'s direction, and the pin will safely remain driven LOW.
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] You do not need to change a single line of your C++ code. Just update your `wst.pio` file
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] to this:
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; wst.pio (c)2026 Simon Armstrong
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; WST Teletext Generator
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; Runs at 13.875 MHz (2 cycles per bit = 6.9375 Mbps)
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] .program wst
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] public start:
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pull block          ; Fetch command from CPU
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out y, 1            ; Shift 1 bit into Y (Flag: 0=Skip, 1=Transmit)
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out x, 31           ; Shift 31 bits into X (Count)
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp !y skip         ; If Y is 0, branch to skip lines
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] transmit:
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait 1 irq 0        ; Wait for video SM (automatically clears flag)
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] word:
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pull block          ; Fetch 32 bits of Teletext payload
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set y, 31           ; Set bit counter for 32 bits
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] bit:
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out pindirs, 1      ; <--- CHANGED: Drive pindirs to match vidblit!
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp y-- bit         ; Loop (Takes 1 cycle). Total = 2 cycles per bit!
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp x-- word        ; Loop until all words in the packet are sent
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set pindirs, 0      ; <--- CHANGED: Force pin back to Black (High-Z)
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp start           ; Fetch next command
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] skip:
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait 1 irq 0        ; Wait for video SM (automatically clears flag)
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp x-- skip        ; Loop until we have skipped the requested number of lines
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp start           ; Fetch next command
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:33:23 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
