2026-04-17 20:35:41 [nitro@ryzen5] no signal still
2026-04-17 20:36:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] I understand exactly why you are still seeing "No Signal." The TV is completely rejecting the video
2026-04-17 20:36:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] because the Horizontal Sync (HSYNC) is still being destroyed, but for a completely different reason
2026-04-17 20:36:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] this time: The Teletext packet is physically too long!
2026-04-17 20:36:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:39 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the math on what is happening:
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • You are sending 12 words (384 bits).
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • At 6.9375 Mbps, 384 bits takes 55.35 µs.
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Your active video line is 51.71 µs, and the Front Porch is 1.64 µs (Total = 53.35 µs).
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your Teletext state machine is running 2 microseconds into the HSYNC pulse. Even with
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] `pindirs`, because the RP2350 logically ORs the pins, the Teletext state machine is forcing the pin
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] HIGH while the Video state machine is desperately trying to pull it LOW for sync. The TV sees no
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] sync pulse, panics, and drops the signal.
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] Furthermore, I missed a critical PIO default: because we didn't explicitly tell the `wst` state
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] machine to set its internal `pins` register to `1`, it defaulted to `0`. So when it enabled
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] `pindirs`, it was actually pulling the line to Black anyway!
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Ultimate Fix
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] We need to stop the Teletext state machine at exactly 360 bits (the official WST standard
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] of 45 bytes).
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 360 bits takes exactly 51.89 µs, which fits perfectly inside the active video window and finishes
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.46 µs before HSYNC even begins!
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because 360 bits is 11 full words plus 8 bits of the 12th word, we can hardcode this exact
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] bit-length into the `wst.pio` assembly. We also add `set pins, 1` so it correctly drives White.
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] Replace your entire `wst.pio` file with this:
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; wst.pio (c)2026 Simon Armstrong
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; WST Teletext Generator
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] ; Runs at 13.875 MHz (2 cycles per bit = 6.9375 Mbps)
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] .program wst
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] public start:
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pull block          ; Fetch command from CPU
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out y, 1            ; Shift 1 bit into Y (Flag: 0=Skip, 1=Transmit)
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out x, 31           ; Shift 31 bits into X (Count for skips)
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp !y skip         ; If Y is 0, branch to skip lines
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] transmit:
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait 1 irq 0        ; Wait for video SM (automatically clears flag)
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set pins, 1         ; CRITICAL: Ensure OUT=1 so pindirs=1 drives White!
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; Send exactly 11 full words (352 bits)
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set x, 10           ; Loop 11 times (0 to 10)
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] word_loop:
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pull block
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set y, 31
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] bit_loop:
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out pindirs, 1      ; Drive OE (1=White, 0=Black)
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp y-- bit_loop
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp x-- word_loop
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	; Send exactly 8 bits of the 12th word (Total = 360 bits / 45 bytes)
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pull block
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set y, 7
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] final_bits:
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out pindirs, 1
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp y-- final_bits
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set pindirs, 0      ; Packet complete. Force pin back to High-Z (Black)
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp start           ; Fetch next command
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:40 [gemini-3.1-pro] skip:
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wait 1 irq 0        ; Wait for video SM
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp x-- skip        ; Loop until we have skipped the requested number of lines
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	jmp start           ; Fetch next command
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why this works:
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. `set pins, 1`: This ensures that when the PIO sets `pindirs` to 1, it actually outputs
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3.3V (White).
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Exact 360-bit cutoff: It pulls the 12th word from your C++ array, outputs the first 8
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] bits of it, and then instantly shuts down and returns to black. It will never bleed into the HSYNC
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulse.
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] You do not need to change a single line of your C++ code. The C++ DMA will still feed 12
2026-04-17 20:36:41 [gemini-3.1-pro] words, and the PIO will consume exactly 12 words.
