2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. A Tiny C++ Parity Fix
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] While checking the data, I also realized I gave you the wrong hex code for the empty spaces.
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Teletext requires Odd Parity (an odd number of `1`s in the byte).
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] An ASCII space is `0x20` (in binary: `00100000`). Because it only has a single `1`, it
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] already has odd parity! My previous suggestion of `0xA0` had two `1`s (even parity), which
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] the TV would reject as a corrupted transmission.
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] Update the `initTeletext()` loop in your `vidblit.cpp` to use `0x20` instead of `0xA0`:
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Words 1 to 11: The remaining 40 bytes of the packet (plus padding)
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for (int i = 0; i < 11; i++) wstEven[p++] = 0x20202020; // 0x20 is Odd Parity Space
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Make sure to update this in both the `wstEven` and `wstOdd` sections).*
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] With the `irq clear 0` instruction in place, the Teletext state machine will now obediently wait
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] for the Video state machine on every single line, fire exactly 360 bits, and cleanly shut down well
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] before HSYNC.
2026-04-17 20:38:46 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
