2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is a very tight and elegant loop, but there are two hardware-specific bugs in it that will
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] prevent it from working.
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. `out pindirs, 0` does not do what you think.*
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] The `out` instruction tells the PIO to shift a specific number of bits (from 1 to 32) out of the
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] Shift Register. `out pindirs, 0` actually tells the PIO to shift 32 bits (0 is interpreted
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] as 32 in PIO assembly), which will instantly empty your OSR and write garbage to the pin
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] directions.
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] To force the pin to a specific value, you must use `set`.
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. `pins` must be High.*
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you only toggle `pindirs` (Output Enable), the actual voltage driven to the TV depends on the
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] `pins` (Output Level) register. If `pins` defaults to `0`, setting `pindirs` to `1` will drive 0V
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] (Black). We must explicitly set `pins` to `1` so that enabling the output drives 3.3V (White).
2026-04-17 21:48:32 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
