2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] Looking at your files, I can see exactly why the video is still dropping: The C++ arrays and
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] the PIO program are speaking two different languages.
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] You successfully updated `wst.pio` to the ultra-compact 8-instruction version.
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, your `initTeletext()` function is still using the 15-word array structure from the older
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] design (e.g., `(5 << 1) | 0`).
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the new 8-instruction PIO simply reads `x` and loops that many times, when it reads `(5 <<
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1) | 0` (which evaluates to 10), it thinks it needs to transmit 11 bits of garbage. When it reads
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] `(304 << 1) | 0` (which evaluates to 608), it blasts 609 bits of garbage, completely destroying the
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] video sync.
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Fix
2026-04-17 21:51:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] For the 8-instruction PIO, the DMA must feed exactly one command word per line of video.
2026-04-17 21:51:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] • To skip a line, send `0`.
2026-04-17 21:51:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] • To transmit Teletext, send `359`, immediately followed by the 12 payload words.
2026-04-17 21:51:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:51:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the exact updates for `vidblit.cpp` to align the memory with the new PIO logic:
