2026-04-17 21:53:21 [nitro@ryzen5] how many bits per line and how many lines?
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the exact breakdown of the numbers and how they dictate the memory layout:
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. How many lines?
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] A standard PAL signal has 625 lines per frame, split across two interlaced fields:
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Even Field: 312 lines
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Odd Field: 313 lines
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because our new PIO program requires exactly one command word for every single video line, the
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] baseline size of our arrays must be 312 words and 313 words, respectively.
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. How many bits per line?
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] A standard World System Teletext (WST) packet is exactly 45 bytes long.
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 45 bytes × 8 = 360 bits per line.
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] At 6.9375 Mbps, 360 bits takes exactly 51.89 microseconds to transmit, which fits perfectly inside
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] the 52µs active video window.
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. How this equals 324 and 325 words
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because our DMA and FIFO operate in 32-bit words, we have to pack those 360 bits into 32-bit
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] chunks.
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 360 bits / 32 = 11.25 words.
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Therefore, we must provide 12 payload words to the PIO to fulfill the 360 bits.
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (The PIO shifts the 360 bits, and the remaining 24 bits of the 12th word are instantly flushed
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] from the shift register when the PIO pulls the command word for the next line).*
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] So, the math for the DMA arrays is:
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Even Field (`wstEven`):*
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 311 lines skipped = 311 words
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1 line transmitting = 1 command word + 12 payload words
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total = 324 words
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Odd Field (`wstOdd`):*
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 312 lines skipped = 312 words
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1 line transmitting = 1 command word + 12 payload words
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Total = 325 words
2026-04-17 21:53:42 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:32 [nitro@ryzen5] so teletext just transmits a single payload line per frame?
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Actually, no! Real broadcast Teletext transmits on many lines per frame.
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] I only put a single payload line (Line 6) into our `initTeletext()` function as a "Hello World"
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] minimal test. A single line broadcasting the Page Header is the absolute minimum amount of data
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] required to wake up the TV's decoder and prove our hardware timing is perfect.
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] How Real Teletext Works
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Vertical Blanking Interval (VBI) is the dark area at the top of the screen where the CRT
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] electron beam is moving back to the top left corner. In the PAL standard, lines 6 through 22 (and
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] lines 318 through 335 on the odd field) are invisible on the screen.
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Broadcasters would pack Teletext data into all of those invisible lines!
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If you transmit 1 line per field (like our current code), you send 50 packets per second.
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since a full Teletext page is 24 rows, it takes about half a second to transmit one page.
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] • If you transmit 16 lines per field (the broadcast standard), you send 800 packets per
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] second. This allows you to transmit entire carousels of hundreds of pages that the user can
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] instantly browse.
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] Expanding our Architecture
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] The beauty of the 9-instruction PIO and DMA architecture we just built is that it can scale to as
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] many lines as you want!
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you want to transmit 10 lines of Teletext data per field, you simply change the C++ array to
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] send the `359` command and 12 payload words 10 times in a row during the VBI window.
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] For example, to transmit on Lines 6, 7, and 8, the C++ logic would look like this:
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Lines 0-5: Skip
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i=0; i<6; i++) wstEven[p++] = 0;
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Line 6: Transmit Header (Row 0)
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = 359;
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = 0x15E45555; // Header Payload...
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i=0; i<11; i++) wstEven[p++] = 0x20202020;
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Line 7: Transmit Text (Row 1)
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = 359;
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = 0x...       // Row 1 Payload...
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i=0; i<11; i++) wstEven[p++] = 0x20202020;
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Line 8: Transmit Text (Row 2)
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = 359;
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = 0x...       // Row 2 Payload...
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i=0; i<11; i++) wstEven[p++] = 0x20202020;
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Lines 9-311: Skip
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i=0; i<303; i++) wstEven[p++] = 0;
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (You would just need to increase the array size and the DMA transfer count by `12` for every
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] extra line you add).*
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] For right now, transmitting just 1 line is the safest way to ensure the TV locks onto the signal
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] and the video remains stable. Once we see that "P100" or the black Teletext screen on your TV, we
2026-04-17 21:54:51 [gemini-3.1-pro] can easily expand the arrays to transmit the entire 24-row page in a fraction of a second!
