2026-04-17 21:57:56 [nitro@ryzen5] sorry i feel stupid, how does transmitting 360 bits not leave unwanted bits in the fifo?
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please do not feel stupid at all. You have zeroed in on the exact mechanism that makes or breaks
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] this entire architecture. It is a brilliant question.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are absolutely correct: shifting 360 bits out of 384 bits does leave 24 bits left over.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] The secret lies in the difference between the TX FIFO (where the DMA puts the words) and
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] the OSR (Output Shift Register, where the PIO holds the word it is currently shifting), and
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] how the `pull` instruction behaves.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the exact step-by-step of how the hardware perfectly cleans up those 24 leftover bits
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] without losing sync:
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Setup
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] For a transmitting line, the DMA pushes exactly 13 words into the TX FIFO:
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Word 1: The Command (`359`)
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Words 2 to 13: The Payload (12 words = 384 bits)
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Execution Trace
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. The Manual Pull: At the very top of the PIO loop, it executes `pull block`. This takes
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Word 1 (the command) out of the FIFO and puts it into the OSR.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Emptying the OSR: The PIO executes `out x, 32`. The command moves into the X register,
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] and the OSR is now completely empty.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. The Autopull kicks in: The PIO enters the 360-bit loop and executes `out pindirs, 1`.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the OSR is empty, the hardware `autopull` automatically fetches Word 2 from the
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] FIFO into the OSR, and shifts out the first bit.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Shifting: The PIO continues shifting. Every time it shifts 32 bits, the OSR empties, and
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] `autopull` automatically grabs the next word from the FIFO.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 5. The 352nd bit: The OSR empties for the 11th time. Autopull grabs Word 13 (the
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 12th and final payload word) from the FIFO.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 6. The 360th bit: The PIO shifts 8 bits out of Word 13. The loop finishes.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] At this exact moment, you are right: there are 24 bits of garbage left sitting in the OSR.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Magic Cleanup
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] The loop ends, and the PIO jumps back to the `start` label:
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] public start:
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	set pindirs, 0
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	pull block          ; <--- THE MAGIC HAPPENS HERE
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	out x, 32
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] When the PIO executes that explicit, manual `pull block` instruction for the next video
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] line, it reaches into the FIFO, grabs the next Command word, and hard-overwrites the OSR.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] Those 24 leftover bits from the previous line are instantly destroyed. They are never shifted to
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] the pin.
2026-04-17 21:58:16 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why the FIFO doesn't desync
2026-04-17 21:58:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the DMA sent exactly 13 words, and the PIO consumed exactly 13 words (1 manual pull + 12
2026-04-17 21:58:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] autopulls), the TX FIFO is perfectly aligned. For the next line, the DMA sends a `0` (Skip
2026-04-17 21:58:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] command), the PIO manually pulls it, overwrites the OSR, sees the `0`, and skips the line.
2026-04-17 21:58:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 21:58:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] It is a hardware sleight-of-hand: we use `autopull` to feed the payload, and a manual `pull` to
2026-04-17 21:58:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] flush the garbage and load the next command!
2026-04-17 21:58:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
