2026-04-17 22:15:45 [nitro@ryzen5] Not Available when i hit the teletect button - TV may only decode teletext from RF
2026-04-17 22:16:22 [nitro@ryzen5] many times odd and even fields are swapped, are we missing bits that tell receiver which frame it is
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] You do not need an RF modulator. Almost all European TVs decode Teletext directly from the
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Composite AV input perfectly fine!
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your hypothesis about the TV rejecting the data is correct, but it is not because of the Odd/Even
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] fields. I reviewed the raw bits we are sending, and there are two critical data formatting
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] bugs causing the TV's decoder to ignore the packet.
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Bug 1: The Framing Code is Backwards
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] The WST standard says the Framing Code is `11100100` (in order of transmission).
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] In my previous code, I gave you `0xE4`. But because our PIO shifts bits out Least Significant
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Bit (LSB) first, sending `0xE4` actually transmits `00100111` over the wire! The TV is looking
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] for the sync pattern and seeing complete garbage.
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] To send `11100100` LSB-first, the byte must be `0x27`.
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Bug 2: The Data is Arriving Too Early
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Analog Teletext decoders are incredibly strict about timing. The standard dictates that the Clock
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Run-In must begin exactly 12.0 microseconds after the falling edge of the HSYNC pulse.
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because our `vidblit.pio` triggers the Teletext IRQ the exact moment the active video starts, our
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] Clock Run-In is starting at 10.5 µs. We are 1.5 µs too early, meaning the TV's data slicer
2026-04-17 22:17:33 [gemini-3.1-pro] hasn't even opened its window yet!
2026-04-17 22:17:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:17:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Fix: We can perfectly delay the signal by simply prepending one byte of `0x00`
2026-04-17 22:17:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] (Black) to the start of the packet. At 6.9375 Mbps, 8 bits takes 1.15 µs. This pushes our Clock
2026-04-17 22:17:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] Run-In to exactly 11.65 µs*, hitting the TV's sweet spot perfectly.
2026-04-17 22:17:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:17:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] The C++ Update
2026-04-17 22:17:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] I noticed in your last file that the `initTeletext()` function was still using the hardcoded
2026-04-17 22:17:34 [gemini-3.1-pro] `0x15E45555` array instead of calling the `buildwstheader` function.
