2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] How a TV Identifies Odd vs. Even Fields
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] In an analog video signal, there are no digital "flags" or hidden bits that say "I am the Odd
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] field." The TV determines the field identity purely through analog timing—specifically, the
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] relationship between the Vertical Sync (VSYNC) and the Horizontal Sync (HSYNC).
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] In a true interlaced PAL signal:
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Even Field: The VSYNC pulse aligns perfectly with the start of a horizontal scanline.
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Odd Field: The VSYNC pulse is offset by exactly half a scanline (32
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] microseconds).
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] The TV's sync separator measures this offset. If it sees the half-line offset, it confidently
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] routes the video to the Odd field buffer.
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why Your Fields are Inverting
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Look at your `initFrame()` function:
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	evenFrame[0]=28511; // VSYNC pulse length
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	...
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	oddFrame[0]=28511;  // VSYNC pulse length
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are sending the exact same 28,511-cycle VSYNC pulse for both fields!
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because there is no half-line offset, the TV's sync separator is flying blind. It sees 312 lines,
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] then 313 lines, but the VSYNC timing never shifts. The TV is literally guessing which field is
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] which, which is why they randomly swap every time you turn it on.
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why This Kills Teletext
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Teletext decoders are notoriously strict. The WST standard dictates that data on lines 6-22 belongs
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] to the Even field, and data on lines 318-335 belongs to the Odd field.
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] If the TV's sync separator cannot mathematically prove which field it is looking at (because the
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] half-line offset is missing), the Teletext decoder assumes the signal is coming from a non-standard
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] source (like a VCR in fast-forward, or an old 288p game console) and it completely disables
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] itself to prevent drawing corrupted garbage on the screen.
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Fix: The Half-Line Offset
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your PIO runs at 9,504 cycles per line.
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] Exactly half a line is 4,752 cycles.
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] To create a mathematically perfect interlace that the TV and Teletext decoder will recognize, we
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] just need to add exactly 4,752 cycles to the Odd field's VSYNC pulse!
2026-04-17 22:21:29 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
