2026-04-17 22:33:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is a brilliant question. Looking at the C++ code, it seems impossible for the TV to know the
2026-04-17 22:33:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] difference, because `evenFrame` and `oddFrame` are now mathematically identical.
2026-04-17 22:33:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:33:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] The secret is not in the data we send, but in the continuous timeline of the PIO state
2026-04-17 22:33:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] machine.
2026-04-17 22:33:17 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is how the TV's sync separator figures it out:
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. The HSYNC Metronome
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Inside the TV, there is a phase-locked loop (PLL) that locks onto your Horizontal Sync (HSYNC)
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulses. Think of this like a metronome ticking exactly every 64 microseconds. It never stops, and
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] it expects a pulse on every single beat.
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. The 0.5 Remainder
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because we set the total length of our field to exactly 312.5 lines, we created a
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] deliberate mathematical offset.
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] Let's trace the PIO as it runs continuously:
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Start of Frame: The PIO starts Field 1 exactly on the beat (Phase 0.0).
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • End of Field 1: The PIO runs for exactly 312 and a half lines. Therefore, Field 1
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] finishes exactly halfway between two beats of the TV's metronome (Phase 0.5).
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Start of Field 2: The PIO does not stop. It immediately begins Field 2 at Phase 0.5.
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • End of Field 2: Field 2 runs for 312.5 lines. `0.5 + 312.5 = 313.0`. Field 2 finishes
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] exactly on the beat again!
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. The TV's Perspective
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] The TV determines the field by looking at the relationship between the VSYNC pulse and its internal
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] HSYNC metronome.
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Odd Field (Field 1): The TV detects the VSYNC pulse ending perfectly aligned with an
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] HSYNC pulse. It says, "Ah, they align perfectly. This is the Odd field. I will start drawing at
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] the top-left of the screen, and I will route the VBI data to the Teletext decoder as Lines
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 6-22."
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Even Field (Field 2): The TV detects the VSYNC pulse ending exactly halfway
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] between two HSYNC pulses. It says, "Ah, there is a half-line offset. This is the Even field. I
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] will shift my electron beam down by half a scanline to weave the image, and I will route the VBI
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] data to the Teletext decoder as Lines 318-335."
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] By making the fields exactly 312.5 lines long, your RP2350's hardware naturally creates this phase
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] shift every single time the DMA loops. You don't have to send a flag or tell the TV which field is
2026-04-17 22:33:18 [gemini-3.1-pro] which; the physical timing of the signal forces the TV to figure it out automatically.
