2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why the Fields are Flipping
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because our PIO uses a simplified VSYNC (just holding the pin at 0V for a few lines), it lacks the
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] complex "equalizing pulses" that broadcast TV uses to explicitly tell the TV which field is which.
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Without a half-line phase shift between the fields, the TV just guesses when it turns on. If it
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] guesses wrong, the fields are swapped, and the interlace jitters backwards.
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Earlier, we tried to fix this by adding a half-line to the VSYNC (3.5 lines / `33263` cycles).
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, holding the pin at 0V for 3.5 lines starved the TV's horizontal phase-locked loop (PLL),
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] causing it to drop the signal entirely.
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] The "2.5 Line" Fix
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Instead of adding a half-line to VSYNC, we can subtract a half-line.
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Standard PAL broadcast VSYNC (the broad pulses) is actually exactly 2.5 lines long!
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] If we make the Even field VSYNC 2.5 lines, and the Odd field VSYNC 3.0 lines, we create the exact
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 180-degree phase shift the TV needs to lock the field dominance, but we keep the 0V pulse short
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] enough that the TV's horizontal sync won't crash.
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] Half a line is exactly 4,752 cycles.
2026-04-17 22:42:09 [gemini-3.1-pro] `28511 - 4752 = 23759`.
