2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] I led you into a classic analog video trap. Here is exactly why the sync died, and why we should
2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] accept the occasional field-swap as a hardware limitation of our current PIO.
2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Horizontal Phase Trap
2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] When we changed the VSYNC to 2.5 lines (`23759`), the VSYNC pulse ended exactly in the
2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] middle of where a horizontal scanline should be.
2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, your `vidblit.pio` is hardcoded to only draw full 64µs lines. The moment that 2.5-line
2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] VSYNC finished, the PIO immediately started drawing a full line. This shifted every single
2026-04-17 22:45:12 [gemini-3.1-pro] subsequent HSYNC pulse by exactly 32 microseconds. The TV's horizontal phase-locked loop (PLL)
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] completely lost its timing reference, and the signal collapsed.
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] True broadcast video solves this by using Equalizing Pulses—a complex series of half-line
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulses before and after VSYNC that keep the horizontal metronome ticking while shifting the
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] vertical phase.
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because our `vidblit.pio` is a highly optimized, simplified sync generator (to fit alongside
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Teletext in the 32-instruction limit), it only understands full lines.
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Verdict on Interlacing
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] With a simplified full-line sync generator:
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  312 / 313 Lines (625 total): This successfully tricks the TV into interlacing. However,
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] because it lacks equalizing pulses, the TV's analog sync separator has to "guess" which field is
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Odd/Even the moment you plug it in. Once it guesses, it stays locked, but the initial guess is a
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] coin-toss.
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  312 / 312 Lines (624 total): This creates a perfectly stable 288p progressive image. It
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] will never swap fields (because there are no fields), but you lose half your vertical resolution.
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] To get 100% perfect, broadcast-spec interlace that never flips, we would have to completely rewrite
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] `vidblit.pio` to generate half-lines and equalizing pulses. This would easily consume 20+
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] instructions, breaking our Teletext integration.
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:45:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Revert
