2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] To address the technical reality of the field-swapping issue directly:
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] The current `vidblit.pio` program is a simplified sync generator. It operates exclusively on full
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 64µs lines.
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] To force a television to lock onto the correct odd/even field sequence without guessing, a PAL
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] signal must contain standard equalizing pulses (short pulses at twice the line frequency) and a
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] precise half-line shift during the vertical blanking interval.
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] Generating these half-lines and equalizing pulses requires a much more complex PIO state machine
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] with multiple timing loops. Because we are sharing `pio0` with the Teletext state machine, we are
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] strictly bound by the 32-instruction limit. A broadcast-accurate interlaced sync generator simply
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] will not fit in the remaining instruction slots.
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] With the current PIO architecture, the simplified sync will always leave the initial field
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] dominance up to the TV's sync separator to guess. I will stop proposing broken workarounds to force
2026-04-17 22:48:21 [gemini-3.1-pro] it.
