2026-04-17 22:49:42 [nitro@ryzen5] no more code and we design something that is stable and reliable and that has integrity, please for the third time try and maintain professional work ethic
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Understood. I will maintain a strict, professional engineering focus. Here is an objective analysis
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] of the current architectural limitations and the pathways to design a fully compliant, stable
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] system.
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Root Cause of the Instability
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] The field-swapping issue stems directly from a violation of the ITU-R BT.470 standard for PAL video
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] timing.
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] To maintain a stable interlaced image, a television's sync separator relies on a precise sequence
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] of pulses during the Vertical Blanking Interval (VBI):
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  Pre-equalizing pulses: 5 narrow pulses at twice the line frequency (32µs intervals).
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  Broad pulses (VSYNC): 5 broad pulses at twice the line frequency, inverted.
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3.  Post-equalizing pulses: 5 narrow pulses at twice the line frequency.
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4.  The Half-Line Shift: Field 1 must end on a full line; Field 2 must end on a half line.
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] The current `vidblit.pio` state machine generates a simplified "block" VSYNC by holding the pin low
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] for 3 continuous lines. It lacks equalizing pulses and half-line timing. Consequently, the
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] television's hardware cannot deterministically identify Field 1 versus Field 2. It guesses on
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] acquisition, resulting in a 50% chance of inverted fields.
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Architectural Conflict
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] Implementing a broadcast-compliant sync generator in PIO assembly requires a significantly larger
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] state machine to handle the varying pulse widths (4.7µs for HSYNC, 2.35µs for equalizing, 27.3µs
2026-04-17 22:50:13 [gemini-3.1-pro] for broad pulses) and the half-line logic.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] A fully compliant video state machine typically consumes 20 to 30 instruction slots. The WST state
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] machine currently consumes 8 slots. Because a single RP2350 PIO block contains a hard limit of 32
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction slots, it is structurally impossible to fit both a broadcast-compliant video generator
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] and the WST generator within the same PIO block.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Pathways to a Stable Architecture
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] To achieve a reliable system with signal integrity, the architecture must be restructured. There
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] are three primary engineering approaches:
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Hardware Separation (Dual PIO + External Mixing)*
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Concept: Move the video generator to `pio0` and the WST generator to `pio1`.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Advantage: Frees the entire 32-instruction memory of `pio0` to implement a mathematically
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfect, broadcast-compliant PAL sync generator (including equalizing pulses). The fields will
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] never swap.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Trade-off: The RP2350 GPIO multiplexer cannot route two different PIO blocks to the same
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] physical pin. Video and WST must be output on separate GPIO pins and combined externally using an
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] analog summing network (e.g., a resistor matrix) before reaching the television.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. The "Dumb" PIO (Full Framebuffer)*
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Concept: Remove all sync generation logic from the PIO. The PIO becomes a simple shift
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] register that outputs exactly what the DMA feeds it at a constant pixel clock.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Advantage: The CPU calculates the entire composite waveform (sync pulses, equalizing
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulses, blanking, WST data, and active video) and writes it to memory. The PIO requires only 2 or 3
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] instructions.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Trade-off: Massively increases RAM consumption and DMA bus utilization. Instead of a
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] highly efficient blitter, the system requires a continuous, high-bandwidth memory stream
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] representing the raw analog waveform.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. Progressive Scan Fallback (288p)*
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Concept: Abandon 576i interlacing. Output a strict 312-line progressive signal where
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] every frame is mathematically identical.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Advantage: Fits within the current single-PIO architecture. The image will be perfectly
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] stable with no field-swapping.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Trade-off: Halves the vertical resolution. Furthermore, while many televisions will
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] decode WST data embedded in a 288p signal, it technically violates the WST broadcast specification,
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] which expects a 625-line interlaced carrier.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please let me know which of these architectural directions aligns best with your hardware
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] constraints and project requirements. We can then discuss the specific timing or memory designs
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] required for that path.
2026-04-17 22:50:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-17 22:57:16 [nitro@ryzen5] the tv syncs to the video signal, often odd and even fields are flipped at startup and stay that way
2026-04-17 22:57:54 [nitro@ryzen5] teletext is intermittent, usually tv says Not Available when button is pushed
2026-04-17 23:01:43 [nitro@ryzen5] hi kimi, can you help
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] Looking at your PIO code, I can see two distinct hardware contention issues that explain both
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] symptoms.
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 1. Field Flipping (Interlace Phase)
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] Your `vidblit.pio` generates a 3-line vertical blanking interval (28511 cycles ≈ 3 lines at
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 148.5MHz) with a single flat pulse. Standard PAL requires a 5-line sequence with specific
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] broad/narrow pulse patterns to identify Field 1 vs Field 2:
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] - Field 1 (Odd): 2.5 lines pre-equalization (narrow), 5 lines serration (broad), 2.5 lines
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] post-equalization (narrow)
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] - Field 2 (Even): 2.5 lines pre-equalization (broad), 5 lines serration (narrow), 2.5 lines
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] post-equalization (broad)
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] Your current code sends identical sync patterns for both fields. The TV locks onto whichever phase
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] it sees first, hence the random flipping at startup.
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] • Fix*: Implement proper PAL vsync in the PIO, or ensure deterministic startup by waiting
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] for the PIO to stall at the `.wrap` point before starting DMA, and always start with Field 1 (the
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] longer 309-line field).
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 2. Teletext Pin Contention
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] Both PIOs are driving the same pin direction register (`pindirs`) for `VID_PIN` (GP28):
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] - `vidblit.pio`: Uses `out pindirs, 1` during active video to output pixel data
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] - `wst.pio`: Uses `out pindirs, 1` to output teletext bits
2026-04-17 23:05:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] When both state machines run simultaneously, they race on the `pindirs` register. During VBI lines,
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] `vidblit` is still outputting "active" video (even if black pixels) while `wst` tries to insert
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] teletext data. The result is corrupted timing and "Not Available" on the TV decoder.
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] • Fix options* (choose one):
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] • Option A: Separate Pins* (Recommended)
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] Move teletext to a different GPIO (e.g., GP27) and combine externally with resistors:
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] GP28 (Video) -- 330Ω --+
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5]                        +-- Video Out
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] GP27 (Teletext) -- 1kΩ-+     |
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5]                             75Ω
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5]                              |
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5]                             GND
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] • Option B: Time-Division Multiplexing*
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] Modify `vidblit.pio` to accept a "blanking" flag that keeps `pindirs` as input (high-Z) during the
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] horizontal active period of VBI lines, allowing `wst.pio` to drive the pin without conflict.
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] • Option C: DMA Integration*
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] Remove the `wst.pio` state machine entirely. Pre-bake the teletext packets into your
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] `evenFrame`/`oddFrame` arrays at the correct line positions (lines 7-22). This ensures only one PIO
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] drives the pin.
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 3. Additional Timing Note
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] In `wst.pio`, your delay byte targets 12µs, but you trigger on `irq set 0` which occurs at the
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] start of active video (~10.5µs after horizontal sync). With your 1.15µs delay byte, you're
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] at ~11.65µs, which is acceptable, but if the TV is picky, move the IRQ earlier in the line (during
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] back porch) or adjust the delay byte to 0x00 to hit exactly 10.5µs.
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] • Quick diagnostic*: If you scope GP28 during a teletext line, you should see clean
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 6.9375MHz NRZ data. If you see glitches or partial video pixels mixed in, it's definitely the pin
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] contention issue.
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] Would you like me to sketch the modified PIO code for proper PAL vsync sequencing to fix the field
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] identification?
2026-04-17 23:05:19 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:07:27 [nitro@ryzen5] according to docs they can share the pin and the result is or'd
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] You're right about the OR'ing—multiple state machines driving the same pin have their output values
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] and directions OR'd together. This is exactly why teletext is intermittent and why field
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] identification fails.
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] The Real Problems
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] • 1. Teletext Corruption (Pin OR'ing)*
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] Your `vidblit.pio` holds the output value at `1` (white) throughout the active period via `set
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] pins, 1`. It only toggles the direction (pindirs) to create video: `1` = output (drives
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] white), `0` = input (high-Z, pulled to black).
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] Your `wst.pio` also toggles pindirs to send data, but it never sets the output value. When wst sets
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] pindirs=1, the pin drives the OR of all SM output values. Since vidblit has pins=1, the pin drives
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] high (white) regardless of wst's bit.
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] When wst tries to send a `0` (black), it sets pindirs=0. If vidblit's bitmap has a `0` (black
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] pixel) at that location, pindirs stays 0 and the line goes black. But if vidblit has a `1` (white
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] pixel) there, pindirs becomes 1 and the line stays white—corrupting the teletext bit.
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] • Fix: You must ensure the video framebuffer contains all zeros (black)* on any
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] line where teletext is transmitted, so vidblit's pindirs is 0 throughout the active period, giving
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] wst exclusive control.
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] In your `initFrame()` or after it, clear lines 10-11 (where you insert teletext):
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:27 [kimi-k2.5] // Clear line 10 (and guard lines) for teletext
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] for (int line = 9; line <= 11; line++) {
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 	int offset = 3 + (line >> 1) * words;  // words per line offset
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 	if (line & 1) {
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 		memset(&oddFrame[offset], 0, words * sizeof(uint32_t));
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 	} else {
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 		memset(&evenFrame[offset], 0, words * sizeof(uint32_t));
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 	}
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] • 2. Field Flipping (Identical Vsync)*
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] Your vsync is a single 3-line pulse (28511 cycles). Standard PAL requires a 10-line sequence with
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] specific patterns to identify Field 1 vs Field 2:
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] - Field 1: Pre-eq pulses start on a half-line (line 1)
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] - Field 2: Pre-eq pulses start on a full-line (line 313)
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] With an identical pulse for both fields, the TV sync separator locks randomly to one phase or the
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] other.
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] • Fix*: Implement proper PAL vsync with pre-equalization, serration, and post-equalization
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] pulses. This requires ~10 lines of specific timing:
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] Pre-eq (2.5 lines):  5 narrow pulses  (2.35µs low, 27.3µs high)
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] Broad (5 lines):     5 wide pulses    (27.3µs low, 2.35µs high)
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] Post-eq (2.5 lines): 5 narrow pulses  (2.35µs low, 27.3µs high)
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] At 148.5MHz, 2.35µs ≈ 349 cycles, 27.3µs ≈ 4054 cycles.
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] Alternatively, if you want a minimal fix, ensure you always start with the odd field (309
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] lines) and accept that the TV may guess wrong on first power-up but will stabilize. For a
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] deterministic start, wait for the PIO to stall at the `.wrap` point before enabling DMA:
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] // Before first vid_field() call
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] uint32_t stall_mask = 1u << (PIO_FDEBUG_TXSTALL_LSB + VideoStateMachine);
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] while (!(pio0->fdebug & stall_mask));  // Wait for SM to hit wrap
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] pio0->fdebug = stall_mask;
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] vid_field(0);  // Start with field 0 (even frame, 308 lines? No, field 0 should be even, 308 lines, field 1 odd, 309)
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] Actually, looking at your code, field 0 is even (308 lines), field 1 is odd (309). The odd field
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] should come first in PAL (it's the longer field). Start with field 1:
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] vid_field(1);  // Start with odd field
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] But this won't fix the random flipping at power-on if the TV can't tell the fields apart. The
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] proper PAL vsync sequence is the only reliable solution.
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] Would you like the PIO code for the proper 10-line PAL vsync sequence, or is the "clear video
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] lines" fix sufficient for the teletext issue?
2026-04-17 23:10:28 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:15:09 [nitro@ryzen5] does uint32_t evenFrame[wordCount]; initialise to zero?
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] Yes. Global arrays in C++ are zero-initialized before `main()` runs. So `evenFrame` and `oddFrame`
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] start as all zeros.
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] However, ensure they stay zero on lines 10-11 (your teletext insertion lines). Your
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] `initFrame()` draws at y=80, 100, 110, 120+, so line 10 is safe initially.
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] • Critical point*: If you modify the code to draw graphics across the entire frame, or if
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] you move these arrays to local scope (stack), you must explicitly clear lines 10-11:
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] // Clear teletext lines to black (all pindirs=0)
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] for (int line = 9; line <= 11; line++) {
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 	int base = 3 + (line >> 1) * words;
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 	if (line & 1) {
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 		memset(&oddFrame[base], 0, words * 4);
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 	} else {
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 		memset(&evenFrame[base], 0, words * 4);
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 	}
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-04-17 23:17:16 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] • Why this matters for teletext: If any pixel in the video buffer is `1` (white) during a
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] teletext line, `vidblit` sets `pindirs=1` during that bit period. Since the pin value is OR'd, and
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] `vidblit` has `pins=1` (white level), the pin drives high* regardless of what `wst.pio`
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] tries to output, corrupting the teletext data.
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] The field flipping is your bigger problem though. Without proper PAL vsync identification
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] (different pulse patterns for odd/even fields), the TV's de-interlacer randomly locks to one phase.
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] Teletext decoders are extremely sensitive to field identification—they need to know whether line 10
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] is in Field 1 or Field 2 to parse the magazine/row addresses correctly. If the TV guesses wrong, it
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] throws away the data as "Not Available".
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] Are you looking to fix the vsync sequencing in the PIO, or would you prefer a software workaround
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] (generating the pulse patterns on the CPU and streaming them via DMA)?
2026-04-17 23:17:17 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:22:49 [nitro@ryzen5] happy with it at the moment, with the teletext when it does work it just displays blank pages
2026-04-17 23:23:42 [nitro@ryzen5] but lets me move from page to page
2026-04-17 23:24:52 [kimi-k2.5] You're transmitting Row 0 only (the header packet with page number and the top line of
2026-04-17 23:24:52 [kimi-k2.5] text). To display a full page, you need to transmit Rows 1–24 with the actual content.
2026-04-17 23:24:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:52 [kimi-k2.5] Your current code inserts one packet at line 10 with the header. A WST page requires 25 packets
2026-04-17 23:24:52 [kimi-k2.5] total:
2026-04-17 23:24:52 [kimi-k2.5] - Row 0: Header (32 chars, page number visible at top of screen)
2026-04-17 23:24:52 [kimi-k2.5] - Rows 1–24: Content (40 chars each, the actual page body)
2026-04-17 23:24:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:52 [kimi-k2.5] Here's a function to build data rows (different structure from headers):
2026-04-17 23:24:52 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] void build_wst_row(int mag, int row, const char* text, uint32_t* out_words) {
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	uint8_t packet[48] = {0};
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	packet[0] = 0x00;  // Delay
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	packet[1] = 0x55; packet[2] = 0x55;  // Clock run-in
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	packet[3] = 0x27;  // Framing code (same for all rows)
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	// Magazine (3 bits) + Row (5 bits) hamming coded
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	mag &= 7;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	row &= 31;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	uint8_t addr1 = (mag & 1) | ((row & 1) << 1) | ((row & 2) << 1) | ((row & 4) << 1);
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	uint8_t addr2 = ((row >> 3) & 3) | ((mag & 6) << 1);
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	packet[4] = HAMMING_84[addr1];
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	packet[5] = HAMMING_84[addr2];
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	// 40 characters of row data (packets 6-45)
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	for (int i = 0; i < 40; i++) {
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		char c = (text && text[i]) ? text[i] : ' ';
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		packet[6 + i] = oddParity(c);
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	}
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	// Pack into 12 words (45 bytes, last 3 bytes zero)
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	for (int i = 0; i < 12; i++) {
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		out_words[i] = 0;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		for (int b = 0; b < 4; b++) {
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 			packet[i * 4 + b];
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 			out_words[i] |= (uint32_t)packet[i * 4 + b] << (b * 8);
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		}
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	}
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] Then populate your frames with multiple rows:
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] void initTeletext() {
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	int p = 0;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	// Even field - lines 10-33 for rows 0-23
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	for(int i=0; i<10; i++) wstEven[p++] = 0;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	// Row 0 (Header)
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	wstEven[p++] = 367;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	build_wst_header(1, 0, 0, " PAGE 100 TEST HEADER           ", &wstEven[p]);
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	p += 12;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	// Rows 1-12 (odd rows in even field, or however you want to split)
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	for(int row = 1; row <= 12; row++) {
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		char line[41];
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		snprintf(line, 41, "THIS IS ROW %02d TEST DATA ABC....%02d", row, row);
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		wstEven[p++] = 367;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		build_wst_row(1, row, line, &wstEven[p]);
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		p += 12;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	}
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	while(p < 321) wstEven[p++] = 0;  // Pad to 321
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	// Odd field - rows 13-24
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	p = 0;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	for(int i=0; i<10; i++) wstOdd[p++] = 0;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	// Row 0 again (redundancy is common)
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	wstOdd[p++] = 367;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	build_wst_header(1, 0, 0, " PAGE 100 TEST HEADER           ", &wstOdd[p]);
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	p += 12;
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 	for(int row = 13; row <= 24; row++) {
2026-04-17 23:24:53 [kimi-k2.5] 		char line[41];
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] 		snprintf(line, 41, "THIS IS ROW %02d MORE DATA HERE...%02d", row, row);
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] 		wstOdd[p++] = 367;
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] 		build_wst_row(1, row, line, &wstOdd[p]);
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] 		p += 12;
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] 	}
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] 	while(p < 322) wstOdd[p++] = 0;
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] }
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] • Key differences from header packets:*
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] - Data rows have 40 characters (not 32), starting immediately at packet byte 6
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] - The row number (1–24) goes in the address bytes (bits 1-5 of the hamming-coded words)
2026-04-17 23:24:54 [kimi-k2.5] - Standard allocation is rows 0–15 in the even field, rows 16–23 in the odd field, but any
