2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] The missing characters are likely caused by the bit count mismatch combined with autopull
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] behavior.
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] You're sending 12 words (384 bits) but telling the PIO to shift only 368 bits. With
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] autopull enabled, this leaves 16 bits stranded in the Output Shift Register when the loop
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] exits. When the state machine wraps back to `pull block`, the remaining bits are discarded to load
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] the next command word.
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] Since 368 bits = 46 bytes, and your packet is exactly 46 bytes, the math seems correct. However,
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] with 12 words (48 bytes), bytes 46 and 47 are zero padding. The issue is which bytes end up in
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] those last 16 bits.
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] With your packing loop:
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] - Word 11 (the 12th word) contains `packet[44], packet[45], packet[46], packet[47]`
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] - Bits 0-15: `packet[44], packet[45]` (last 2 characters of your 32-char header)
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] - Bits 16-31: `packet[46], packet[47]` (zero padding)
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] If the PIO stops at 368 bits, it sends bits 0-15 of word 11 (the last characters), and the padding
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] in bits 16-31 is lost. So the characters should be there.
2026-04-17 23:52:58 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] • But* with autopull enabled and a non-multiple-of-32 bit count, the interaction between
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] the final partial shift and the next `pull` can cause the last word to be refetched incorrectly or
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] the shift counter to desynchronize.
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] • Fix:* Send all 384 bits to ensure clean word boundaries:
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] wstEven[p++] = 384;  // Send all 12 words = 384 bits
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] The extra 16 zero bits (2 bytes) are harmless black level and ensure the OSR is completely empty
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] when the `pull block` executes, preventing any state machine stalls or FIFO misalignment.
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] If characters are still missing after this change, verify the `oddParity` function is generating
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] correct parity for all characters (some TVs are stricter than others on parity errors in the header
2026-04-17 23:52:59 [kimi-k2.5] text).
