2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] • Trace with 368 bits:*
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] 1. `out x, 32` - Autopulls command word (368), shifts into X
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] 2. Loop 368 times:
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5]    - Bits 0-31: Word 1 (autopull at bit 32)
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5]    - Bits 32-63: Word 2 (autopull at bit 64)
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5]    - ...
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5]    - Bits 320-351: Word 11 (autopull at bit 352)
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5]    - Bits 352-367: First 16 bits of Word 12
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] 3. Loop exits with 16 bits remaining in OSR, shift counter = 16
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] 4. `set pindirs, 0` - Return to black (back porch)
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] 5. `out x, 16` - Shifts final 16 bits into X (discarded), shift counter becomes 32, triggers
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] autopull of next command word
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] 6. `.wrap` to `start`
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] This ensures the last 2 characters (bytes 44-45 of your 46-byte packet) are actually shifted out to
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] the pin instead of being discarded.
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] • One caution:* The `set pindirs, 0` happens immediately after the last data bit, but
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] before the `out x, 16` discards the remaining bits. During those 16 shift cycles (32 PIO cycles at
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] 2 cycles/bit), the pin is held at black level while the state machine "chews through" the remaining
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] OSR bits. This extends the teletext packet slightly into the back porch, but that's acceptable as
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] long as it doesn't run into the next line's active video.
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] If characters are still missing after this change, check that your C code still has autopull
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] enabled (`true` in the third parameter of `smconfigsetoutshift`), otherwise the
2026-04-18 00:00:14 [kimi-k2.5] `out x, 16` will stall waiting for FIFO data instead of consuming the existing OSR contents.
2026-04-18 00:00:15 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:05:36 [kimi-k2.5] The `out x, 16` addition fixes the alignment issue. With 368 bits (46 bytes), you transmit exactly
2026-04-18 00:05:36 [kimi-k2.5] 11.5 words. The loop sends the first 368 bits (all your packet data including the last two
2026-04-18 00:05:36 [kimi-k2.5] characters), then `out x, 16` consumes the final 16 padding bits to align the shift register for
2026-04-18 00:05:36 [kimi-k2.5] the next autopull.
2026-04-18 00:05:36 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:05:36 [kimi-k2.5] • Why characters were missing before:*
2026-04-18 00:05:36 [kimi-k2.5] Without the trailing `out`, the autopull triggered at the end of the 368th bit left 16 bits
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] stranded in the OSR. When the SM wrapped back to `start`, those 16 bits were discarded to load the
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] next command word, chopping off the last two characters of your 32-character header.
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] • Current state:*
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] - Loop sends bits 0-367 (bytes 0-45: delay, CRI, framing, addresses, and all 32 header chars)
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] - `set pindirs, 0` forces pin to black (input)
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] - `out x, 16` shifts out the padding (bytes 46-47) to align the shift counter to 32
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] - Autopull triggers cleanly for the next line's command word
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] If specific characters still show as spaces, check the parity on those particular characters—some
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] TVs are stricter about odd parity errors than others, and will display a space or block character
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] rather than correcting a single bit error. The hardware framing is now correct.
2026-04-18 00:05:37 [kimi-k2.5] 
