2026-04-18 00:21:14 [nitro@ryzen5] the teletext header is not displaying correctly
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] The most likely cause is an error in the magazine and row address encoding. In WST, the
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] first two bytes after the framing code use Hamming 8/4 protection where the magazine bits must be
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] shifted to make room for row address bit 0.
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] Current incorrect code:
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] packet[4] = HAMMING_84[mag & 1];
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] packet[5] = HAMMING_84[(mag >> 1) & 3];
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] Correction for row 0 (header packet):
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] uint8_t row = 0;
2026-04-18 00:24:17 [kimi-k2.5] packet[4] = HAMMING_84[((mag & 7) << 1) | (row & 1)];  // Magazine (3 bits) + Row bit 0
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] packet[5] = HAMMING_84[(row >> 1) & 0x0F];             // Row bits 4-1
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] For magazine 1, row 0 (header), this generates `0x49, 0x15` instead of the incorrect `0x02, 0x15`.
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] • Additional checks:*
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 1. PIO Program Handling: Ensure your `wst.pio` program treats the first word (368) as a bit
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] counter, not video data. If the PIO shifts out the value 368 (0x170) to the pin, it will corrupt
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] the signal. The PIO should:
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5]    - Pull the count into a scratch register
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5]    - Loop for that many bits, pulling subsequent words from FIFO
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 2. Byte Packing Verification: The packing loop places byte 0 at bits [7:0]. With
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] `smconfigsetoutshift(&config2, true, true, 32)` (shift right), the PIO outputs bit
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 0 first (LSB), which matches WST requirements. Confirm your PIO uses `shift_right` (first parameter
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] `true`).
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 3. Timing: Verify the clock divider calculation. For 6.9375 Mbps with 2 PIO cycles per bit:
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5]    // 148.5 MHz / (6.9375 MHz * 2) = 10.7027...
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5]    sm_config_set_clkdiv(&config2, 10.7027f);
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5]    If the bit rate is off by more than ±3%, decoders will fail to lock.
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 4. DMA Transfer: The buffer contains 321/322 words with mostly zeros. Ensure your PIO
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] correctly interprets the zero words as "do nothing" (output constant black level) and doesn't try
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] to shift them out as Teletext data. The command word (368) should only trigger the bit output
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] sequence for the subsequent 11.5 words (368 bits).
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] 
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] • Debug suggestion*: Temporarily replace the Teletext payload with a known test pattern
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] (e.g., all `0x55` bytes after the framing code). If you see alternating white/black dashes on the
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] line where Teletext should appear, the bit timing and DMA are working, and the issue is definitely
2026-04-18 00:24:18 [kimi-k2.5] the packet encoding.
