2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] Important observation about your PIO transmitter
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] Your `wst.pio` does this:
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] out	x, 32
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] wait	1 irq 0
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] irq	clear 0
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] jmp	!x start
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] bit_loop:
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	out	pindirs, 1
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	jmp	x-- bit_loop
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] set	pindirs, 0
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] out	x, 16
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] This is a very clever trick: using `pindirs` as the modulated output against the resistor network.
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] But it also means the actual transmitted bitstream depends entirely on the OSR bit order
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] and the exact semantics of `out pindirs, 1`.
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] Since you configured:
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] sm_config_set_out_shift(&config2, true, true, 32);
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] that means shift right, autopull at 32 bits.
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] So bits are emitted LSB first from each 32-bit word.
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] That is probably what you want for teletext bytes, but there is a subtle consequence:
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] Your byte packing must exactly match the bit order expected by the TV
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] You pack like this:
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] out_words[i] |= (uint32_t)packet[idx] << (b * 8);
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] So within each 32-bit word:
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] - byte 0 goes in bits 7:0
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] - byte 1 in 15:8
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] - byte 2 in 23:16
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] - byte 3 in 31:24
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] With shift-right output, that transmits:
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] - byte 0 first, LSB-first
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] - then byte 1, LSB-first
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] - then byte 2, LSB-first
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] - then byte 3, LSB-first
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] That is internally consistent.
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] So if some characters are wrong, the packing itself is probably not grossly wrong.
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:47 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] The next strongest suspect: clock divider accuracy
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] You wrote:
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] sm_config_set_clkdiv(&config2, 10.7027f);
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] At 148.5 MHz, that gives:
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 148.5 / 10.7027 ≈ 13.8749 MHz
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] That’s very close.
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] But teletext decoders can be a bit picky. Small timing error plus analogue edge quality can corrupt
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] some bits while leaving most bytes readable.
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] Since you are seeing mostly correct text with a few wrong chars, this really does smell
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] like marginal decode quality, not a structural encoding bug.
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] Another likely cause: your CRI start position may be slightly off
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] You currently insert:
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[0] = 0x00; // 1.15us Delay to hit the 12.0us CRI sweet spot
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] That gives an extra 8 bits of blank before the 2-byte CRI.
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] At 6.9375 Mb/s, one byte is about 1.153 us, yes.
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] If the teletext burst is slightly too early or too late within the line, some TVs will still lock
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] but decode marginally.
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] That can produce exactly what you’re seeing:
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] - page recognised
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] - header visible
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] - occasional wrong characters
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] So I would absolutely treat horizontal placement as a prime suspect.
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] Also worth checking: line choice
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] You currently do teletext after 10 skipped lines in each field.
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:42:48 [gpt-5.4-2026] That may be okay, but some TVs are fussier about which VBI lines carry packet 0. If you’re landing
