2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] What that strongly suggests
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] A teletext decoder is seeing a valid-looking packet stream, but the packet identity and/or byte
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] alignment is wrong often enough that:
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] - the TV is treating many packets as belonging to many pages
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] - your header text bytes are only being decoded correctly about half the time
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] - the corruption is structured rather than random
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] That points to one of these:
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] 1. address bytes are wrong
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] 2. framing/byte alignment is off by one bit or one byte
2026-04-18 06:50:49 [gpt-5.4-2026] 3. clock run-in / framing code is not quite in the expected place
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 4. your first few bytes are shifted relative to what the TV expects
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] And from your code, I think the biggest remaining issue is:
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] Your packet 0 address field is almost certainly encoded incorrectly
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] This bit:
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] mag &= 7;
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[4] = HAMMING_84[mag & 1];
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[5] = HAMMING_84[(mag >> 1) & 3];
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] is not a proper packet address encoding.
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] For teletext, the two address bytes after framing are not simply:
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] - low bit of magazine
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] - upper two bits of magazine
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] They encode magazine and row number together as two 4-bit nibbles, then each nibble is
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] Hamming 8/4 encoded.
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] For packet 0, row = 0, but the row bits still occupy the address nibbles in a defined way.
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] If those two bytes are wrong, a decoder may:
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] - still recognise teletext
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] - still recover some bytes
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] - but assign packets inconsistently to pages/rows
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] That would fit your “all pages have some data” symptom very well.
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] Why “all pages have some data” is the key clue
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] If you were transmitting a clean packet 0 for page 100 only, then:
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] - page 100 should show your header
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] - most other pages should be blank or absent
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] But if all pages show some data, then the decoder is not seeing a stable page address.
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] That means the page number / magazine / row decoding is not reliable.
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] This is much more consistent with bad address bytes or framing alignment than with mere
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] signal noise.
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] Your page number split is also still awkward
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] You still have:
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] wstHeader(1, 0, 0, title, &wstEven[p]);
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] wstHeader(1, 0, 1, title, &wstOdd[p]);
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] So even/odd fields are still advertising different pages.
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] That alone can smear the decoder’s page cache.
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] Before anything else, make both fields the same page.
2026-04-18 06:50:50 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] wstHeader(1, 0, 0, title, &wstEven[p]);
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] wstHeader(1, 0, 0, title, &wstOdd[p]);
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] This is not optional for debugging now. You want one stable page only.
