2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] I think the next fix should be: correct packet 0 address encoding
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] For teletext packet address:
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - first address nibble = magazine + row bit 0..?
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - second address nibble = remaining row bits
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] For row 0:
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - the row contribution is zero
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - but the magazine still occupies the low 3 bits of the first nibble
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - the second nibble is zero
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] So for packet 0, magazine 1, the expected address bytes should be more like:
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - byte 1 = Hamming(0x1)
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - byte 2 = Hamming(0x0)
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] not your current split across `mag & 1` and `(mag >> 1) & 3`.
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] So this:
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[4] = HAMMING_84[mag & 1];
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[5] = HAMMING_84[(mag >> 1) & 3];
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] should probably be something like:
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] int row = 0;
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] int addr1 = (mag & 0x7) | ((row & 0x1) << 3);
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] int addr2 = (row >> 1) & 0xF;
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[4] = HAMMING_84[addr1];
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[5] = HAMMING_84[addr2];
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] For row 0, that becomes:
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `addr1 = mag`
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `addr2 = 0`
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] For magazine 1:
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `packet[4] = HAMMING_84[1]`
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `packet[5] = HAMMING_84[0]`
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] That is much more plausible.
2026-04-18 06:50:51 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
