2026-04-18 06:57:18 [nitro@ryzen5] 13.875 is definitly bit rate for WST?
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] Yes — 6.9375 Mb/s is the teletext bit rate, and if your PIO emits one bit every 2
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] state-machine cycles, then the SM clock must be 13.875 MHz.
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] So your comment:
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] - bit rate = `6.9375 MHz`
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] - PIO clock = `13.875 MHz` when using 2 cycles per bit
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] is correct.
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] In your PIO
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] This loop:
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] bit_loop:
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	out	pindirs, 1
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	jmp	x-- bit_loop
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] takes 2 instructions per transmitted bit:
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `out pindirs, 1`
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `jmp x-- bit_loop`
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] So one data bit lasts 2 SM cycles.
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] Therefore:
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] SM clock = 2 × bit rate
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 		 = 2 × 6.9375 MHz
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 		 = 13.875 MHz
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] And with a 148.5 MHz system clock:
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] clkdiv = 148.5 / 13.875
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	   ≈ 10.7027027
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] So this line is mathematically right:
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] sm_config_set_clkdiv(&config2, 10.7027f);
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] So the teletext rate is probably not your main bug
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] Given your symptoms, I would be more suspicious of:
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] - packet address encoding
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] - page number differing between fields
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] - burst start position within the line
2026-04-18 06:57:26 [gpt-5.4-2026] - framing / byte alignment
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] than the nominal bit rate itself.
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] One subtle point
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] People sometimes say “13.875 MHz teletext clock”, but that usually refers to the generator
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] clock in implementations that need 2 cycles per bit.
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] The actual transmitted symbol/bit rate for WST is:
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 6.9375 Mb/s
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] So both numbers can appear, depending on context.
2026-04-18 06:57:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
