2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] With page number now stable across fields, and magazine 1 accidentally okay for row 0, the next
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] strongest suspect is:
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] framing / run-in / byte alignment
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] You now have:
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[0] = 0x55;
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[1] = 0x55;
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[2] = 0x55;
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] packet[3] = 0x27;
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] Previously you had a delay byte then two `0x55`.
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] Now you have three `0x55` and no explicit delay.
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] That may be closer, but teletext decoders are picky about where the framing code lands relative to
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] the line.
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] If the decoder locks but samples one bit late/early, you get exactly the kind of “about half the
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] chars right” behaviour you describe.
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] Why 50% right matters
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] That does not feel like ordinary noise.
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] It feels like the decoder is:
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] - finding the packet
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] - but byte/bit slicing is marginal or offset
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] That can happen if:
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] - CRI is the wrong length
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] - framing code is not at the expected phase
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] - the burst starts too early or too late in the line
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] - the waveform polarity/level is slightly wrong
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] One important electrical/protocol detail
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] Your WST PIO outputs bits by driving `pindirs`, not `pins`:
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:45 [gpt-5.4-2026] out	pindirs, 1
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] That means a transmitted “1” is not “video high”, it is “actively drive whatever output value is
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] already latched”, while “0” is high-Z.
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] So the actual teletext waveform depends on:
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] - the latched output level on GP28
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] - resistor network
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] - TV termination
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] - what the video generator is doing at the same time
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] That is clever, but it also means your teletext eye pattern may not be especially clean.
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] If the TV recognises teletext but decodes characters inconsistently, this analogue side is a
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] serious suspect.
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] New/different content worth noting
2026-04-18 07:00:46 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
