2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] What you want instead
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] You want the WST trigger to occur at a fixed point in the blanking interval, ideally referenced
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] from the start of line timing so the teletext packet begins at about the correct horizontal
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] position.
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] Best fix
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] Move the `irq set 0` in `vidblit.pio` so it occurs during blanking, not at active start.
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] For example, place it after hsync and after enough back porch delay to hit the desired teletext
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] start.
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] Rough target
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:09 [gpt-5.4-2026] PAL teletext packet start is typically around 12 µs after sync leading edge.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] Your hsync is about 4.66 µs.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] So after hsync ends, you need roughly another:
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 12.0 - 4.66 = 7.34 µs
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] At 148.5 MHz that is:
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 7.34e-6 × 148.5e6 ≈ 1090 cycles
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] Your current back porch is only about 872 cycles, and your IRQ is at the end of that.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] So the IRQ is currently around:
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 4.66 + 5.87 ≈ 10.53 µs after sync start
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] depending on exact reference.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] That is already close-ish numerically, but the important thing is the teletext burst starts
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] immediately from that point and your preamble/framing timing may not line up with the decoder’s
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] expected window.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] Also, because your line structure is ordered active → front porch → sync → back porch, the IRQ
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] placement is being thought about relative to active video, which makes reasoning awkward.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] More practical interpretation
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] The teletext decoder wants to see:
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] - line sync
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] - then blanking
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] - then CRI
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] - then framing code
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] - all before visible picture
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] Your current WST launch is tied to the video state machine’s active section boundary, not
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] explicitly to the sync edge. That makes the burst placement fragile.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] Recommended change
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] Option 1: trigger WST from a point inside the back porch
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] Move `irq set 0` into the back porch loop at a chosen offset.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] For example, in `vidblit.pio`, instead of:
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] mov	y,isr
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] irq	set 0
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] active:
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] raise the IRQ during back porch after a controlled number of cycles.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] That way the WST packet starts in the VBI blanking interval.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] Option 2: add a programmable delay in `wst.pio`
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] Keep the line IRQ where it is, but after `wait 1 irq 0`, burn a known number of cycles before
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] outputting bits.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] This is probably the easiest experiment.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] For example:
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] public start:
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	out	x, 32
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	wait	1 irq 0
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	irq	clear 0
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	jmp	!x start
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	set	y, N
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] delay:
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	jmp	y-- delay
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] bit_loop:
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	out	pindirs, 1
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	jmp	x-- bit_loop
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] Then you can tune `N`.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] That lets you move the teletext burst horizontally without disturbing the main video timing.
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 07:27:10 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] How much delay to try
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] One WST bit takes 2 SM cycles at 13.875 MHz:
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 1 / 6.9375 MHz ≈ 144 ns per bit
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] One WST SM cycle is about:
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 1 / 13.875 MHz ≈ 72 ns
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] So a delay loop count gives you fairly coarse but useful movement.
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] Try enough delay to move the CRI/framing a few microseconds.
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] A starting sweep might be around:
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 0
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 16
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 32
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 48
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 64 cycles of the WST SM
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] That corresponds to roughly:
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 0 µs
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 1.15 µs
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 2.30 µs
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 3.46 µs
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - 4.61 µs
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] which is exactly the sort of range that can make or break teletext decode.
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] Why this matches your symptoms
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] Your symptoms have been:
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - TV recognises teletext
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - header sometimes partly right
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - behaviour changes with small preamble tweaks
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] - not strongly affected by small divider tweaks
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] That is classic horizontal placement sensitivity, not bit-rate sensitivity.
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] My recommendation
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] The easiest next test is:
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 1. keep your current packet contents
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 2. keep same page both fields
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 3. add a configurable delay in `wst.pio` after the IRQ wait
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 4. sweep that delay
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] That will tell you very quickly whether the burst is simply landing in the wrong place.
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] If you want, I can sketch the exact `wst.pio` change for a tunable post-IRQ delay.
2026-04-18 07:27:11 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:26 [nitro@ryzen5] i can use a plain nop [20] style delay before the bit_loop
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] Yes, absolutely.
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] A plain delay before `bit_loop` is a good simple test.
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] For example, after:
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] wait	1 irq 0
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] irq	clear 0
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] jmp	!x start
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] you can insert something like:
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 	nop		[20]
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] or several of them before `bit_loop`.
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] What that gives you
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] At your WST SM clock of `13.875 MHz`:
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] - one SM cycle ≈ `72.07 ns`
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `nop [20]` takes `21` cycles total
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] - so one `nop [20]` delays by about:
2026-04-18 07:44:36 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 21 / 13.875e6 ≈ 1.51 µs
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] That is a useful-sized step.
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] Useful delay values
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] You can think of it roughly like this:
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `nop [6]` ≈ 0.50 µs
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `nop [13]` ≈ 1.01 µs
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `nop [20]` ≈ 1.51 µs
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] - two `nop [20]` ≈ 3.03 µs
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] - three `nop [20]` ≈ 4.54 µs
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] That is a very good range for finding the decoder sweet spot.
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] Why this is a good experiment
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] It changes only the horizontal placement of the teletext burst, without changing:
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] - packet bytes
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] - bit rate
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] - DMA format
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] - video timing
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] So it isolates one important variable cleanly.
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] Suggested approach
2026-04-18 07:44:37 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
