2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] What “Not Available” tells us
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] That usually means the set is no longer seeing a decodable teletext packet at all, not just
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] corrupted text.
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] So that setting moved the framing/run-in outside the acquisition window.
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] That’s useful because it brackets the valid region.
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] Practical strategy
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] You now want to find the range where:
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] - teletext button gives a valid page/header
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] - header is most stable
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] Then, once found, if you want a final tiny trim, you can add a very small WST-side delay like:
2026-04-18 07:49:27 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:49:28 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `nop [2]`
2026-04-18 07:49:28 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `nop [4]`
2026-04-18 07:49:28 [gpt-5.4-2026] - `nop [6]`
2026-04-18 07:49:28 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:49:28 [gpt-5.4-2026] instead of `nop [20]`
2026-04-18 07:49:28 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
2026-04-18 07:49:28 [gpt-5.4-2026] At 13.875 MHz:
2026-04-18 07:49:28 [gpt-5.4-2026] 
