2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 3. What “Thumb-2” actually means on M33
2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] On the Cortex-M33, the processor is always in Thumb-2 mode. It can execute both:
2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] - Narrow (16-bit) instructions — when they exist and are sufficient.
2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] - Wide (32-bit) instructions — which start with 0xFxxx or 0xExxx.
2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] Many operations that were only available as 16-bit on M0+ now have better 32-bit versions on M33
2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] (especially loads, stores, adds with large immediates, moves of 16-bit constants, etc.).
2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:52:29 [grok-4.20-0309] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
