2026-04-20 16:58:19 [nitro@ryzen5] what is naming convention for thin and wide in m33 isa?
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] • Direct answer:*
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] In the official ARM Cortex-M33 ISA documentation, the naming convention for narrow and wide
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] instructions is:
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] - Narrow (16-bit) instructions are written with the suffix `.N`
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] - Wide (32-bit) instructions are written with the suffix `.W`
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] Examples from the M33 ISA:
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] ╭──────────────────────┬───────────────────┬───────────────────╮
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] │  Operation           │  Narrow form      │  Wide form        │
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] ├──────────────────────┼───────────────────┼───────────────────┤
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] │  Add                 │  `ADD.N`          │  `ADD.W`          │
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] │  AND                 │  `AND.N`          │  `AND.W`          │
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] │  Branch              │  `B.N`            │  `B.W`            │
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] │  Load Register       │  `LDR.N`          │  `LDR.W`          │
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] │  Move                │  `MOV.N`          │  `MOV.W`          │
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] │  Compare             │  `CMP.N`          │  `CMP.W`          │
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] ╰──────────────────────┴───────────────────┴───────────────────╯
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:23 [grok-4.20-0309] This `.N` and `.W` suffix is the official convention used in the ARM Architecture Reference
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] Manual (ARMv8-M) and in the Cortex-M33 Technical Reference Manual.
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] How this is typically reflected in code generators:
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] Most Thumb-2 assemblers and code generators follow one of these two styles:
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 1. Suffix style (most common in real toolchains):
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309]    add_n(reg d, reg s);     // narrow
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309]    add_w(reg d, reg s);     // wide
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 2. Explicit style:
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309]    add_narrow(...);
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309]    add_wide(...);
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] • For your project*, the cleanest and most widely understood convention on M33/Thumb-2 is:
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] - `xxx_n()` for narrow (16-bit)
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] - `xxx_w()` for wide (32-bit)
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] Would you like me to show how this would look applied to `adc`, `add`, and `ands` using this
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] convention?
2026-04-20 16:58:24 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:59:03 [nitro@ryzen5] no inane questions
2026-04-20 16:59:04 [grok-4.20-0309] • Answer:*
2026-04-20 16:59:04 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:59:04 [grok-4.20-0309] In the Cortex-M33 ISA, the standard naming convention is:
2026-04-20 16:59:05 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:59:05 [grok-4.20-0309] - Narrow (16-bit) instructions use the suffix `.N`
2026-04-20 16:59:05 [grok-4.20-0309] - Wide (32-bit) instructions use the suffix `.W`
2026-04-20 16:59:05 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:59:05 [grok-4.20-0309] So the canonical forms are `ADD.N`, `AND.N`, `LDR.N`, `MOV.N` etc. for narrow, and `ADD.W`,
2026-04-20 16:59:05 [grok-4.20-0309] `AND.W`, `LDR.W`, `MOV.W` etc. for wide.
2026-04-20 16:59:05 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 16:59:05 [grok-4.20-0309] That is the naming used in the official ARMv8-M Architecture Reference Manual.
2026-04-20 16:59:05 [grok-4.20-0309] 
