2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] • Positive changes:*
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] - All data-processing and load/store narrow instructions now consistently use `n` (e.g.
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] `adcn`, `andsn`, `addn`, `ldrn`, `lsln`, etc.).
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] - This is a clear improvement over the previous version.
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] • Remaining issues:*
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] 1. Inconsistent application
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309]    - `push` and `pop` still have no suffix (`push`, `pop`).
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309]    - `b` (both conditional and unconditional) still have no suffix.
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309]    - `swi` has no suffix.
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309]    - The two `bl` functions are still called `bl0` and `bl1` (these are the wide BL instruction).
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:00:09 [grok-4.20-0309] 2. Overloaded function names
2026-04-20 17:00:10 [grok-4.20-0309]    You now have multiple `addn` functions with different signatures. In C++ this is allowed
2026-04-20 17:00:10 [grok-4.20-0309] (overloading), but it becomes messy quickly when you start adding the wide versions later. For
2026-04-20 17:00:10 [grok-4.20-0309] example you currently have four different `addn` functions.
2026-04-20 17:00:10 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:00:10 [grok-4.20-0309] 3. Still no wide instructions
2026-04-20 17:00:10 [grok-4.20-0309]    The file is called `thumb2.h` and claims to be a "CORTEX-M33 thumb2 code generator", yet it only
2026-04-20 17:00:10 [grok-4.20-0309] contains narrow instructions except for the two `bl` halves. There are still no `.W` (32-bit)
