2026-04-20 17:04:27 [nitro@ryzen5] in the official isa not in my implementation
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] • Direct answer:*
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] In the official ARMv8-M ISA (the one used by the Cortex-M33), narrow instructions
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] always produce a 16-bit result.
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] There are zero narrow instructions that return a 32-bit result.
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] Official rule:
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] - Narrow (`.N`) = 16-bit encoding → always emits 16 bits.
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] - Wide (`.W`) = 32-bit encoding → emits 32 bits.
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] This is a hard architectural rule in Thumb-2. There is no such thing as a "narrow instruction that
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] generates 32 bits".
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] The only time you see 32-bit output from what looks like a narrow mnemonic is when the assembler
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] automatically promotes it to `.W` (e.g. `MOV.W`, `ADD.W`, `LDR.W`), but then it is no longer a
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] narrow instruction.
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] • Final answer:*
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] • 0*
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] No narrow instructions in the official M33 ISA return a 32-bit result. All narrow instructions are
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] strictly 16-bit encodings.
2026-04-20 17:04:29 [grok-4.20-0309] 
