2026-04-20 17:07:02 [nitro@ryzen5] explain how bl is 32 bit but can be used in narrow form
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] • Direct, clear explanation:*
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] • BL cannot be used in narrow form.*
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] `BL` (Branch with Link) is always a 32-bit instruction on Cortex-M33 (and all Thumb-2
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] processors). There is no 16-bit narrow version of `BL`.
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] Why this is the case:
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] - The 16-bit `B` instruction (which you have as `b_n`) can only reach ±2KB and does not
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] save the return address (no link).
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] - The `BL` instruction needs to store the return address in `LR` (`R14`). This requires more bits
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] than a 16-bit instruction can hold.
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] - Therefore, ARM defined `BL` as a permanent 32-bit instruction.
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] It is encoded using two 16-bit halves:
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] - First half (`bl0`): `0xF000` + upper 11 bits of offset
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] - Second half (`bl1`): `0xF800` + lower 11 bits of offset
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] Together they form one 32-bit instruction.
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] Official ISA names:
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] - `B.N`  → 16-bit narrow branch (no link)
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] - `B.W`  → 32-bit wide branch (no link)
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] - `BL`   → always 32-bit (there is no `BL.N`)
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] There is no such thing as `BL.N`.
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] What this means for your code:
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] In your current file you had:
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:06 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] u16 bl0(int off);
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] u16 bl1(int off);
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] This was actually the correct low-level way to emit a 32-bit `BL`.
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] The correct modern naming for a clean Thumb-2 generator should be either:
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] u32 bl(int offset);           // returns full 32-bit instruction as one value
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] // or
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] u32 bl_w(int offset);         // explicitly wide
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] Would you like me to show you the correct implementation for `bl` (returning `u32`) that follows
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] the same style as your current `_n` functions?
2026-04-20 17:07:07 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:08:51 [nitro@ryzen5] 
2026-04-20 17:09:08 [nitro@ryzen5] yes please this is the naming no?
2026-04-20 17:09:08 [nitro@ryzen5] 	u32 bl_w(int off) {return 0xf000|((off>>12)&0x7ff);}
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] • No. That is not correct.*
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] Here is the accurate answer:
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] Correct naming and implementation for `BL` on Cortex-M33:
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] // Correct: returns a 32-bit value containing both halves
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] u32 bl_w(int offset)
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] {
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309]     u32 upper = (offset >> 12) & 0x7FF;
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309]     u32 lower = (offset >> 1)  & 0x7FF;
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309]     return (0xF0000000u | (upper << 16)) | (0xF8000000u | (lower << 0));
2026-04-20 17:09:12 [grok-4.20-0309] }
