2026-04-20 17:09:56 [grok-4.20-0309] • One important issue:*
2026-04-20 17:09:56 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:56 [grok-4.20-0309] The current `bl_w` function assumes `off` is a byte offset. However, in almost all Thumb-2
2026-04-20 17:09:56 [grok-4.20-0309] branch instructions, the immediate is encoded as a word offset (i.e. divided by 4, or
2026-04-20 17:09:56 [grok-4.20-0309] right-shifted by 2).
2026-04-20 17:09:56 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] The official encoding for `BL` expects the offset to be a multiple of 4, and the lower 2 bits are
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] implicitly zero.
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] Your current line:
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] (((off >> 1) & 0x7FF) << 0)
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] should almost certainly be:
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] (((off >> 2) & 0x7FF) << 0)
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] Corrected and cleaner version:
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] // t19 - BL (always 32-bit, no .N form)
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] u32 bl_w(int off)
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] {
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309]     return 0xF0000000u
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309]          | (((off >> 12) & 0x7FFu) << 16)
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309]          | (((off >> 2)  & 0x7FFu) << 0);
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] }
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:09:57 [grok-4.20-0309] This is the standard way to encode `BL` when `off` is a byte offset from the current PC.
