2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] Why it looks like bits are repeated:
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] Because they are repeated in the encoding.
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] The BL instruction is a special 32-bit encoding that splits the 23-bit signed offset across two
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] 16-bit halves like this:
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] - Bits [10:0] of the target address go into the first half (`0xF000`)
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] - Bits [21:11] of the target address go into the second half (`0xF800`)
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] That’s why you see both `>> 12` and `>> 1` — they are pulling different chunks of the same offset.
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] 
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] The `>> 1` (not `>> 2`) is correct because the lowest bit of the address is always 0 in Thumb
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] state, so the encoding stores 23 bits of precision, not 22.
2026-04-20 17:11:42 [grok-4.20-0309] 
