2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] Actually, wait - I need to reconsider. Let me check the ARM Architecture Reference Manual...
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] For Thumb branches:
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] - The offset in the instruction is the number of halfwords (not bytes)
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] - So if you want to branch 4 bytes forward, you need `off = 2` (halfwords)
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] But your `bl_w` does `(off >> 1)` which suggests `off` is in bytes. So there's inconsistency.
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] Actually, looking at your code again:
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] - `bl_w` does `(off >> 1)` - so `off` is in bytes
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] - But `b_n` doesn't shift - so `off` would be in halfwords?
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] That's the real issue: inconsistency in what `off` represents.
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] The actual error:
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] Your branch functions are inconsistent:
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] - `bl_w(int off)` expects `off` in bytes (does `>> 1`)
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] - `b_n(int off)` expects `off` in encoded offset units (no shift)
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] They should all treat `off` the same way. Either:
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] 1. All take byte offsets and shift internally, OR
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] 2. All take halfword offsets and don't shift
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] Given that `blw` shifts, it seems the intent is that `off` is in bytes. So the `bn`
2026-04-20 18:13:55 [deepseek-chat] functions should also shift.
