2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] At a high level, you likely care about two encodings:
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] 1. `BLX (register)` — 16-bit Thumb encoding (often listed as T1)
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Branches to address in a register and sets `LR` for return (a call).
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Can switch instruction set state depending on target bit0 semantics (on M-profile, effectively
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] always Thumb state, but alignment/bit0 rules still matter).
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- This is probably what your stub `blx_n(reg16 rm)` is aiming for.
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] 2. `BLX (immediate)` — 32-bit encoding in ARMv7 (A/R profiles)
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- On Cortex-M33 specifically, interworking to ARM state is not a thing, so immediate `BLX` is
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] generally not part of normal M-profile usage the way `BL` is.
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- For your RP2350 Cortex-M33 generator, the practical “far call immediate” is usually just
2026-04-20 19:57:36 [gpt-5.3-codex] `BL` wide, not `BLX` immediate.
