2026-04-20 20:21:48 [nitro@ryzen5] does pico2 have commands for executable memory?
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] On Pico 2 (RP2350, Cortex‑M33) there isn’t an OS-level `mprotect`-style API because it’s
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] bare-metal by default.
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] Short answer:
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] - No virtual-memory execute permission commands like desktop OSes.
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] - You place code in executable memory regions (typically SRAM or XIP flash mapping as configured).
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] - If you generate/patch code at runtime, the key “commands” are cache + pipeline sync steps
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] (clean D-cache if present/used, invalidate I-cache, `DSB`, `ISB`) so the core executes fresh
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] instructions.
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] So for a JIT, the practical concern is usually coherency/barriers, not page permission
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] flips.
2026-04-20 20:21:55 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-21 06:22:21 [nitro@ryzen5] hi kimi
2026-04-21 06:22:28 [kimi-k2.6] Hello! How can I help you today?
