2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] This is a dense, purpose-built encoding surface for Thumb-1 and Thumb-2. A few observations:
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] • Register type safety* — splitting R0–R7, R8–R15, SP and PC into separate enums is a
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] deliberate design choice. It forces the caller to prove they know which register bank an
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] instruction accepts, and it prevents passing an R0–R7 value to something like `bxn`. That is
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] clean, though it does mean some helpers (e.g. `movww`) currently only accept `reg16` (R8–R15)
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] even though the MOVW/MOVT encoding itself supports the full 0–15 range in bits[11:8]. If you ever
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] need to load an immediate into R0–R7 you will likely want an overload taking `reg` or a unified
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] register type.
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] • Type tags as parameters* — `ldrn(reg d, regpc, int imm8)` uses `regpc` as an unnamed
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] parameter type. The caller must pass `PC`, but the value is otherwise ignored by the encoder
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] because the architecture implies the base. That is a neat self-documenting idiom. Likewise
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] `addn(regsp d, int imm7)` forces the caller to pass `SP`, although the body does not reference
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] `d`.
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] • Class-level constant* — `const int NONE = 255;` inside the struct body declares a
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] non-static data member. It will technically work as a compile-time value in some contexts, but it
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] carries instance storage semantics and ODR quirks. `static constexpr int NONE = 255;` is the safer
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] modern C++ form.
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] • Immediate discipline* — the helpers trust the caller to pre-scale or pre-mask values
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] (e.g. `imm8` in the PC-relative LDR is presumably already shifted, `imm7` in the SP add is
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] presumably already divided by 4). That is perfectly reasonable for a backend emitter, but it places
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] the burden on whoever builds the operand.
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] • Wide encodings* — the bit-twiddling in `bw`, `bcw`, `blw`, `movww` and
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] `movt_w` matches the standard Thumb-2 split-immediate layouts. The base masks (`0xf0009000`,
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] `0xf000d000`, etc.) are the expected HW1/HW2 pairs.
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 06:54:56 [kimi-k2.6] If you have the corresponding `.cpp` or the `rmachine`/`lmachine` implementation, I can also
2026-04-21 06:54:57 [kimi-k2.6] comment on the jump linking or register allocation logic.
2026-04-21 06:54:57 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] A few observations on the header:
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] • Architecture target*
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] This is a JIT code generator for the Raspberry Pi RP2350’s Cortex-M33, implementing the 16-bit
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] Thumb-1 “narrow” encodings (T1–T18) and a handful of 32-bit Thumb-2 “wide” encodings (`B.W`, `BL`,
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] `BLX`, `MOVW`, `MOVT`). The split between `n` and `w` helpers is clean.
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] • Notable API patterns*
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] • You use empty enum-type parameters (e.g. `u16 ldr_n(reg d, regpc, int imm8)`) to force the caller
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] to pass the `PC` enumerator. It’s a light-weight compile-time constraint even though the value
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] isn’t used in the body.
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] • `add_n` is heavily overloaded by parameter types (register/register, register/imm3,
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] register/imm8, `regsp`/imm7, etc.). That keeps the call sites readable but relies on the enum types
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] being distinct.
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] • Potential C++ gotchas*
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] • `const int NONE = 255;` inside the struct is a non-static data member; every `thumb2` object
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] carries it. If you meant it as a compile-time constant it should be `static constexpr int NONE =
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] 255;`.
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] • `and` → `ands` in the changelog makes sense because `and` is an alternative operator token in C++
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] and cannot be used as an ordinary identifier.
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] • The two `compile` overloads return different types (`int *` vs `int`). That’s legal, just
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] something to watch when maintaining call sites.
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] • Instruction encoding*
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] The wide branch helpers (`bw`, `bcw`, `blw`) implement the standard Thumb-2 S/J1/J2
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] bit-twiddling. For `bw` you chose a base of `0xF0009000` (hw2 = `1001…`), which sets bit 12;
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] that differs from the conditional branch base (`0xF0008000`). If that’s intentional to match the
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] ARMv7-M T4 unconditional-branch encoding, it’s fine—just worth double-checking against the ARM ARM
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] if you haven’t already run these on real silicon.
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] Overall it looks like a pragmatic, low-overhead assembler DSL layered over an `rmachine` /
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] `lmachine` compiler pipeline. Is this a fresh port from an earlier Thumb-1-only backend, or are you
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] expanding the wide encoding coverage further?
2026-04-21 07:02:54 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] A few observations on the Thumb-1 / Thumb-2 assembler helpers:
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] • `NONE` is an instance member*
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] `const int NONE = 255;` inside the struct allocates storage in every `thumb2` object. It should be
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] `static constexpr int NONE = 255;` so it compiles as a true compile-time constant without
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] per-object overhead.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] • Unmasked immediates*
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] Several narrow encoders accept `int` immediates but do not mask them, so out-of-range values
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] silently corrupt neighbouring bit fields:
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] - `movn`, `cmpn`, `addn`, `subn` (t3) should probably force `imm8 & 0xff`.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] - `addn` / `subn` (t2) should force `imm3 & 0x7`.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] - `ldr_n(reg, regpc, int)` should force `imm8 & 0xff`.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] - `pushn`, `popn`, `stmian` and `ldmian` should mask `rlist` to `0x1ff` (or `0xff`)
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] so high bits do not overwrite the opcode prefix.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] • SP add/sub immediate scaling*
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] `add_n(regsp d, int imm7)` encodes the raw 7-bit field directly. The Thumb-1 `ADD SP, #imm`
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] instruction scales that field by 4, so the helper either expects the caller to pre-divide or the
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] name `imm7` is misleading. Documenting the contract (raw field vs. byte offset) would avoid bugs.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] • `b_n` naming*
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] `b_n(int off12)` masks with `0x7ff` after shifting right by 1. The T2 unconditional branch encodes
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] an 11-bit word offset, so `off12` is a confusing name.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] • Wide opcode storage order*
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] The wide helpers return `u32` values such as `0xf0009000 | …`, which places the prefix halfword
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] (`0xf000`) in the upper 16 bits and the suffix halfword (`0x9000`) in the lower 16 bits. If you
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] emit these by storing the full 32-bit word to little-endian memory, the fetch order will be
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] reversed (suffix first). As long as your emitter splits the `u32` with the upper halfword first,
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] this is fine, but it is worth a comment because the `u32` literal itself looks big-endian in terms
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] of instruction stream order.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] • Branch encoding formulas*
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] The `j1` / `j2` calculations in `bw`, `bcw` and `bl_w` rely on `~` having higher precedence
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] than `&`, which they do, so `(~(i1^s)&1)` behaves correctly here. It is slightly opaque though;
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] `((i1 ^ s) ^ 1)` or simply `!(i1 ^ s)` makes the intent clearer.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] • Correctness of constants*
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] The wide encoding bases (`0xf0009000` for B, `0xf0008000` for Bcc, `0xf000d000` for BL,
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] `0xf2400000` / `0xf2c00000` for MOVW/MOVT) match the ARMv7-M T32 encodings, assuming the caller
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] splits them into halfwords high-then-low.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] Overall this is a straightforward and usable set of raw encoders; the main risks are unmasked
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] immediate fields and the word-order assumption for 32-bit instructions.
2026-04-21 07:10:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] Thanks for sharing. A few observations on the Thumb-2 codegen header:
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] - The encoding helpers for narrow and wide instructions are clean, and the overloading by register
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] class is a nice way to enforce valid operand ranges at compile time.
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] - You already note the `and=>ands` rename in the header comment; aligning the C++ names with the
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] architectural mnemonics (e.g. `ands_n`) is worthwhile since `and` is an alternative token in C++.
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] - In the branch width helpers (`bw`, `bcw`, `bl_w`), the `~` operator has lower precedence
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] than `&`. The expression `(~(i1^s)&1)` happens to work because of the final mask, but `(~(i1 ^ s) &
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] 1)` or `(1 ^ (i1 ^ s))` would make the intent explicit and harder to break during edits.
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] - `movww` and `movtw` take `reg16`, which starts at R8. Those instructions are valid for
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] R0–R15, so you may want a unified register argument type there (or an overload for the low
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] registers).
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] - `disasm(u16 code)` accepts a single halfword. Because Thumb-2 is a variable-length ISA,
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] disassembling wide instructions will need a second halfword or a buffer pointer/length.
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] - `const int NONE = 255;` inside the struct is fine, but `static constexpr int NONE = 255;` avoids
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] per-instance storage and is more idiomatic in modern C++.
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] Overall it looks like a solid, lightweight JIT backend. Let me know if you want a second pair of
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] eyes on the implementation side (`compile`, `linkjumps`, etc.).
2026-04-21 07:14:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:23:58 [nitro@ryzen5] can enum reg16 somehow extend r8?
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] No, a C++ enum cannot inherit from or extend another enum. Unscoped enum constants are simply
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] integer constants, so you can at least document the continuation:
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] enum reg  { R0, R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6, R7 };
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] enum reg16{ R8 = R7 + 1, R9, R10, R11, R12, R13, R14, R15 };
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] But `reg` and `reg16` remain unrelated types, and `R0` through `R7` are not members of `reg16`.
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] If you want a single register namespace with type-safe overloading for "low" vs "high" operands,
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] the usual codegen idiom is to keep one flat `enum` and use tag wrappers for overload resolution:
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] enum reg { R0, R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6, R7, R8, R9, R10, R11, R12, R13, R14, R15 };
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] struct low_reg { reg r; explicit low_reg(reg r_) : r(r_) {} };
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] struct any_reg { reg r; explicit any_reg(reg r_) : r(r_) {} };
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] // any_reg accepts R0–R15; low_reg accepts R0–R7 and asserts
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] This avoids duplicating R8–R15 in a second type and matches the architecture better: many Thumb-2
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] wide instructions (e.g. `MOVW`) accept the full range R0–R15, so restricting them to your current
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] `reg16` is unnecessary.
2026-04-21 07:24:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:49 [nitro@ryzen5] so c++ wont enforce enum parameter values?
2026-04-21 07:25:55 [kimi-k2.6] No. C++ does not enforce that an enum parameter holds a valid enumerator value.
2026-04-21 07:25:55 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:55 [kimi-k2.6] • Unscoped enums* (what you are using) implicitly convert to and from `int`, so any integer
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] value can be passed without complaint:
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] enum reg { R0, R1, R2 };
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] void f(reg r) {}
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] f(static_cast<reg>(99));   // compiles fine
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] f(reg(200));               // also fine
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] • Scoped enums (`enum class`) stop the implicit* conversion—you must cast
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] explicitly—but the compiler still does not validate that the resulting value is a declared
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] enumerator:
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] enum class reg { R0, R1, R2 };
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] void f(reg r) {}
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] f(static_cast<reg>(99));   // compiles fine; undefined/invalid value at runtime
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] If you want actual enforcement, you need a wrapper class with a validating constructor or an
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] assertion, not a bare enum.
2026-04-21 07:25:56 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:28:52 [nitro@ryzen5] can i mix narrow and wide in my output?
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] Yes. Thumb-2 is specifically designed as a variable-length ISA: 16-bit narrow instructions and
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] 32-bit wide instructions can be interleaved freely. The processor distinguishes them by the prefix
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] of the first halfword (`111xx` in bits [15:11] for 32-bit).
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] For your generator, this means your output buffer should be a `u16` array, not a `u32`
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] array:
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] u16 *out;      // emission pointer or array index
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] int pc = 0;    // counts halfwords, not instructions
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] - Narrow: emit one halfword and advance by 1.
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] - Wide: emit the two halfwords sequentially and advance by 2.
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] Because your wide helpers return a `u32` with the first halfword in the upper 16 bits and the
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] second in the lower 16 bits, you can emit them consistently as:
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] void emit_narrow(u16 op) {
2026-04-21 07:29:38 [kimi-k2.6] 	out[pc++] = op;
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] void emit_wide(u32 op) {
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] 	out[pc++] = static_cast<u16>(op >> 16);   // first halfword (prefix)
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] 	out[pc++] = static_cast<u16>(op);          // second halfword
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] Key consequences of mixing:
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] - PC-relative offsets (branches, literal loads, ADR/ADD Rd,PC) are measured in bytes from
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] the address of the instruction. Because narrow and wide instructions are different lengths, you
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] must compute offsets based on the actual halfword count between the source and target, not a simple
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] instruction index difference.
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] - Alignment: 32-bit instructions only need halfword alignment in Thumb-2, so no padding is
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] required before emitting a wide instruction.
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] - Branch targets: Any halfword-aligned address can be a target, regardless of whether the
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] instruction at that address is narrow or wide.
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] If you are currently using `int ` to represent the code buffer, you may want to change the
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] internal representation to `u16 ` (or `std::vector<u16>`) and only cast to an executable
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] pointer after generation.
2026-04-21 07:29:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:31:27 [nitro@ryzen5] cool, it now makes sense why they called it thumb-2 and went with the oddball encoding in places
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] Exactly. You have hit on the core design trick that makes Thumb-2 work.
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] Original Thumb was purely 16-bit. It was dense, but crippled: no access to high registers for most
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] operations, no conditional execution except branches, and tiny immediate ranges. ARM wanted to fix
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] that without abandoning the density or forcing a full 32-bit mode switch.
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] Their solution was to treat certain 16-bit patterns in the original Thumb map as escape
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] sequences. Specifically, if the first halfword has bits `[15:11]` equal to `0b11101`,
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] `0b11110`, or `0b11111`, the decoder knows this halfword and the next one form a single 32-bit
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] instruction. Anything else is a standalone 16-bit instruction.
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] This is why the encoding looks so odd and "bit-scattered": the architects had to compress a 32-bit
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] instruction into two consecutive halfwords where the first one is locked into a very specific
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] prefix mask. All the architectural fields—condition codes, immediates, register specifiers—get
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] ripped apart and shoved into whatever gaps remain across those two halfwords. Your `bw` and
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] `bcw` helpers, with their `j1`/`j2` bits and scattered immediates, are directly reconstructing
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] that escape encoding.
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] The genius is that this gives the decoder length determination at a glance. The CPU can
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] fetch a halfword, look at five bits, and immediately know whether to fetch one more halfword. It
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] also means the instruction stream is randomly accessible at any halfword boundary: you do not need
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] to decode from the start of a basic block to know where an instruction boundary lies. That property
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] is essential for branch targets and interrupt entry.
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] So the "oddball" feel is not accidental. It is the direct consequence of ARM bolting a 32-bit
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] instruction set onto a 16-bit encoding while maintaining backward compatibility and fast sequential
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] decode. Thumb-2 is not really "16-bit with some 32-bit extensions" or "32-bit cut in half"; it is a
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] genuinely variable-length architecture wearing a 16-bit bus mask.
2026-04-21 07:31:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:32:34 [nitro@ryzen5] slightly risc
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] Aye, it is a bit of a RISC to take.
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] What is ironic is that ARM built its reputation on being the clean RISC: fixed 32-bit
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] instructions, regular encoding, three-operand datapath, and a pipeline you could reason about in
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] your sleep. Thumb-2 is where ARM got positively CISC-y. Variable length, complex prefix decode,
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] split immediates stitched together from halfword fragments, and an instruction length you cannot
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] determine without inspecting the bit pattern. Very un-RISC.
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] The "risk" paid off, though. They got ARMv7 code density within spitting distance of x86 and 68k,
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] without sacrificing the register model or dropping to a pure 16-bit ISA like original Thumb. It is
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] RISC philosophy with CISC packaging tape.
2026-04-21 07:32:39 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:47:51 [nitro@ryzen5] with my generators is there a way to make the block all constexpr?
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] You can make the encoding helpers `constexpr` very easily, since they are pure functions:
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] static constexpr u16 lsl_n(reg d, reg s, int imm5) {
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 	return 0x0000 | ((imm5 & 0x1f) << 6) | (s << 3) | d;
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] static constexpr u32 b_w(int off25) {
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 s  = (off25 >> 24) & 1;
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 i1 = (off25 >> 23) & 1;
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 i2 = (off25 >> 22) & 1;
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 j1 = (~(i1 ^ s) & 1) << 13;
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 j2 = (~(i2 ^ s) & 1) << 11;
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 imm10 = (off25 >> 12) & 0x3ff;
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 imm11 = (off25 >> 1) & 0x7ff;
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 	return 0xf0009000 | (s << 26) | (imm10 << 16) | j1 | j2 | imm11;
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] This is useful for unit tests and building static opcode tables at compile time.
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] However, your entire `compile()` pipeline cannot be `constexpr` as it stands, for several
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] hard reasons:
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] - Virtual base. `thumb2` inherits from `rmachine`. Virtual functions cannot be used in
2026-04-21 07:48:32 [kimi-k2.6] constant expressions until C++20, and even then the object must be constexpr-friendly.
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] - Runtime pointers. `addnative(void *f, ...)` takes a runtime function address. A pointer
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] value to a native function is never a constant expression.
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] - Dynamic allocation. `compile()` returns `int*`, implying heap or mutable runtime storage.
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] `constexpr` `new` only works in C++20 and only for transient allocation within the constant
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] evaluation; you cannot leak a pointer to runtime.
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] - Two-pass linking. `linkjumps()` patches a buffer after the fact. Doing this at compile
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] time requires the buffer to live as a `constexpr` array, and the whole pipeline must be side-effect
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] free.
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] If you genuinely want a compile-time-generated code block, you would need to redesign
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] around C++20/23 `constexpr`:
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 1. Drop the virtual base; use a plain struct or CRTP.
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 2. Emit into a `std::array<u16, N>` rather than `int*`.
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 3. Replace runtime native function addresses with `constexpr` indices or enum tags; resolve them
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] later in a runtime trampoline.
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 4. Perform the compile and link passes as `constexpr` array manipulation with no I/O (`dump`,
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] `disasm`, and `printf` are forbidden in constexpr).
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 5. Materialize the final array as a `constinit` blob in `.rodata`, then copy it to executable RAM
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] once at startup.
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] For a live JIT targeting RP2350, that is usually overkill. The practical win is making the emitters
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] `static constexpr` so you can write compile-time assertions and static tests:
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] static_assert(thumb2::mov_n(R0, 42) == 0x202a);
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] That catches encoding bugs at build time rather than at test time.
2026-04-21 07:48:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:14 [nitro@ryzen5] is there any convention for using a short version of static constexpr
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] No. In standard C++ there is no keyword or abbreviation that means exactly `static constexpr`.
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] If the verbosity bothers you, the idiomatic workaround is to move the pure emitters out of the
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] class into a namespace, where plain `constexpr` suffices:
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] namespace thumb2_enc {
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 	constexpr u16 lsl_n(reg d, reg s, int imm5) {
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 		return 0x0000 | ((imm5 & 0x1f) << 6) | (s << 3) | d;
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 	}
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 	// ...
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] struct thumb2 : rmachine {
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 	// runtime state and compile pipeline here
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] };
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] Inside a class, a `constexpr` member function is still non-static unless you write `static`. It
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] carries an implicit `this`, so you cannot call `thumb2::lsl_n(...)` without an object unless you
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] keep the `static`.
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] Some codebases define a macro:
2026-04-21 07:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] #define SC static constexpr
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] but that is non-standard, opaque to tools, and generally discouraged in modern C++.
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] For data members, `static constexpr` is as short as it gets. (Since C++17 it is also implicitly
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] `inline`, so you do not need an out-of-class definition, but you still must write both keywords.)
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] If you want the strictest compile-time guarantee and are on C++20, you can write `consteval`
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] instead of `constexpr`, but that is longer, not shorter.
2026-04-21 07:51:47 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:02 [nitro@ryzen5] namespace seems like a good decision, maybe this is an optimise too early thing
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] It is not premature optimization. It is separation of concerns, and your instincts are right.
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] Your `thumb2` class currently holds two very different things: the stateful JIT engine
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] (`hicode`, compile buffers, linkage tables, virtual dispatch via `rmachine`) and stateless
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] opcode factories (bit-twiddling helpers that touch no member data). Those do not belong in the
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] same type.
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] Moving the emitters to a namespace or a plain `struct` of static functions untangles them from the
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] object lifecycle. That is useful immediately, not later:
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] - Your unit tests can validate encodings without constructing a `thumb2` instance.
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] - A future disassembler or assembler can include the same logic without dragging in the JIT.
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] - You stop fighting `static constexpr` noise inside a class body.
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] On the RP2350 you are already memory-constrained and generating code to RAM. Keeping the encoding
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] math available at compile time is actually a practical win today, because you can build static
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] literal pools or test vectors without runtime cost.
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] If you want to keep the convenience of `thumb2::add_n(...)` at the call site, a namespace is the
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] simplest change:
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] namespace thumb2_enc {
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 	constexpr u16 add_n(reg d, reg s0, reg s1) { ... }
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] struct thumb2 : rmachine {
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 	// JIT state only
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] };
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] You are not over-engineering; you are noticing that the abstraction boundaries were slightly wrong
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] and fixing them before the file grows larger. That is exactly the right time to do it.
2026-04-21 07:53:18 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:41 [nitro@ryzen5] is #pragma once a standard now?
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] No. It is not in the ISO C++ standard, and there is no guarantee a strictly conforming compiler
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] must support it.
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] That said, every major compiler you are likely to use—GCC, Clang, MSVC, ARM Compiler, and IAR—does
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] support it. In practice it is portable unless you are targeting an unusual or strictly
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] standards-compliant toolchain.
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] The standard committee has discussed standardizing it (P2465), but as of C++23 it has not been
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] adopted.
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] Your header already uses the classic include guard:
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] #ifndef thumb2_h
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] #define thumb2_h
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] // ...
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] #endif
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] That remains the fully portable approach. If you prefer `#pragma once` for brevity, the pragmatic
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] choice is to use both:
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] #pragma once
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] #ifndef thumb2_h
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] #define thumb2_h
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] // ...
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] #endif
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] This gives you the convenience on common compilers and a fallback on any compiler that ignores the
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] pragma. On the RP2350 you are almost certainly using GCC or LLVM, so either works. Keep the guard
2026-04-21 07:54:52 [kimi-k2.6] if you want to be strictly correct; drop it if you do not mind relying on a de-facto standard.
2026-04-21 07:54:53 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:55:46 [nitro@ryzen5] what is arms code names for the thumb-2 isa?
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] ARM does not use an internal code name for the Thumb-2 ISA itself; it was simply called
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] Thumb-2 (or T2 in internal shorthand) during and after development.
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] What you are probably seeing in documentation are the architecture version monikers rather than
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] project code names:
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] - ARMv6T2 — the architecture where Thumb-2 debuted (the "T2" literally means Thumb-2)
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] - ARMv7 — the first architecture where Thumb-2 was mandatory in all profiles
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] - ARMv7-M — the microcontroller profile that is Thumb-2 only (no 32-bit ARM mode at
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] all)
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] - ARMv8-M — what your RP2350 Cortex-M33 actually implements; it is still a Thumb-2-based
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] ISA with TrustZone and the new M-Profile Vector Extension (MVE, or Helium)
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] Within ARM, 32-bit instructions are sometimes referred to as "wide" instructions and 16-bit
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] ones as "narrow" instructions, which is exactly the terminology used in the ARM
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] Architecture Reference Manual. But that is architectural nomenclature, not a code name.
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] If you are looking for the traditional ARM core code names (the ones used for processor
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] implementations before marketing names), those belong to specific microarchitectures, not
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] the ISA:
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] - Cortex-M3 was derived from the Swift family research
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] - Cortex-M4 was sometimes associated with Eagle internally
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] - Cortex-M7 had various internal project names
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] But Thumb-2 itself was always just Thumb-2. There is no exotic internal name like "Jazelle" or
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] "Helium" for the base ISA.
2026-04-21 07:56:14 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:57:12 [nitro@ryzen5] could i declare the thumb2 rmachine inside a T2 namespace ?
2026-04-21 07:57:31 [nitro@ryzen5] and move the constexpr set out of the class?
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] Yes, that is a clean refactor. Move the enums and pure encoding helpers to namespace scope, and
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] leave only the JIT state and runtime interface inside the class.
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] namespace T2 {
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] using u16 = uint16_t;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] using u32 = uint32_t;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] enum reg   { R0, R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6, R7 };
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] enum reg16 { R8 = 8, R9, R10, R11, R12, R13, R14, R15 };
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] enum regsp { SP = 13 };
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] enum regpc { PC = 15 };
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] enum cc    { EQ, NE, CS, CC, MI, PL, VS, VC, HI, LS, GE, LT, GT, LE, AL, NV };
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] constexpr int NONE = 255;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] constexpr u16 lsl_n(reg d, reg s, int imm5) {
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 	return 0x0000 | ((imm5 & 0x1f) << 6) | (s << 3) | d;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] // ... remaining constexpr emitters ...
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] constexpr u32 b_w(int off25) {
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 s  = (off25 >> 24) & 1;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 i1 = (off25 >> 23) & 1;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 i2 = (off25 >> 22) & 1;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 j1 = (~(i1 ^ s) & 1) << 13;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 j2 = (~(i2 ^ s) & 1) << 11;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 imm10 = (off25 >> 12) & 0x3ff;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 	u32 imm11 = (off25 >> 1) & 0x7ff;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 	return 0xf0009000 | (s << 26) | (imm10 << 16) | j1 | j2 | imm11;
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] // ... etc ...
2026-04-21 07:58:19 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] struct thumb2 : ::rmachine {
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 	int hicode;
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 	thumb2();
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 	int *compile();
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 	rnative *addnative(void *f, const char *n, int s);
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 	void dump(int *code);
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 	void addvregs();
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 	void linkjumps();
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 	void disasm(u16 code);
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 	int mapreg(int r);
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 	int compile(u16 *code);
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] };
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] } // namespace T2
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] A few notes:
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] - Because the enums live in the same namespace as the emitters, overload resolution between `reg`
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] and `reg16` works exactly as it did inside the class.
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] - Qualify the base class as `::rmachine` if it lives in the global namespace; otherwise use its
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] actual namespace.
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] - Do the same for any external types such as `rnative` if they are not inside `namespace T2`.
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] External call sites would then use `T2::R0`, `T2::b_w(...)`, and instantiate `T2::thumb2`. If you
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] want the old unqualified names inside a specific `.cpp` file, a few targeted `using` declarations
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] are cleaner than a blanket `using namespace T2`.
2026-04-21 07:58:20 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 07:59:53 [nitro@ryzen5] thanks that is helpful
2026-04-21 08:00:16 [nitro@ryzen5] for the u16 u32 that is global style i would like to conform to across project
2026-04-21 08:00:25 [nitro@ryzen5] what conventions could i consider?
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] If you want consistency across the project, there are a few conventions that fit low-level and VM
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] code well:
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] • Project-namespace aliases.*
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] Put `using u16 = std::uint16_t;` inside your project namespace (for example, `namespace acidvm`).
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] This keeps the global namespace clean while every module still sees the same short names. It also
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] avoids collisions if you ever link a library that defined its own `u32` globally.
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] • A single global `types.h`.*
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] This is extremely common in embedded, kernel, and compiler code. It is pragmatic and immediately
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] familiar to anyone reading ARM-targeted source. The trade-off is namespace pollution, though in a
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] self-contained project that rarely causes real problems.
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] • Use `std::uint16_t` directly and skip aliases entirely.*
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] This is the most portable and explicit approach. The downside is verbosity in dense encoding tables
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] where `u16` reads more naturally than `std::uint16_t`.
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] • Hybrid exact-width / abstract.*
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] Reserve `u16` and `u32` strictly for hardware-facing code: opcodes, register fields, bit
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] extraction, and memory-mapped structures. Use plain `int` or `unsigned` for loop counters, sizes,
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] and general arithmetic. This matches the C++ Core Guidelines advice to avoid unsigned for
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] arithmetic, while keeping bit-twiddling code explicit.
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] A few implementation notes:
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] - Prefer `using` over `typedef` if you are on C++11 or later; it reads left-to-right and behaves
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] better with templates if you ever need them.
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] - Include `<cstdint>` and qualify `std::uint16t` in the alias definition. Relying on a global
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] `uint16t` from `<cstdint>` is not strictly guaranteed by the C++ standard, even though every
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] implementation provides it.
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] - If you namespace the aliases, you can still get brevity inside a `.cpp` with a targeted `using
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] acidvm::u16;` at function scope rather than a blanket `using namespace`.
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] Given that this is an instruction encoder where the types map literally to halfwords and words,
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] exact-width aliases are a strong fit. Whether they live in a namespace or a global header is mostly
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] a question of how many external dependencies you expect. For a standalone VM, a global `types.h` is
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] perfectly reasonable and often clearer than repeating `std::` in every emitter signature.
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] Which style dominates the rest of your codebase?
2026-04-21 08:01:02 [kimi-k2.6] 
