2026-04-23 07:30:47 [nitro@ryzen5] does one have faster rise time?
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] Excellent question! Rise time is critical for video signals. Let me analyze the frequency response
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] of both options.
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] Circuit Analysis for Rise Time
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] The rise time is determined by the RC time constant formed by the output impedance and the TV's
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] input capacitance.
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] Circuit Model:
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] Source ──── R_out ────┬─── VIDEO OUT ──── 75Ω ──── GND
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat]                       │
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat]                      C_tv (~100pF typical for TV input)
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] Where R_out is the Thévenin equivalent output impedance looking back from the VIDEO OUT node.
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] Option 1: 470Ω/150Ω
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 3.3V ──── 470Ω ────┬─── VIDEO OUT
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat]                    │
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] GP28 ──── 150Ω ────┘
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] • Output impedance calculation:*
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] When GPIO switches, we have two parallel paths:
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 1. From 3.3V through 470Ω
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 2. From GPIO through 150Ω
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] The Thévenin impedance looking back from VIDEO OUT:
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] - With both sources active (worst case): R_out = 470Ω || 150Ω = 113.7Ω
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] - Plus TV's 75Ω in parallel: R_total = 113.7Ω || 75Ω = 45.2Ω
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] • Time constant:*
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] τ = Rtotal × Ctv = 45.2Ω × 100pF = 4.52ns
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] • 10-90% Rise time:*
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] t_rise ≈ 2.2 × τ = 2.2 × 4.52ns = 9.94ns
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] Option 2: 390Ω/180Ω
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 3.3V ──── 390Ω ────┬─── VIDEO OUT
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat]                    │
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] GP28 ──── 180Ω ────┘
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] • Output impedance:*
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] - R_out = 390Ω || 180Ω = 123.2Ω
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] - R_total = 123.2Ω || 75Ω = 46.4Ω
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] • Time constant:*
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] τ = 46.4Ω × 100pF = 4.64ns
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] • Rise time:*
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] t_rise ≈ 2.2 × 4.64ns = 10.21ns
2026-04-23 07:31:18 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] Comparison
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] ╭──────────────────────────┬──────────────┬─────────────╮
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] │  Parameter               │  470Ω/150Ω   │  390Ω/180Ω  │
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] ├──────────────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────┤
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] │  Output Impedance        │  45.2Ω       │  46.4Ω      │
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] │  Rise Time (100pF load)  │  **9.94ns**  │  10.21ns    │
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] │  Black Level             │  0.32V       │  0.31V      │
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] │  White Level             │  0.78V       │  0.76V      │
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] │  Current from 3.3V       │  6.4mA       │  7.2mA      │
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] ╰──────────────────────────┴──────────────┴─────────────╯
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] Winner: 470Ω/150Ω
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] The 470Ω/150Ω combination has slightly faster rise time (9.94ns vs 10.21ns) because:
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 1. Lower output impedance (45.2Ω vs 46.4Ω)
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 2. The 150Ω GPIO resistor provides a stronger drive than 180Ω
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] Video Bandwidth Considerations
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] For standard composite video:
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] - NTSC: 4.2MHz bandwidth → requires ~83ns rise time (10-90%)
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] - PAL: 5.0MHz bandwidth → requires ~70ns rise time
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] • Both options are excellent* - they provide ~10ns rise time, which is 8× faster than
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] needed for NTSC and 7× faster than needed for PAL.
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] Recommendation
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] Use 470Ω/150Ω because:
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 1. Fastest rise time (9.94ns)
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 2. Voltage levels are perfect for your requirements (0.32V black, 0.78V white)
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 3. Lower power consumption (6.4mA vs 7.2mA)
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] The circuit will produce sharp, clean video edges with no visible smearing.
2026-04-23 07:31:19 [deepseek-chat] 
