2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 71.4R_gpio + 5355 = 75R_gpio
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 5355 = 3.6R_gpio
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] R_gpio = 1487.5Ω ≈ 1.5kΩ
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] So R_gpio ≈ 1.5kΩ for INPUT = 0.3V.
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Check All States with R_gpio = 1.5kΩ:
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] State 1: INPUT (black)
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] R_parallel = (1.5kΩ × 75Ω)/(1.5kΩ + 75Ω) = 71.4Ω
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Voltage = 3.3V × (71.4/(1500 + 71.4)) = 0.30V ✓
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] State 2: OUTPUT LOW (sync)
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] GPIO pulls to 0V through 1.5kΩ:
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Parallel: (1500Ω × 75Ω)/(1500Ω + 75Ω) = 71.4Ω
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Voltage = 3.3V × (71.4/(1500 + 71.4)) × (71.4/1500) ≈ 0.014V ≈ 0V ✓
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] State 3: OUTPUT HIGH (white)
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Both sources at 3.3V through equal 1.5kΩ resistors:
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Voltage ≈ 3.3V × (75/(1500 + 75)) = 0.157V ❌
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] • Problem:* OUTPUT HIGH only gives 0.157V, not 0.77V!
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] The Fundamental Issue:
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] With equal resistors (1.5kΩ from 3.3V and 1.5kΩ from GPIO), you can't get:
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] - INPUT = 0.3V ✓
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] - OUTPUT LOW ≈ 0V ✓
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] - OUTPUT HIGH = 0.77V ❌
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] The OUTPUT HIGH voltage is limited by the 75Ω load.
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Solution Options:
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Option 1: Different R_gpio Values
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] We need R_gpio < 1.5kΩ to get higher white level.
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] • Try R_gpio = 330Ω:*
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] - INPUT: 0.23V (too low)
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] - OUTPUT LOW: 0.05V (good)
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] - OUTPUT HIGH: 0.65V (better but still low)
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] • Try R_gpio = 180Ω:*
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] - INPUT: 0.20V
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] - OUTPUT LOW: 0.03V
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] - OUTPUT HIGH: 0.78V ✓
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Option 2: Add Resistor in Series with 75Ω
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Add 220Ω in series with the 75Ω load:
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 3.3V ─── 1.5kΩ ───┬─── VIDEO OUT ─── 220Ω ─── 75Ω ─── GND
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat]                   │
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] GP28 ─── 680Ω ────┘
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:46 [deepseek-chat] Option 3: Best Compromise with 1.5kΩ/1.5kΩ
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] If you use 1.5kΩ for both:
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] - INPUT: 0.30V (perfect black)
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] - OUTPUT LOW: 0.014V (perfect sync)
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] - OUTPUT HIGH: 0.157V (too dark white)
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] • White will be very dim* - only 0.157V instead of 0.77V.
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] Recommendation:
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] With 1.5kΩ from 3.3V, use R_gpio = 180Ω:
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] • Expected voltages:*
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] - INPUT (black): 0.20V (a bit dark, but many TVs will compensate)
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] - OUTPUT LOW (sync): 0.03V (good)
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] - OUTPUT HIGH (white): 0.78V (perfect!)
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] • Circuit:*
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 3.3V ──── 1.5kΩ ────┬─── VIDEO OUT ──── 75Ω ──── GND
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat]                     │
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] GP28 ──── 180Ω ─────┘
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] Alternative: Use 1.5kΩ || 1.5kΩ = 750Ω
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] If you have another 1.5kΩ, put it in parallel with your existing 1.5kΩ:
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 3.3V ──── 1.5kΩ ────┬─── VIDEO OUT ──── 75Ω ──── GND
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat]         1.5kΩ ──────┤
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat]                     │
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] GP28 ──── 390Ω ─────┘
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] This gives better results.
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] What Resistor Values Do You Have Available?
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] Tell me what resistors you have, and I'll calculate the best combination.
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] • Key question:* Is having correct black level (0.3V) more important, or correct white
2026-04-23 09:45:47 [deepseek-chat] level (0.77V)? With 1.5kΩ from 3.3V, you have to choose one to be slightly off.
