2026-04-23 10:45:16 [nitro@ryzen5] how can black level be ok if it is way less than 0.3V???
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] You're absolutely right to question this! 0.155V is NOT OK for proper black level in
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] composite video. It's way too low - about half of what it should be.
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] Composite Video Standards:
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] - Sync tip: 0V (your 0.115V is acceptable)
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] - Black level: 0.3V (your 0.155V is 45% too low!)
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] - White peak: 1.0V above black = 1.3V total (your 0.921V is actually OK relative to your
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] black)
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] What Happens with 0.155V Black:
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 1. Picture will be VERY DARK
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 2. Blacks will crush - dark details lost
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 3. TV might auto-brightness but poorly
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 4. Colors will be desaturated
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] Why Your Circuit Gives 0.155V:
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] With 1.45kΩ from 3.3V and TV connected:
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] - The TV's input isn't a simple 75Ω resistor
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] - It has DC blocking capacitors
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] - When GPIO is INPUT, current flows through 1.45kΩ into TV's input stage
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] - TV's input impedance at DC might be much higher than 75Ω
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] The Fix:
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] You need smaller resistance from 3.3V to get 0.3V in INPUT mode.
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] Calculation for 0.3V:
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 0.3V = 3.3V × (R_tv / (R_3v3 + R_tv))
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] Where R_tv = TV's DC input impedance
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] If we assume TV's DC impedance is ~1kΩ (typical with coupling caps):
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 0.3 = 3.3 × (1000/(R_3v3 + 1000))
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] R_3v3 = (3.3 × 1000 / 0.3) - 1000 = 10,000Ω - 1000 = 9kΩ
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] Wait, that gives larger resistor, not smaller! Let me think...
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] Actually, when TV has coupling capacitors, the DC path is through bias resistors inside the TV,
2026-04-23 10:45:48 [deepseek-chat] which are typically high impedance (~10kΩ-100kΩ).
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] So Your 0.155V Actually Suggests:
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] With 1.45kΩ and 0.155V:
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 0.155 = 3.3 × (R_tv/(1450 + R_tv))
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] R_tv ≈ 86Ω
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] The TV appears as ~86Ω at DC - which means it might NOT have AC coupling or has a
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] different input circuit.
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] Practical Solution:
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] • Reduce your 1.45kΩ resistor significantly:*
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] Try 470Ω:
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 3.3V ─── 470Ω ───┬─── VIDEO OUT ──── TV
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat]                  │
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] GPIO ── 178Ω ────┘
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] Expected (if TV = 75Ω at DC):
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] - INPUT: 0.42V (too high)
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] - But TV might not be 75Ω at DC...
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] Better: Empirical Adjustment:
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] • Start with 1kΩ and adjust:*
2026-04-23 10:45:49 [deepseek-chat] 
