2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] Thank you for sharing your code and the details of your project. Using the RP2040's PIO and DMA to
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] generate a 13.875 MHz WST Teletext signal is a fantastic and highly efficient approach!
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] Based on the schematic in your comments, you are using a single tristate GPIO pin alongside a fixed
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] pull-up resistor (R1) to generate your video levels across a standard 75Ω television termination.
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the calculations for your resistor values, along with an important observation about how
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] composite video levels work with this specific circuit topology.
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] Option 1: Targeting your exact voltages (0.3V and 0.77V)
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] To hit exactly 0.3V for black and 0.77V for your 66% white level using a 3.3V logic supply, the
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] closest standard E12 resistor values are:
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] • R1 = 750 Ω
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] • R2 = 360 Ω
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How this behaves:*
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  Black Level (GPIO Input / High-Z): R2 is disconnected. R1 (750Ω) and the TV (75Ω) form
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] a voltage divider. 3.3V × (75 / 825) = 0.30V.
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  White Level (GPIO Output High): R1 and R2 act in parallel to pull up to 3.3V. 3.3V ×
2026-04-23 10:58:58 [gemini-3.1-pro] (75 / 318) = 0.77V.
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • The Limitation (The 0V Blank/Sync Level):*
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because R1 is permanently pulling the video line up to 3.3V, driving the GPIO low (0V) will
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] not pull the video line all the way down to 0V. It creates a divider between R1 (3.3V) and
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] R2 parallel to the 75Ω load (0V).
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sync Level (GPIO Output Low): 3.3V × (62 / 812) = 0.25V.
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Standard composite video relies on a 300mV difference between the sync tip (usually 0V) and the
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] black level (0.3V). With this setup, your sync tip is 0.25V, leaving only a 50mV difference. Many
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] televisions and capture cards will fail to detect this tiny sync pulse.
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Option 2: The AC-Coupled Solution (Highly Recommended)
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Fortunately, televisions almost universally use AC-coupled inputs. They do not care about the
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] absolute DC voltage (like starting exactly at 0V); they only care about the relative
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] voltage steps between your sync, black, and white levels.
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] We can adjust R1 and R2 to provide the exact standard voltage swings (a 300mV step for sync, and a
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] ~470mV step for teletext data), even if the whole signal is riding on a higher DC offset. The TV's
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] internal clamping circuit will automatically adjust it back to 0V.
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Using standard E12 values, I highly recommend:
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • R1 = 120 Ω
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • R2 = 150 Ω
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How this behaves:*
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  Sync Level (GPIO Output Low): Yields ~0.97V.
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  Black Level (GPIO Input / High-Z): Yields ~1.27V.
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro]        (Difference from Sync: 300mV — Perfect standard sync depth!)*
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3.  White Level (GPIO Output High): Yields ~1.75V.
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro]        (Difference from Black: 480mV — Perfect for your 66% teletext payload!)*
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Summary for your software
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you choose the recommended Option 2 (120Ω / 150Ω), your software logic remains exactly
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] the same as you have it written, but it will be much more reliable on actual hardware:
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sync (Lowest level): GPIO Out, Low
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Black (Middle level): GPIO In (High-Z)
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Teletext Data (Highest level): GPIO Out, High
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] Please let me know if you would like me to calculate values for a different logic voltage, or if
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] you'd like to explore a 2-pin DAC topology which is sometimes used to achieve an absolute 0V DC
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] reference!
2026-04-23 10:58:59 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:00:44 [nitro@ryzen5] if R2 is 180 what is suitable R1?
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you fix R2 at 180 Ω, it introduces an interesting engineering trade-off in this specific
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1-pin circuit topology.
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a helpful rule for this circuit: R1 completely dictates the ratio between your
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Teletext data swing and your Sync swing, while R2 dictates the overall strength of the
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] signal.
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Specifically, the ratio of your White Swing to your Sync Swing will always be exactly R1 /
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 75.
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because R2 = 180 Ω is slightly higher than the ideal 150 Ω, the overall signal will be slightly
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] "smaller" (attenuated). You have to choose whether you want to sacrifice a little bit of sync
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] depth, or a little bit of teletext brightness.
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are the most suitable values for R1 depending on what you want to prioritize:
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Option 1: R1 = 120 Ω (Best Overall Balance)
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is the safest bet for maximum compatibility, keeping the proportions of the signal as close to
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] the PAL standard as possible.
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sync Swing: ~260 mV (Standard is 300mV, but 260mV is easily read by almost all
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] TVs)
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Teletext Swing: ~415 mV (Standard is ~470mV, so the data will be slightly dimmer, but
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] well within decoding range)
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Option 2: R1 = 130 Ω (Better Teletext Level)
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you find the teletext decoder is struggling to read the data with Option 1, raising R1 will
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] increase the data swing at the expense of the sync pulse.
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sync Swing: ~250 mV (Getting a bit weak, some picky USB capture cards might
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] complain)
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Teletext Swing: ~435 mV (Much closer to the ideal 66% white level)
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Option 3: R1 = 100 Ω (Strongest Sync)
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your display is losing tracking or the image is rolling, you need a stronger sync pulse.
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] Lowering R1 achieves this, but your teletext data will be dimmer.
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sync Swing: ~270 mV (Very solid sync)
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Teletext Swing: ~360 mV (Dimmer, roughly 50% white instead of 66%)
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Summary:*
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you have a 120 Ω or 130 Ω resistor on hand, that will be your best choice to
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] pair with your 180 Ω R2!
2026-04-23 11:01:38 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:03:57 [nitro@ryzen5] what voltages can i expect to read from meter with tv connected if R1 is 120 and R2 is 180
2026-04-23 11:04:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Assuming your RP2040 is running at a standard 3.3V logic level and the 75Ω TV load
2026-04-23 11:04:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] is plugged in, here are the exact DC voltages you will read on your multimeter at the video output
2026-04-23 11:04:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] pin for each state.
2026-04-23 11:04:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:04:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because your test code steps through the states slowly using `sleep_ms(1000)`, a standard
2026-04-23 11:04:14 [gemini-3.1-pro] multimeter will have plenty of time to give you accurate readings for these:
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Sync Level (GPIO Output Low / 0V)*
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Expected Reading: ~1.01V
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How it works: The 180Ω resistor and the TV's 75Ω load are in parallel to ground (acting
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] as ~53Ω). The 120Ω resistor pulls up to 3.3V, creating a voltage divider.
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Black Level (GPIO Input / High-Z)*
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Expected Reading: ~1.27V
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How it works: The GPIO pin is disconnected. The circuit is a simple voltage divider
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] between the 120Ω pull-up to 3.3V and the TV's 75Ω pull-down to ground.
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Note: 1.27V - 1.01V = 0.26V Sync step)
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 3. Teletext Data Level (GPIO Output High / 3.3V)*
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Expected Reading: ~1.68V
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How it works: Both the 120Ω and 180Ω resistors are pulling up to 3.3V (acting as a
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] parallel 72Ω pull-up). The TV's 75Ω load pulls to ground.
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Note: 1.68V - 1.27V = 0.41V Data step)
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] • A quick testing tip:*
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you unplug the video cable from the TV (removing the 75Ω load to ground), these voltages will
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] jump significantly (Sync will be ~1.98V, Black will be 3.3V, and White will be 3.3V). Always ensure
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] the TV or a 75Ω dummy load is connected when verifying your video DAC voltages with a meter!
2026-04-23 11:04:15 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:09:18 [nitro@ryzen5] i want sync below 0.3 v and black just above
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] To achieve those exact absolute voltages with your current 1-pin circuit, the closest standard
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] resistor values are:
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] • R1 = 750 Ω
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] • R2 = 360 Ω
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] With these values, you will get:
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Black Level (GPIO In): ~0.30 V
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sync Level (GPIO Out Low): ~0.25 V
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] • White Level (GPIO Out High): ~0.77 V
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Electrical Limitation
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] While this hits your target of keeping Sync below 0.3V and Black just above it, it introduces a
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] physical limitation of this specific 1-pin circuit design.
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] The difference between your Black level (0.30V) and your Sync level (0.25V) is only 0.05V
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] (50mV).
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] Standard PAL video requires a 300mV difference between Black and Sync. Because R1 is
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] permanently pulling the voltage up to 3.3V, driving the GPIO low cannot overcome R1 enough to pull
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] the line all the way down to 0V. Most televisions and capture cards will not recognize a 50mV drop
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] as a valid sync pulse, resulting in a rolling or dropped image.
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] With a 1-pin circuit, you are forced to choose between having absolute DC voltages (which ruins the
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] sync depth) or having the correct relative voltage steps (which shifts the whole signal to a higher
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] DC offset, as calculated in the previous 120Ω/150Ω recommendation).
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Solution: A 2-Pin DAC
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you want to achieve a true 0V Sync, a 0.3V Black, and a 0.77V White, the standard approach is to
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] use two GPIO pins instead of one, removing the permanent R1 pull-up entirely.
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] It works like this:
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] • GPIO A (Sync Pin) connected via 680 Ω to Video Out
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] • GPIO B (Data Pin) connected via 390 Ω to Video Out
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How the states work in software:*
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1.  Sync (0.00 V): GPIO A = Low, GPIO B = Low
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2.  Black (0.28 V): GPIO A = High, GPIO B = Low
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3.  White (0.76 V): GPIO A = High, GPIO B = High
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you would prefer to stick to your current 1-pin hardware setup, the AC-coupled approach
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] (R1=120Ω, R2=150Ω) is highly recommended for standard compatibility. If you are open to using two
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] GPIO pins, the 2-pin DAC will give you the exact absolute voltages you are looking for.
2026-04-23 11:10:00 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:17:15 [nitro@ryzen5] no 1 want to use 1 pin, any other ideas? does current or internal pull up settings allow better results?
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] To answer your direct questions first:
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. Can internal pull-ups or pull-downs help?*
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Unfortunately, no. The RP2040's internal pull-up and pull-down resistors are roughly 50kΩ. When
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] placed across a heavy 75Ω television load, they are far too weak to be useful. 3.3V through a 50kΩ
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] resistor into 75Ω will only shift the voltage by about 5 millivolts.
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. Can drive current settings help?*
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] Changing the pad drive strength (2mA, 4mA, 8mA, 12mA) changes the internal output impedance of the
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] GPIO pin (roughly ~100Ω at 2mA down to ~30Ω at 12mA). While setting it to 12mA provides a slightly
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] "harder" pull to ground or 3.3V, it does not change the fundamental Ohm's Law math of the external
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] resistor divider.
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:36 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Mathematical Roadblock of the 1-Pin Circuit
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] The reason you cannot get Sync < 0.3V, Black ~0.3V, and White ~0.77V with this specific 1-pin
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] passive circuit is due to an unavoidable physical limitation of the voltage divider.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the engineering puzzle:
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. To get Black = 0.3V, R1 must be 750Ω.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Because R1 is permanently tied to 3.3V, it is always pulling the video line up.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. Standard video requires a 300mV drop from Black to Sync. If Black is 0.3V, Sync must
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] be 0.0V.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. To overcome the R1 pull-up and hit exactly 0.0V, R2 would have to be 0Ω (a direct wire
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] from the GPIO to the TV).
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 5. But if R2 is 0Ω, when you output High for White, you are dumping a direct 3.3V into the TV. This
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] is vastly out of spec (video max is ~1.0V) and will blow out the picture.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because R2 has to be high enough to keep White at 0.77V, it will always act as a bottleneck when
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulling down, preventing you from reaching 0.0V for Sync.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Are there any other 1-pin ideas?
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you absolutely must use 1 pin and want those specific absolute DC levels, here are two hardware
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] "hacks" to bypass the math limitation:
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Idea 1: The Schottky Diode Hack*
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can add a Schottky diode (like a BAT54 or 1N5819) in parallel with R2.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Connect the Anode to the Video Out.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Connect the Cathode (the striped end) to the GPIO pin.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • How it works:*
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Sync (GPIO Low): The diode conducts, acting like a direct wire and bypassing R2. It yanks
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] the voltage down hard to about ~0.2V (the forward voltage drop of the diode).
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Black (GPIO High-Z): The diode blocks. R1 sets the voltage to 0.3V.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • White (GPIO High): The diode blocks. R1 and R2 set the voltage to 0.77V.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Note: This gets your Sync down to 0.2V, giving you a 100mV sync depth. It's still smaller
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] than the standard 300mV, but it is twice as deep as the passive resistor version.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Idea 2: Use an active component (Transistor/FET)*
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Instead of R1 going to 3.3V, you use the GPIO pin to switch a transistor that pulls the line up to
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3.3V, while using the same GPIO to pull it down. However, at 13.875 MHz, finding a discrete
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] transistor that switches fast enough without smearing your teletext data becomes very difficult.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Standard Industry Solution
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] This exact mathematical roadblock is why almost all 1-pin microcontroller video generation (like
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] the classic Arduino TVout library) uses the AC-Coupled / DC-offset approach I mentioned
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] earlier (R1=120Ω, R2=150Ω).
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] Televisions have a circuit called a "DC Restorer" or "Clamper" on their input. When they see a Sync
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulse, they don't care if it's 0.0V or 1.0V. They grab whatever the lowest voltage is, internally
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] redefine that exact voltage as 0.0V, and measure the Black and White levels relative to it.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] If your goal is simply to make the teletext readable on a TV, abandoning absolute DC voltages and
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] focusing on the relative 300mV / 470mV steps (using 120Ω / 150Ω) is the most reliable 1-pin
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] solution.
2026-04-23 11:19:37 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:25:46 [nitro@ryzen5] ok thanks, just to be clear, r1 on the 3.3V pin is 150 and r2 on the GPIO pin is 120
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] Actually, it is the other way around!
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] To get the perfect 300mV sync drop and the correct ~470mV Teletext data jump, you want:
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] • R1 (connected to the 3.3V pin) = 120 Ω
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] • R2 (connected to the GPIO pin) = 150 Ω
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you swap them (R1=150, R2=120), your sync pulse will still be fine, but your Teletext data will
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] be much brighter than the 66% white level you are aiming for (it will jump to nearly 95% white).
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] Sticking with R1 = 120 Ω and R2 = 150 Ω will give you the exact proportions you
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] need for standard PAL compatibility!
2026-04-23 11:26:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:55:56 [nitro@ryzen5] video signal looks good, teletext is nearly there, only 2 chars in header are bung
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] It looks like you have done a great job getting the PAL video and Teletext signals working.
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Generating a stable WST signal is no small feat!
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] Based on the symptoms of exactly two characters being "bung" (corrupted or missing) in the header,
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] there are two highly likely culprits in how the WST standard expects the packet data to be
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] formatted.
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Missing Alpha Control Character
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] In Teletext, the first character of the header text (character position 8 on the screen) is almost
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] always expected to be an alphanumeric color control code (e.g., `0x07` for Alpha White).
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you transmit standard printable characters (like `'A'` and `'B'`) at the very beginning of the
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] string without setting a color first, many TV decoders will either swallow the first character(s),
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] display them in black (invisible), or glitch.
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] You can fix this by prepending the control code to your title string. (Note: this consumes 1
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] character of your 32-character limit, so you'll need to trim a character to keep it exactly 32
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] bytes).
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] // \x07 is the Teletext control code for Alpha White
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] const char *title32 = "\x07" "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUV-01:23:45";
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. The "Erase Page" Control Bit
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] I noticed a mismatch between your comment and the code in the control bytes section of `wstHeader`:
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] // and Control Bits (Defaults)
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[0]; // C4=1: Erase Page
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[0];
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] By passing `0` to the Hamming table, you are setting all control bits in that byte to `0` (meaning
2026-04-23 11:59:49 [gemini-3.1-pro] C4 is `0`). If the Erase Page bit is not set, the TV might attempt to overlay the new header text
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] on top of whatever was previously in its memory buffer, causing characters to merge and look
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] corrupted.
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] In Control Byte 1, the `C4` (Erase Page) bit is the LSB (D1). To set it to `1`, you should pass `1`
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] into the Hamming table.
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the corrected snippet for your `wstHeader` function:
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Sub-page S1 S2 S3 S4
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[0];
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[0];
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[0];
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[0];
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Control Bits
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Byte 1 (C4, C5, C6, C7) -> D1 is C4 (Erase Page)
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[1];
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Byte 2 (C8, C9, C10, C11) -> Default 0
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[0];
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] Summary
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your Hamming table, parity calculations, and LSB-first bit/byte PIO shifting logic are all
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly aligned with the WST specification. Applying the `0x07` color control code to the start
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] of your string and ensuring the `C4` bit is asserted should clear up those last two bung characters
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] for you.
2026-04-23 11:59:50 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:04:16 [nitro@ryzen5] the erase bit means no header is show, is my tv expecting a full page?
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, you hit the nail exactly on the head!
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Most modern (post-1990s) Teletext decoders use a double-buffer system. When the TV sees a Row 0, it
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] clears the current capture buffer (if the Erase bit is set) and starts capturing the rows that
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] follow. It waits until it receives the next Row 0 before it flips that buffer to the
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] screen.
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] If you are continuously sending Row 0 with the Erase bit set to 1, the TV is clearing its buffer 50
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] times a second and never actually rendering it to the screen. For a standalone rolling header (like
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] a clock or a static title), you should definitely leave the Erase bit set to `0` (using your
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] `HAMMING_84[0]`) so the TV just updates the header row in place without wiping the page.
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] Since the Erase bit wasn't the fix for the two bung characters, it usually comes down to one of two
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] things. Which two characters in the string are glitching?
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 1. If it's the FIRST two characters:*
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] The TV is almost certainly expecting a Teletext Control Code at the start of the text block. In the
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] WST specification, the character at position 8 (which is the first character of your 32-byte
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] string) dictates the text color. If you send standard letters instead of a control code, some
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] decoders will swallow the first character or two while trying to establish the formatting.
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Fix:* Try making the first character `\x07` (Alpha White) to see if the rest of the string
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] perfectly aligns.
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • 2. If it's the LAST two characters:*
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] This is likely a horizontal timing cutoff. Your DMA transfer is exactly 48 bytes (3 bytes of `0x00`
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] delay + 45 bytes of WST data). At the Teletext clock rate of 6.9375 MHz, 48 bytes takes about 55.35
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] µs. Depending on exactly when your PIO state machine starts relative to the horizontal sync pulse,
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] those last two characters might be bleeding into the front porch or the next sync pulse, causing
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] the TV to cut them off.
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Fix:* Try reducing your initial delay from 3 bytes of `0x00` to 2 bytes or 1 byte, which will
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] shift the whole data packet slightly to the left on the scanline and might save the end of the
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] string.
2026-04-23 12:05:22 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:32 [nitro@ryzen5] for a wstBody function is this appropriate
2026-04-23 12:09:33 [nitro@ryzen5] void wstBody(int mag, int tens, int singles, int row, const char* text32, uint32_t* out32) {	uint8_t packet[48] = {0};	int p=0;	packet[p++] = 0x00;	packet[p++] = 0x00;	packet[p++] = 0x00;	packet[p++] = 0x55;	packet[p++] = 0x55;	packet[p++] = 0x27;	int magrow=(mag & 7)|((row&31)<<3);	packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[magrow&15];packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[(magrow>>4)&15];}
2026-04-23 12:09:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are very close, and your Magazine and Row Address Group (MRAG) calculation is exactly right.
2026-04-23 12:09:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] However, there is a key difference in the structure of a Teletext body row compared to a header
2026-04-23 12:09:52 [gemini-3.1-pro] row.
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because body rows (Rows 1 through 23) do not contain page numbers (`tens`, `singles`) or control
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] bits, those 8 bytes are freed up. This means a standard Teletext body row holds 40
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] characters of text, rather than 32. The TV decoder knows which page these rows belong to
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] because it remembers the last Row 0 header it received for that magazine.
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is the completed and appropriate `wstBody` function. I have removed the unused `tens` and
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] `singles` parameters, expanded the text buffer to 40 characters, and added the parity and 32-bit
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] packing loops to match your DMA setup:
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] void wstBody(int mag, int row, const char* text40, uint32_t* out32) {
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	uint8_t packet[48] = {0};
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int p = 0;
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Front porch delay
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = 0x00;
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = 0x00;
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = 0x00;
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Clock Run-In and Framing Code
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = 0x55;
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = 0x55;
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = 0x27;
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Magazine and Row Address Group (MRAG)
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int magrow = (mag & 7) | ((row & 31) << 3);
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[magrow & 15];
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	packet[p++] = HAMMING_84[(magrow >> 4) & 15];
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// 40 characters of text with odd parity
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for (int i = 0; i < 40; i++) {
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		packet[p + i] = oddParity(text40[i]);
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// Pack into 12 32-bit words for the PIO DMA
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for (int i = 0; i < 12; i++) {
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		uint32_t out = 0;
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		for (int b = 0; b < 4; b++) {
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			int index = (i * 4) + b;
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 			out |= ((uint32_t)packet[index]) << (b * 8);
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		}
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 		out32[i] = out;
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	}
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] To use it, you just pass a 40-character string (padding with spaces if your text is shorter) like
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] this:
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] const char *body40 = "\x02""Green text!                            ";
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] wstBody(1, 1, body40, &wstOdd[p]);
2026-04-23 12:09:53 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
