2026-04-23 12:21:35 [nitro@ryzen5] still lost sync
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are still falling victim to the copy-paste bug in `initTeletext()`!
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] In the Odd Field generation, you are still passing `&wstEven[p]` to the `wstBody` function instead
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] of `&wstOdd[p]`.
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Why this causes "Lost Sync"
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because `wstOdd` is a global array, the 12 words where the body text should be are left initialized
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] to exactly `0x00000000`.
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] When the Teletext PIO state machine reads those zeros, it shifts them out to the video pin. In a
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] composite video signal, `0` is the Sync Level (0 Volts). Shifting out 48 bytes of zeros at
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 6.9375 MHz holds the video pin at 0V for about 55 microseconds.
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] A normal Horizontal Sync (HSYNC) pulse is only 4.7 µs long. When your TV sees the signal drop to 0V
2026-04-23 12:23:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] for 55 µs right in the middle of the active picture, it interprets it as a Vertical Sync (VSYNC)
2026-04-23 12:23:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] pulse and completely loses horizontal and vertical lock!
2026-04-23 12:23:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:23:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Fix
2026-04-23 12:23:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] Change `&wstEven[p]` to `&wstOdd[p]` on line 186.
2026-04-23 12:23:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:23:03 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
