2026-04-23 12:25:43 [nitro@ryzen5] the 368+16 is wrong
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] You are absolutely right, and you just found the exact reason for the massive sync loss!
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] I can tell you exactly why `368+16` (which equals 384) is wrong, and why adding the second
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] line caused your TV to completely lose sync.
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] The PIO `N-1` Loop Bug
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] Your Teletext data is exactly 48 bytes long (3 bytes of `0x00` delay + 45 bytes of WST data).
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 48 bytes × 8 bits = 384 bits.
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] In the RP2040 PIO, loops are typically written using the `jmp x--` instruction. A quirk of this
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] instruction is that it loops X + 1 times. To shift out 384 bits, you must send the PIO a
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] command of 383.
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] By sending `384` (`368+16`), you told the PIO to shift out 385 bits.
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Cascade Failure
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] When you only had one Teletext line, the PIO shifted 385 bits, which slightly misaligned the FIFO,
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] but it just ate one of the `0`s from your empty lines and the Vertical Sync eventually reset
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] everything.
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] But when you added the second Teletext line (the body row), here is exactly what happened:
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. The PIO reads `384` and shifts out 385 bits for the Header row.
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Because it needed 1 extra bit, it pulls the next 32-bit word from the DMA to get it.
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 3. The next word in the DMA was supposed to be the command for the Body row (`384`), but the PIO
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] just consumed it as video data!
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 4. Now the PIO needs a command for the Body row, so it reads the next word. The next word
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] is the first 4 bytes of your Body text: `0x00`, `0x00`, `0x00`, `0x55`.
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 5. Because of your LSB-first packing, that word is `0x55000000`.
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 6. The PIO interprets `0x55000000` as the loop counter, and attempts to shift out 1.4 Billion
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] bits!
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] At your Teletext clock speed, shifting 1.4 billion bits takes about 3.5 minutes. The
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] Teletext PIO completely hangs, pulls all remaining data out of the DMA, and holds the video pin
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] hostage (likely at 0V), completely destroying the sync pulses from your Video PIO.
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] The Fix
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] Change all four of your command words to 383:
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] void initTeletext() {
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int p = 0;
2026-04-23 12:27:01 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int skip1=10;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	int skip2=297;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// --- EVEN FIELD (309 Lines) ---
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i=0; i<skip1; i++) wstEven[p++] = 0;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = 383; // 384 bits - 1
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstHeader(1, 0, 0, title32, &wstEven[p]); p += 12;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstEven[p++] = 383; // 384 bits - 1
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstBody(1, 1, line40, &wstEven[p]); p += 12;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i=0; i<skip2; i++) wstEven[p++] = 0;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// --- ODD FIELD (310 Lines) ---
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	p = 0;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i=0; i<skip1; i++) wstOdd[p++] = 0;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstOdd[p++] = 383; // 384 bits - 1
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstHeader(1, 0, 1, title32, &wstOdd[p]); p += 12;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstOdd[p++] = 383; // 384 bits - 1
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	// (And keep the pointer fix from earlier!)
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	wstBody(1, 1, line40, &wstOdd[p]); p += 12;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 	for(int i=0; i<skip2+1; i++) wstOdd[p++] = 0;
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] }
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] This will keep the 32-bit DMA words perfectly aligned, prevent the PIO from eating the next line's
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] command word, and your sync will return immediately!
2026-04-23 12:27:02 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 12:33:03 [nitro@ryzen5] should wstbody send 40 chars instead of the control bits like header?
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] Yes. `wstBody` is correct to send 40 characters, and the header is correct to send only 32
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] characters plus control metadata.
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] In World System Teletext:
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] • Row 0 (Page Header): After the magazine/row address, the packet carries the page number
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] (2 bytes), sub-page code (4 bytes) and control bits (2 bytes) — all Hamming 8/4 encoded. That
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] consumes 8 bytes, leaving 32 bytes for the header text.
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] • Rows 1–24 (Body): After the magazine/row address there is no page metadata. Those 8 bytes
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] are free for displayable text, giving 40 characters per line.
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] Both cases fill the same 48-byte payload (12 DMA words) because the header sacrifices 8 bytes of
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] text for the page/control data. Your sizing matches this exactly:
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:34:10 [kimi-k2.6] • `wstHeader`: 3 delay + 3 run-in + 2 mag/row + 2 page + 4 sub-page + 2 control + 32 text = 48
2026-04-23 12:34:11 [kimi-k2.6] bytes
2026-04-23 12:34:11 [kimi-k2.6] • `wstBody`: 3 delay + 3 run-in + 2 mag/row + 40 text = 48 bytes
2026-04-23 12:34:11 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:34:11 [kimi-k2.6] So the distinction between the two functions is correct.
2026-04-23 12:34:11 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:47:08 [nitro@ryzen5] i am getting a stall with TeletextStateMachine causing led to flash
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] The stall is almost certainly a DMA underflow between fields, not a problem with the 40 vs
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 32 byte payload layout.
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] Your `wstEven` / `wstOdd` arrays contain exactly one field worth of data (333 / 334 words). Once
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] the WST state machine pulls the last word, the TX FIFO is empty. If the SM executes another `out`
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] or `pull`—even briefly—before `vid_field()` reloads the DMA for the next field, the hardware
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] latches the sticky `TXSTALL` flag. Your main loop flips the LED every time it sees that flag, so
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] you get a flash roughly every field (~50 Hz in PAL).
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] How to confirm this is the cause
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] If the LED toggles at a steady field rate and your teletext packets are visible on screen, the
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] stall is purely inter-field. If the LED is flickering erratically or staying on, the SM is starving
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] mid-field because the PIO program expects more words than the DMA is providing.
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] Fixes
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] • 1. Ignore the inter-field stall (simplest)*
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] Since the gap is expected, don't use `TXSTALL` on the teletext SM as an error indicator. You can
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] still monitor the video SM for stalls, or only flag an error if the teletext SM stalls many times
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] within a few milliseconds.
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] • 2. Pause the teletext SM during the gap*
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] When the WST DMA finishes, briefly disable the SM so it cannot attempt another pull. Re-enable it
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] in `vid_field()` before pushing the next buffer.
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] void vid_field(int field) {
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 	// Re-enable SM before feeding it new data
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 	pio_sm_set_enabled(pio0, TeletextStateMachine, true);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 	if (field & 1) {
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 		dma_channel_set_trans_count(video_dma_chan, wordCount + words, false);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 		dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, oddFrame, true);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 		dma_channel_set_trans_count(wst_dma_chan, wstWords + 1, false);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 		dma_channel_set_read_addr(wst_dma_chan, wstOdd, true);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 	} else {
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 		dma_channel_set_trans_count(video_dma_chan, wordCount, false);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 		dma_channel_set_read_addr(video_dma_chan, evenFrame, true);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 		dma_channel_set_trans_count(wst_dma_chan, wstWords, false);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 		dma_channel_set_read_addr(wst_dma_chan, wstEven, true);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 	}
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] void wst_idle_guard() {
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 	static bool was_busy = true;
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 	bool busy = dma_channel_is_busy(wst_dma_chan);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 	if (was_busy && !busy) {
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 		// DMA exhausted; pause SM to prevent end-of-field stall
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 		pio_sm_set_enabled(pio0, TeletextStateMachine, false);
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 	}
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 	was_busy = busy;
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] }
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] Call `wstidleguard()` in your main loop.
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] • Caveat:* ensure the last packet has fully shifted out before disabling, or the final line may be
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] truncated. A small delay or checking `piosmget_pc()` against the program end can help if
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] exact timing matters.
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] • 3. Fix it in PIO (most robust)*
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] Change the WST program so that after the last line of the field it enters a `wait irq` state
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] instead of relying on an empty FIFO. Use `piosmexec()` or an IRQ from the video SM to wake
2026-04-23 12:51:45 [kimi-k2.6] the teletext SM at the start of the next field, then trigger the DMA. This eliminates the stall
2026-04-23 12:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] entirely.
2026-04-23 12:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] • 4. Double-check the word count*
2026-04-23 12:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] If the PIO program consumes a sentinel word after the last packet—e.g., a final zero to return to
2026-04-23 12:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] an idle loop—make sure your `wstWords` count includes it. An off-by-one here would cause a
2026-04-23 12:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] mid-field stall rather than a clean end-of-field pause.
2026-04-23 12:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] Given your buffer math (`skip1` + packets + `skip2` = 333 / 334) lines up exactly with the array
2026-04-23 12:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] sizes, the end-of-field underflow is the most likely culprit.
2026-04-23 12:51:46 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 12:54:38 [nitro@ryzen5] fixed that issue, still losing sync due to sending new body line telext row 1
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] The buffer layout is correct: row 0 (header) carries 32 displayable characters plus the
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] page/sub‑page/control Hamming bytes, and rows 1‑24 (body) carry 40 displayable characters. Your
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] `wstWords` math (10 + 1 + 12 + 1 + 12 + 297 = 333) also matches the array size.
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] The sync loss when you add the second packet almost certainly means the teletext state machine
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] is not idling for a full line when it sees a zero command, so the whole `wstEven`/`wstOdd`
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] buffer is being blasted out as a single burst at the top of the field. One short packet might fit
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] inside a vertical‑blanking interval without destroying sync, but two back‑to‑back packets (≈110 µs
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] of active data) overrun into horizontal or vertical sync and the TV loses lock.
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] How to tell if this is happening
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] Temporarily set `skip1` to something large like 100 and recompile.
2026-04-23 13:03:33 [kimi-k2.6] • If the teletext still appears at the very top of the frame (or the LED flash / sync‑loss
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] behaviour does not change), your PIO program is consuming the zero words instantly instead of
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] burning one PAL line time (64 µs) per entry.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] • If the packet moves down the screen by 100 lines, the program is already line‑locked and the
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] problem is something else (see below).
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] What the WST PIO program should do
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] Because your C++ buffer is structured as one entry per scanline, the PIO loop must take
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] exactly one video line period per FIFO word, regardless of the command value.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 1. Pull the command word (the `384` or `0`).
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 2. If non‑zero: shift out that many bits (your 48‑byte packet) and then idle for the
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] remainder of the 64 µs.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 3. If zero: do not drive the pin, but still consume the full 64 µs before the next
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] `pull`.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 4. Only then wrap back to fetch the next word.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] If your program instead does “`pull`, check zero, jump to end, pull next word”, the 297 trailing
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] zeros collapse into a few microseconds and the two real packets emit as one continuous burst.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] Secondary issue: horizontal timing
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] Even when line‑locked, make sure the bitstream does not start immediately after the `pull`.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] The first teletext bit must wait out the horizontal sync pulse and colour burst (roughly 10–12 µs
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] into the line). If the WST SM drives `VID_PIN` during H‑sync, it overrides the video state
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] machine’s sync output. One corrupted sync line might be tolerated; two in a row usually is not.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] Pin ownership note
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] Both your video SM and teletext SM are mapped to `VID_PIN`. On RP2350 the highest‑numbered SM wins
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] when it actively drives the pin. While the WST SM is mid‑packet it completely masks the video SM.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] If your PIO program is simply bursting through FIFO data, the video SM cannot output any sync for
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] the duration of that burst.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] Summary
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] • The 40‑character body packet is correct.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] • The sync loss is almost certainly because the WST SM emits the header and body as a single
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] free‑running burst rather than as two distinct line‑timed injections.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] • Fix the `wst.pio` loop so that every word, including zeros, costs exactly one PAL line
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] (64 µs, i.e. `9504 / clkdiv` PIO cycles). Add a front‑porch delay so data does not clobber H‑sync.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] If you can share the `wst.pio` source, I can point to the exact instruction where the timing slips.
2026-04-23 13:03:34 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:28 [kimi-k2.6] The stall is gone, but now the bit count is off by one, and that corrupts the PIO shift
2026-04-23 13:17:28 [kimi-k2.6] register boundary so the second packet is never read correctly.
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] The bug
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] In `wst.pio`:
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] loop:
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 	out	pindirs, 1
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 	jmp	x--, loop
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] `jmp x--` decrements after the test, so if you load `x = 384` the loop body executes
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 385 times:
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] • `x = 384 … 1` → jump taken (384 iterations)
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] • `x = 0` → jump not taken, but the preceding `out` still runs (1 more iteration)
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] That 385th `out` pulls bit 0 of the next FIFO word (the following line’s command word) into
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] the pin direction, leaving the OSR shift-counter at 1 instead of 0. When the next line starts, `out
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] x, 32` does not autopull a fresh word; it shifts the remaining 31 bits of the command word
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] plus one zero into `x`. The result is a garbage bit-count (often very large), the state machine
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] drives the pin for hundreds of microseconds, and the TV loses horizontal lock.
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] With only one packet in the field the damage may be hidden because the next word is a `0` (no
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] transmission), but with two consecutive packets the second command word (`384`) is the one
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] that gets partially consumed. That is why adding the body line (row 1) kills sync.
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] The fix
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] Change the command words from `384` to `383` (i.e. number of bits minus one).
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] In `initTeletext()`:
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] wstEven[p++] = 383;   // was 384
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] // ...
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] wstOdd[p++] = 383;    // was 384
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] If you ever change the packet size, the rule is:
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] command_word = (packet_bytes * 8) - 1;
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] Why this works
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] With `x = 383`:
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] • Jump taken for `x = 383 … 1` → 383 iterations
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] • Final `out` at `x = 0` → 1 iteration
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] • Total `out` executions = 384 exactly
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 384 bits = 12 PIO FIFO words. The OSR is cleanly emptied, the shift counter resets to 0, and the
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] next `out x, 32` autopulls the next line’s command word correctly.
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] Timing margin check
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] At your divider (≈13.83 MHz) the packet takes roughly:
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] overhead  ~25 cycles
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] 384 bits  768 cycles
2026-04-23 13:17:29 [kimi-k2.6] total     793 cycles  ≈ 57.3 µs
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] One PAL line in your WST clock domain is about 885 cycles. You finish roughly 90 WST cycles (≈6.5
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] µs) before the next line’s IRQ, so the packet fits safely inside the active-to-front-porch window
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] without encroaching on the next horizontal sync.
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] Secondary recommendation
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] 
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] The end-of-field stall you were using to blink the LED will still fire after the last word of every
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] field because the FIFO is empty and the SM is waiting. That is expected; you can safely ignore
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] `TXSTALL` on the teletext state machine, or move the stall check back to `VideoStateMachine` if you
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] want it as a genuine fault indicator.
2026-04-23 13:17:30 [kimi-k2.6] 
