2026-04-23 17:13:55 [nitro@ryzen5] what does spec say about which lines to use for wst bit stream?
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] For 625/50 PAL teletext (WST, ETSI EN 300 706 / ITU-R System B/G), the teletext data is
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] carried in the vertical blanking interval.
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] Commonly used lines are:
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Field 1: lines 6–22
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Field 2: lines 318–335
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] In practice, broadcasters often use a subset (not every line), and some receivers are picky about
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] the earliest lines.
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] So for robust DIY insertion:
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Prefer mid-VBI lines first (not the very earliest available).
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Keep packets fully inside active blanking/data window on each chosen line.
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] - If a TV is touchy, move your rows a few lines later rather than earlier.
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] Given your stable result after moving later, that matches expected receiver behavior.
2026-04-23 17:14:00 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
