2026-04-23 18:00:33 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-23 18:00:33 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 13.5 MHz (ITU-R BT.601 / CCIR 601 luma sample clock) for SD digital video
2026-04-23 18:00:33 [gpt-5.3-codex] - From that, derived clocks like 27 MHz
2026-04-23 18:00:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - For analog encoders, timing is usually locked to line frequency and subcarrier
2026-04-23 18:00:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] relationships via house sync/genlock, not “pixel clock” in the modern HDMI sense
2026-04-23 18:00:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-23 18:00:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] So for PAL composite generation, what matters most is:
2026-04-23 18:00:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-23 18:00:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - accurate line rate (15.625 kHz),
2026-04-23 18:00:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - correct field cadence/interlace timing,
2026-04-23 18:00:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - correct sync/blanking levels and durations,
2026-04-23 18:00:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - and for teletext, precise bit clock and placement in VBI.
2026-04-23 18:00:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
