2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] Yes, I can confirm that the sequence `[hsync, back porch, active, front porch]` is the
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] exact and correct standard order for an analog video line.
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] Regarding adding 32 pixels, there is a slight discrepancy in your current timing that we should
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] address first. Your top comment mentions `10 cycles per pixel`, but your current active loop takes
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 11 cycles (`out pindirs, 1 [9]` takes 10 cycles, plus 1 cycle for the `jmp`).
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] Assuming your current active width is 768 pixels (768 * 11 = 8448 cycles), adding 32 pixels gives
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] you 800 pixels.
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here are two ways to implement this, depending on whether you want to keep the 11-cycle pixels or
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] switch to 10-cycle pixels. Both snippets are mathematically balanced to hit exactly 10,656
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] cycles per line.
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] Option 1: 800 pixels at 11 cycles per pixel
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] At 11 cycles per pixel, 800 pixels takes 52.85 µs. This is slightly wider than the standard PAL
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] active area (52 µs), which means we have to squeeze the front porch down to ~0.5 µs to maintain the
2026-04-23 21:18:10 [gemini-3.1-pro] 64 µs total line time. Most modern displays and CRTs will still sync to this perfectly fine.
