2026-04-23 21:18:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] • Note on C++ integration:*
2026-04-23 21:18:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] Whichever option you choose, remember to update the DMA `wordCount` and the pixel counter loaded
2026-04-23 21:18:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] into the `isr` register from the C++ side. For 800 pixels, the counter pushed to the `isr` should
2026-04-23 21:18:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] be `799`.
2026-04-23 21:18:11 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 21:32:31 [nitro@ryzen5] no, pio loops are count n NOT n+1
2026-04-23 21:33:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] In the RP2040 and RP2350 PIO, a loop ending with `jmp y--, label` actually executes N + 1
2026-04-23 21:33:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] times when `y` is initialized to `N`.
2026-04-23 21:33:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 21:33:26 [gemini-3.1-pro] Because the hardware evaluates the condition before the decrement, a value of `0` still
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] executes the instruction (including its delay cycles) before falling through.
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] Here is a quick trace of `set y, 1`:
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 1. Iteration 1: `y` is 1. `y != 0` is True. Jump is taken. `y` decrements to 0.
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 2. Iteration 2: `y` is 0. `y != 0` is False. Jump is not taken. `y` decrements to -1.
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] Both iterations consume the instruction's base cycle plus any `[delay]` cycles. To get exactly `N`
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] iterations of a loop, the counter must be initialized to `N - 1`.
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] • (Note: In your original code, `set y, 27` followed by `jmp y--, hsync [28]` actually executes 28
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] times, consuming 812 cycles rather than the 783 cycles calculated by `27  29`).
2026-04-23 21:33:27 [gemini-3.1-pro] 
