2026-04-24 09:23:32 [nitro@ryzen5] the erase bit causes the header to be hidden, is it possible the ham table is msb not lsb?
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] Yes — that is very possible, and your symptom matches it.
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] If your Hamming 8/4 LUT bit order doesn’t match transmit bit order on-air, control bits (like
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] erase) can decode wrong while other parts appear “sort of working.”
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] For teletext, the key is:
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] - nibble value (0..15) is Hamming-encoded to 8 bits,
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] - then transmitted LSB first on the line.
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] If your LUT was generated in MSB-first bit significance, it will behave incorrectly when shifted
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] out LSB-first.
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] Given your code path (`smconfigsetoutshift(..., true, true, 32)` and `out
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] pindirs,1`), I’d definitely verify this with a known-good test:
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] - transmit a minimal header where only one control bit changes,
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] - compare decoder behavior for both:
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- current LUT,
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- bit-reversed LUT (reverse each 8-bit codeword).
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] If flipping LUT bit order makes C4 behave correctly, that confirms it.
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] Also possible (second check): C4 might be in a different nibble position than assumed in your
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] header packing, so ensure byte pair mapping for control bits exactly follows packet 0 spec field
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] order.
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] So short answer: yes, LUT bit endianness is a prime suspect.
2026-04-24 09:23:39 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
