2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - PIO clock = 166.5 MHz
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] - WST bit loop = `out ... [22]` + `jmp ...` = 24 cycles/bit
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] So:
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] \[
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] f_{bit} = \frac{166.5\text{ MHz}}{24} = 6.9375\text{ MHz}
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] \]
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] That is exactly the teletext spec nominal (6.9375 Mb/s), assuming your system clock
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] is truly 166.5 MHz.
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] Bit period:
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] \[
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] T_{bit} = \frac{1}{6.9375\text{ MHz}} = 144.0\text{ ns}
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] \]
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:52:08 [gpt-5.3-codex] So on paper you’re spot-on; only real-world error would come from actual clock accuracy
