2026-04-24 09:56:18 [nitro@ryzen5] on the hardware side, is there anything i can do to improve signal quality?
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] Yes—quite a bit, and most of it is simple.
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] Biggest wins
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Use a proper 75 Ω back-terminated source
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	Keep your resistor network but aim for a source impedance close to 75 Ω seen by the cable.
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] Mismatch causes ringing/ghost edges that hurt teletext slicing.
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Short ground return + coax output
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	Use a real coax connector (RCA/BNC) with solid ground close to the pin network. Long jumper
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] grounds are noisy.
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Keep the video node physically tiny
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	Pin + resistors + connector should be tightly clustered. Long traces/wires at that high edge rate
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] degrade eye shape.
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] Edge-rate / EMI control
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Try lower GPIO drive strength (you currently use 4 mA)
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	Start with 2 mA if levels still meet spec. Slower edges can improve composite quality.
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Slew rate
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	You set FAST now; test SLOW. For composite, ultra-fast edges are not required and can
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] increase overshoot.
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Small series damping at the GPIO pin
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	Add ~22–68 Ω in series right at the MCU pin (before your mixer network) to tame ringing.
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] Level / filtering
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Verify white/sync amplitudes at the TV input (75 Ω terminated)
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	Targets are roughly:
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- sync tip: -0.3 V from blank,
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- black/blank at reference,
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- white at +0.7 V above blank.
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Optional mild low-pass shaping
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	A tiny capacitor (few pF to low tens pF) at the output node can soften RF hash. Keep it small—too
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] much will blur luma and teletext edges.
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] Power integrity
2026-04-24 09:56:31 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Good local decoupling near RP2350 and output network
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Clean 3.3 V rail (LDO noise matters because your DAC is resistor-based off IO levels)
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Avoid sharing noisy digital return currents with video return path.
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] Teletext-specific practical tip
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] - If picture looks fine but teletext is flaky, prioritize:
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	1) timing, then
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	2) ringing/overshoot on transitions, then
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	3) exact amplitude at the decoder input.
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] If you want, I can help you choose resistor values for a “known-good” 2-level composite mix
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] (sync/black/white) with your exact pin mode strategy.
2026-04-24 09:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
