2026-04-24 11:31:45 [nitro@ryzen5] default pullup state for rp2350 gpio
2026-04-24 11:31:51 [gpt-5.3-codex] On RP2350 GPIO, after reset the pins are generally inputs with pulls disabled
2026-04-24 11:31:51 [gpt-5.3-codex] (high‑impedance), not pulled up by default.
2026-04-24 11:31:51 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:31:51 [gpt-5.3-codex] So: default = no pull-up, no pull-down unless firmware or boot ROM configures one for a
2026-04-24 11:31:51 [gpt-5.3-codex] specific function.
2026-04-24 11:31:51 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:32:15 [nitro@ryzen5] what does pio_gpio_init do ?
2026-04-24 11:32:19 [gpt-5.3-codex] `piogpioinit(pio, gpio)` prepares that GPIO for PIO control.
2026-04-24 11:32:19 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:32:19 [gpt-5.3-codex] In practice it:
2026-04-24 11:32:19 [gpt-5.3-codex] - sets the pin’s function mux to the chosen PIO block (`PIO0/1/2`),
2026-04-24 11:32:19 [gpt-5.3-codex] - leaves direction/value behavior to the state machine config/instructions (`set pindirs`, `out
2026-04-24 11:32:19 [gpt-5.3-codex] pins`, etc).
2026-04-24 11:32:19 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:32:19 [gpt-5.3-codex] It does not by itself start a state machine or set pull-ups/pull-downs/drive strength.
2026-04-24 11:32:19 [gpt-5.3-codex] Those are separate GPIO/pad settings.
2026-04-24 11:32:19 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:32:58 [nitro@ryzen5] do you still need to call gpio_init ?
2026-04-24 11:33:02 [gpt-5.3-codex] No—if a pin is only used by PIO, `piogpioinit()` is enough.
2026-04-24 11:33:02 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:33:02 [gpt-5.3-codex] You still use `gpio_init()` when you want normal SIO GPIO control/settings (or to be explicit
2026-04-24 11:33:02 [gpt-5.3-codex] before non-PIO config). For PIO-only pins, it’s typically redundant.
2026-04-24 11:33:02 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
