2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] For proper PAL 625/50 interlaced sync, vertical interval uses:
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 5 pre-equalizing pulses
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 5 broad (serration) pulses
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 5 post-equalizing pulses
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] So: 15 half-line pulses total in the vertical sync sequence.
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] Practical notes:
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Equalizing pulses are short sync pulses at half-line rate.
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Broad pulses are long low pulses, also arranged at half-line cadence, with serrations to keep H
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] timing alive.
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] - This pattern is what preserves robust interlace and field identification.
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] So compared with your current single long low, standards-correct behavior is this 5+5+5 pulse train
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] centered around field boundary.
2026-04-24 11:53:27 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:53:56 [nitro@ryzen5] same lengths for both odd and even frames?
2026-04-24 11:53:59 [gpt-5.3-codex] Not exactly — the structure is the same (5 + 5 + 5), but the timing is offset by half a
2026-04-24 11:53:59 [gpt-5.3-codex] line between fields.
2026-04-24 11:53:59 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:53:59 [gpt-5.3-codex] For PAL interlace:
2026-04-24 11:53:59 [gpt-5.3-codex] - both fields use pre-equalizing, broad, post-equalizing pulses,
2026-04-24 11:53:59 [gpt-5.3-codex] - but one field’s sequence is aligned to whole-line boundaries,
2026-04-24 11:53:59 [gpt-5.3-codex] - the other is shifted by ½ line.
2026-04-24 11:53:59 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 11:53:59 [gpt-5.3-codex] That half-line offset is what creates true interlace (line positions between fields).
2026-04-24 11:53:59 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
