2026-04-24 12:08:00 [nitro@ryzen5] describe the serration notches
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] Serration notches are the short high intervals cut into the long broad vertical-sync pulse.
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] In plain terms:
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - During vertical sync, instead of one continuous long low, broadcasters insert repeating “teeth”.
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Each tooth is a brief return toward blank/high level at line-rate timing.
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - This preserves horizontal timing reference while vertical sync is happening.
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] So in your half-line slot model:
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Broad pulse slot is mostly low,
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - but includes a short high notch (serration) at the expected H cadence,
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - then back low.
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] Why they matter:
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - TVs/separators still need horizontal phase info during vertical interval.
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Without serrations, some sets lose horizontal lock or interlace stability.
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] From your PIO perspective, a broad slot becomes multi-segment, e.g.:
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - low segment → short high notch → low segment
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] (with durations chosen so total slot time stays exact).
2026-04-24 12:08:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
