2026-04-24 20:49:48 [nitro@ryzen5] does out y,31 clear all bits and load the bottom 31 bits?
2026-04-24 20:49:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] Yes. In RP2xxx PIO, `out y, 31` shifts 31 bits from OSR into Y.
2026-04-24 20:49:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 20:49:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] So effectively:
2026-04-24 20:49:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `Y[30:0]` = next 31 bits from OSR
2026-04-24 20:49:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `Y[31]` = 0 (cleared)
2026-04-24 20:49:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 20:49:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] So your MSB flag (`SYNC=0x80000000`) is not copied into `Y` by `out y,31`. That part is dropped.
2026-04-24 20:49:52 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 20:50:08 [nitro@ryzen5] why is y[31] cleared?
2026-04-24 20:50:13 [gpt-5.3-codex] Because `OUT` writes exactly the requested bit-count into the destination shift register-style
2026-04-24 20:50:13 [gpt-5.3-codex] field.
2026-04-24 20:50:13 [gpt-5.3-codex] When the destination is a full register (`X`/`Y`/`ISR`) and count is less than 32, the unwritten
2026-04-24 20:50:13 [gpt-5.3-codex] upper bits are zero-filled by the hardware for that transfer width.
2026-04-24 20:50:13 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-24 20:50:13 [gpt-5.3-codex] So with `out y, 31`, PIO defines the result as a 31-bit value in `Y[30:0]`, and the remaining bit
2026-04-24 20:50:13 [gpt-5.3-codex] (`Y[31]`) is cleared. It’s by design so partial-width OUTs are deterministic (no stale high bit
2026-04-24 20:50:13 [gpt-5.3-codex] left over).
2026-04-24 20:50:13 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
