2026-04-25 10:00:43 [nitro@ryzen5] how does tv know odd frames from even?
2026-04-25 10:00:47 [gpt-5.3-codex] Great question. In analog PAL, the TV doesn’t read an “odd/even bit” — it infers field parity from
2026-04-25 10:00:47 [gpt-5.3-codex] timing phase.
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Vertical sync contains equalizing/serration pulses at half-line spacing.
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - One field’s vertical sequence is aligned to whole-line timing.
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - The other is shifted by half a line (32 µs).
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] The deflection/sync separator circuitry locks to that pattern. From the phase of vertical sync
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] relative to horizontal sync, it knows “this is the other field,” so it alternates scan position
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] between odd/even line sets.
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] So: parity is encoded by a 0.5H timing offset, not by metadata.
2026-04-25 10:00:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:05:45 [nitro@ryzen5] for PAL interlace signal each frame begins with 15 half lines and then both have 305 whole lines?
2026-04-25 10:05:49 [gpt-5.3-codex] Close, but not quite.
2026-04-25 10:05:49 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:05:49 [gpt-5.3-codex] For 625/50 PAL interlaced:
2026-04-25 10:05:49 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:05:49 [gpt-5.3-codex] - A full frame has 625 lines total.
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] - It is split into two fields:
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- one field has 312.5 lines
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- the other field has 312.5 lines
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] So each field is effectively:
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] - about 305 active picture lines (depends on blanking details), plus
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] - vertical blanking/equalizing/serration intervals that include the half-line offset
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] between fields.
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] The key interlace point is the 0.5-line offset between odd/even fields, not “15 half-lines
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] then 305 whole lines for both” as a fixed per-frame pattern.
2026-04-25 10:05:50 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:06:30 [nitro@ryzen5] so each field has 15 half lines and 305 active lines?
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] [gpt-5.3-codex] Not exactly.
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] For PAL 625/50 interlace, per field you have:
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 312.5 total lines
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - of which roughly 287.5 are active video lines (so about 576 active lines per
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] frame total)
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - and the rest are vertical blanking/sync lines (including equalizing and serration pulses with
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] half-line timing).
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] So it is not “15 half-lines + 305 active lines” per field.
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] The commonly used active count is ~288 lines per field (interlaced pair gives 576).
2026-04-25 10:06:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:07:42 [nitro@ryzen5] each field has 312.5 half total lines, where does the 0.5 line come from?
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] The “0.5 line” is the intentional time offset between odd and even fields that creates
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] interlace.
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] In plain terms:
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - One horizontal line period is \(H\).
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - One field lasts 312.5 × H instead of 312 × H.
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - That extra 0.5 × H means the next field starts half a line later in vertical
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] position.
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] So:
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Field 1 starts on one set of scan lines (say odd).
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Field 2 starts shifted by half a line, so its lines land between Field 1’s lines (even).
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] How this is done in analog PAL:
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - The vertical sync/equalizing pulse pattern is arranged around half-line timing.
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - That timing causes field boundaries to alternate as 312 lines, then 313 lines in time-equivalent
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] terms, averaging 312.5 per field.
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Result: 625 lines/frame = 312.5 + 312.5, with proper interleaving.
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] So the 0.5 line doesn’t mean a “physical half-drawn picture line” in isolation; it’s a timing
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] offset used to place the second field between the first field’s lines.
2026-04-25 10:07:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:09:19 [nitro@ryzen5] if each field starts with 15 half lines pulses describe the contents of the rest of the field in terms of time
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] Yes—good way to think about it is as time budget per field.
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] For 625/50 PAL interlace, one field is 20 ms = 312.5 H (where \(H\) is one line period,
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] \(64\,\mu s\)).
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] If you model the field start as the classic vertical interval sequence:
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 15 half-lines of equalizing/serration activity
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	→ \(15 \times 32\,\mu s = 480\,\mu s = 7.5H\)
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] then the rest of that field is:
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 312.5H − 7.5H = 305H
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - i.e. 305 full line periods = 19.52 ms
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] Those 305 line periods are not all active picture; they include:
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - remaining vertical blanking lines (depending where you place the field boundary in your
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] description),
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - then the bulk of active video lines (~287.5 lines/field in 576i systems),
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - with normal horizontal blanking inside each line.
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] So in pure timing terms after the 15 half-lines, you have 305 line times left in the 20 ms
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] field.
2026-04-25 10:09:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:16:38 [nitro@ryzen5] that is still confusing, in terms of timing and considering blanking lines and active lines as the same but just with black level pixels
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] Think of everything as just line-time slots (64 µs each), whether they carry picture or
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] black.
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] For PAL 625/50 interlace:
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 1 field = 20 ms = 312.5 line-times
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] - At field start, vertical sync section is:
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- 5 half-lines pre-equalizing
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- 5 half-lines serration
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- 5 half-lines post-equalizing
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- total = 15 half-lines = 7.5 line-times = 480 µs
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] After that, remaining time in the field is:
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 312.5 − 7.5 = 305 line-times (exactly what you computed)
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:16:43 [gpt-5.3-codex] So your simplified view is valid:
2026-04-25 10:16:44 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:16:44 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Field = 7.5 line-times of special sync waveform + 305 line-times of normal line structure
2026-04-25 10:16:44 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Those 305 line-times include both what we call “blanking lines” and “active lines”; electrically
2026-04-25 10:16:44 [gpt-5.3-codex] they are just regular horizontal line periods with different video level/content.
2026-04-25 10:16:44 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:16:44 [gpt-5.3-codex] Why the half-line exists:
2026-04-25 10:16:44 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Because field length is 312.5 (not integer), next field starts shifted by 0.5
2026-04-25 10:16:44 [gpt-5.3-codex] line-time, giving interlace.
2026-04-25 10:16:44 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:18:50 [nitro@ryzen5] the attached syncs verticallay but tv is not table horizontally, especially at top of frame
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] Thanks — this is useful, and your symptom matches what I’d expect from a line-length mismatch
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] between sections.
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] You’re vertically locking, so field timing is close enough.
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] Horizontal instability (worse at top) usually means some lines in/near VBI are not exactly 64 µs
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] equivalent in your generated stream.
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] What stands out
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] In `vidblit.pio` you state:
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] - line = 10656 cycles
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] - hsync/backporch/active/frontporch designed to sum to 10656
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] That part looks coherent.
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] But your vertical sync pulse train is driven by FIFO counts (`E0/E1/B0/B1...`) and loop
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] overhead, and that timing must also map exactly to half-line/line timing. If those pulse/gap words
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] are off by even a few PIO cycles per segment, the first active lines after VBI can have phase
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] error, causing top-of-frame horizontal “tearing/wobble”.
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] Also, your current frame preambles use:
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `29` as edge-width counter
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 30 pulse-width words after that
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] - then `304,863`
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] That gives a simplified model but not a strict 625-equivalent segmentation unless the PIO loop
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] overhead is precisely accounted for in each word value (including `out`/`jmp` costs). I suspect
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] this is the main issue.
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] Secondary issue to check (important)
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] In `monobit.cpp`:
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] - `evenFrame` and `oddFrame` both use `304,863`
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] - but comments and teletext arrays imply asymmetric field line counts (309/310 contexts elsewhere)
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] Your PIO core seems to treat both fields identically after VSYNC. If half-line placement is wrong
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] (or missing explicit half-line handling between fields), you can still get vertical lock but
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] imperfect interlace/horizontal phase behavior near the top.
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] Practical fixes (highest impact first)
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 1. Make every half-line/line segment exact in PIO cycles
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Recalculate `E0/E1/B0/B1/S0/S1/HB` including loop overhead in `pulse/phase`.
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Verify with scope: sync tip width and period at the pin.
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 2. Represent V-sync as explicit half-line units
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Build preamble in half-lines (5 eq + 5 broad + 5 eq) and ensure correct odd/even field half-line
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] offset.
2026-04-25 10:19:05 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Right now `S0/S1` and `HB` are commented out; that likely removes needed alignment structure.
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 3. Confirm per-field total time
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Each field must be exactly 20 ms.
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- With your clock: `166.5e6 * 0.02 = 3,330,000` cycles per field exactly.
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Sum generated cycles from preamble + line loop must equal this for both odd/even fields.
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 4. Check DMA handover timing
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- You restart DMA when channel not busy; that’s fine if FIFO never under-runs.
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- If under-run occurs at field boundary, top-of-frame instability appears first.
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Your TXSTALL LED toggle suggests this may already happen intermittently.
2026-04-25 10:19:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
