2026-04-26 08:13:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] You’re hitting the classic trap: in your implementation, those “line counts” are not just picture
2026-04-26 08:13:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] lines — they’re also what keeps the receiver’s line PLL happy after your custom VBI block. So
2026-04-26 08:13:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] reducing them naïvely breaks total field timing/phase and TV drops lock.
2026-04-26 08:13:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 08:13:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] What’s new in your latest file is mostly comments; timing table is still effectively:
2026-04-26 08:13:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 08:13:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] - 16 half-line pairs in preamble (15 + S pair)
2026-04-26 08:13:40 [gpt-5.3-codex] - then 310/311 line-loop counts
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] That is why it locks robustly on your TV.
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] Key point (implementation-specific)
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] Your preamble pairs are ~5322 cycles, not exactly half of your executed line-loop period
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] unless the loop itself is exactly what you think cycle-wise. Any tiny mismatch accumulates across
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] the VBI segment. Then your post-VBI line count must “absorb” that mismatch to keep field cadence
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] stable.
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] So when you drop line counts to spec-ish values, you removed that compensation and lost lock.
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] What this means
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] - You currently have a working receiver-tolerant timing solution.
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] - It may not map 1:1 to ideal PAL bookkeeping on paper.
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Sync loss when reducing counts confirms hidden cycle offset is still present in PIO-path totals.
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] If you want strict audit next, the right next step is a cycle-true audit of `vidblit.pio`
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] executed paths (not nominal comments), then retarget preamble pair constants and line counts
2026-04-26 08:13:41 [gpt-5.3-codex] together.
