2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] Possible, yes — many modern TVs are pickier than older sets.
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] Short version: Bravia usually does not require all 24 rows, but it often requires a
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] validly structured service (headers, timing, continuity, page metadata) before it shows
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] anything. “No text available” usually means “decoder sees no valid service,” not “you sent too few
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] display rows.”
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] Most likely gotchas:
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Wrong VBI line placement (must be true PAL teletext lines in each field).
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Packet 0/header not consistent over time (magazine/page/control bits).
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Insufficient repeated packets/cycle continuity.
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Framing/clock run-in + framing code tolerances.
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Signal level/timing margins too tight for stricter decoder.
2026-04-26 09:55:53 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Some Bravia models disable analogue teletext unless input mode/region is set appropriately
