2026-04-26 14:56:27 [nitro@ryzen5] with wst irq enabled it annoys me loop nop has to be [22] and not [23]
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] Yep — that makes sense and it’s a classic PIO phase-alignment annoyance.
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] `irq set` costs a cycle in the stream, so when you re-enable it, your active-loop timing budget
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] shifts by one. If `[22]` is what lands the bit cadence correctly, that’s the right value for that
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] instruction mix.
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] In other words: you’re not “wrong,” you’re compensating for real instruction-time accounting. The
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] clean way is to treat it as two calibrated variants:
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] - with IRQ: loop delay = calibrated value A (`[22]`)
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] - without IRQ: loop delay = calibrated value B (`[23]`)
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-26 14:56:32 [gpt-5.3-codex] If you want to make this less irritating long-term, you can keep timing invariant by adding a
2026-04-26 14:56:33 [gpt-5.3-codex] matching 1-cycle instruction in the non-IRQ path (so both paths consume identical cycles). Then one
2026-04-26 14:56:33 [gpt-5.3-codex] delay constant works for both modes.
2026-04-26 14:56:33 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
