2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] [gpt-5.3-codex] Short answer: usually no — a 74LVC245 will likely make shimmer worse,
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] not better for composite PAL.
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] Why:
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - It’s a fast digital bus buffer (very sharp edges, no filtering).
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - It does not reduce jitter/phase noise from timing.
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - It can add ringing/overshoot unless very carefully damped.
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Composite video wants controlled analog levels into 75 Ω, not “harder” digital edges.
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] Better uses for your shimmer issue:
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 1. Stabilize clock/timing (most shimmer is timing jitter).
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 2. Enable slow slew + low drive on video pin.
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 3. Add small series damping resistor at source pin (e.g. 22–68 Ω) before your resistor
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] mixer.
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 4. RC low-pass shaping after the mixer (light filtering, not too aggressive).
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 5. Improve grounding/decoupling near MCU and output network.
2026-04-27 10:49:06 [gpt-5.3-codex] 6. Ensure proper 75 Ω termination at the display/input.
