2026-04-27 10:56:20 [nitro@ryzen5] explain how series R works, i am not familiar
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] A series resistor is just a small resistor placed in line with the signal wire,
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] close to the MCU pin.
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] For your case:
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] `GPIO pin -> 33Ω -> rest of video resistor network`
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] What it does
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - MCU pin is a very fast edge source.
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - PCB trace + cable + input capacitance behave like a little transmission system.
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Fast edges can “ring” (overshoot/undershoot), causing shimmer/noise.
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Series R + line/input capacitance forms light damping (and a tiny low-pass), softening the edge
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] just enough.
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] Why it helps composite video
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] Composite PAL doesn’t need ultra-sharp digital edges.
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] Slightly slower, cleaner transitions usually look better on screen.
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] Typical values
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Start with 33 Ω
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Try 47 Ω if ringing still visible
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Up to 68 Ω sometimes useful
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Too high can soften detail too much
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] Placement rule
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] Put it as close to the GPIO pin as possible.
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] If far away, it loses much of the damping benefit.
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] If you want, I can mark exactly where it should sit relative to your current 150 Ω path in your
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] next schematic.
2026-04-27 10:56:26 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
