2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 1) Keep RP2350 as main computer (cheapest, fastest to ship)
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - CPU, video, audio, keyboard all on one chip.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Add a cartridge edge connector mapped to:
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Address bus (latched/multiplexed if needed)
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- Data bus
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 	- /ROMCS, /IOCS, /RD, /WR, /IRQ
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Cartridge can be simple ROM, RAM, mapper, or MCU-assisted.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] • Pros:* minimal BOM, reuses your current codebase.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] • Cons:* not “period-authentic” bus timing unless you emulate one.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 2) Z80 + RP2350 as video/sound coprocessor
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Z80 runs classic 8-bit style system.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - RP2350 provides PAL video/audio/PS2 and maybe mass storage.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Cart port is very natural (ROM banking like MSX/Spectrum style).
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] • Pros:* retro cred, huge software/tool familiarity.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] • Cons:* more glue logic, two-CPU integration complexity.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 3) 65C02/65C816 + RP2350 video
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Very clean retro architecture.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Cart port similar to C64/NES-like ROM mapping (conceptually).
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] • Pros:* simple bus, hobby ecosystem.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] • Cons:* software ecosystem smaller than Z80 in some areas.
2026-04-27 11:58:48 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
