2026-04-27 15:05:21 [nitro@ryzen5] for C1 which side connects to pin of RCA?
2026-04-27 15:05:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] For C1 (electrolytic coupling cap):
2026-04-27 15:05:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:05:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] - C1 positive (+) → your circuit side (VID_SUM)
2026-04-27 15:05:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] - C1 negative (−) → RCA center pin (video out)
2026-04-27 15:05:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:05:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] RCA shield goes to GND.
2026-04-27 15:05:25 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:06:17 [nitro@ryzen5] one side has stripe with Oo > o markings
2026-04-27 15:06:21 [gpt-5.3-codex] That striped side is the negative (−) lead on electrolytics.
2026-04-27 15:06:21 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:06:21 [gpt-5.3-codex] So for your C1:
2026-04-27 15:06:21 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Striped side (−) → RCA center pin
2026-04-27 15:06:21 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Other side (+) → VID_SUM (resistor network side)
2026-04-27 15:06:21 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:35:23 [nitro@ryzen5] are you sure, circuit is not working with striped side at pin
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] You’re right to question it — and good catch.
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] For single-supply composite output, the capacitor orientation depends on DC bias at each
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] side. In your case, the circuit side is likely at higher DC than the TV input average, so
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] normally:
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - + to circuit (VID_SUM)
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - − to RCA pin
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] But if it’s not working that way, two likely reasons:
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 1. Your output/DC bias is opposite of assumed in this specific build.
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 2. The TV input clamp/termination behavior is shifting average differently than expected.
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] Quick safe test
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] Use a multimeter on DC volts (no video cable first, then with cable):
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Measure average DC at `VID_SUM` to GND
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Measure average DC at RCA center to GND (connected)
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] If RCA side sits higher DC than VID_SUM, flip cap orientation.
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] Practical recommendation
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] For video coupling, easiest is to avoid polarity issues:
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Use a non-polar/bipolar electrolytic for C1, or
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Two electrolytics back-to-back (temporary hack), or
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] - Large film cap (usually bulky/costly at this value)
2026-04-27 15:35:34 [gpt-5.3-codex] 
