2026-04-29 16:59:33 [scudmarks@discord] i am currently stuck enumerating in udev the device once programmed, tty will work soon
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] I understand the hurdle. After programming the RP2350 (Pico 2) with your vidbit firmware, the
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] device may not automatically appear as a serial tty (e.g., `/dev/ttyACM0`) on Linux until udev
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] properly enumerates it.
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] Here are a few common steps to resolve this:
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 1. Check if the device is detected at all
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4]    Run `dmesg | grep -i usb` right after plugging the Pico in. Look for something like `New USB
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] device found, idVendor=2e8a, idProduct=000a` (Raspberry Pi vendor ID). If you see it but no tty
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] appears, the CDC-ACM driver might not be bound.
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 2. Ensure the firmware enables USB serial
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4]    Your `vidbit.cpp` includes `stdioinitall()` which, by default on RP2350, initialises USB
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] serial (CDC ACM) if using the PICO SDK with `PICOSTDIOUSB=1`. Verify that your
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] `CMakeLists.txt` or build flags include this. Without it, the device will only show as a mass
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] storage device (the bootloader).
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 3. If the device appears as a mass storage device only
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4]    That means the Pico is in bootloader mode (e.g., after a reset while holding BOOTSEL). In that
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] case, you need to either:
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4]    - Reset it (press RUN or unplug/replug) without holding BOOTSEL so it runs your firmware.
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4]    - Or your firmware might be crashing and falling back to the bootloader. Check `led` blinking
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] pattern – your code toggles LED on PIO stall; a fast blink indicates trouble.
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 4. Create a udev rule for permissions (optional but recommended)
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4]    If the tty appears but you can't open it, add a rule like:
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4]    SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="2e8a", ATTRS{idProduct}=="000a", MODE="0666"
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4]    Then reload: `sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger`.
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 5. Check for driver conflicts
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4]    Some systems bind the `cdcacm` module, others might need `usbserial` or `pl2303`. The
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] Pico CDC-ACM usually works out of the box on recent kernels.
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] If you still see no tty, your firmware might not be calling `stdiousbinit()` correctly.
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] Since you're using `stdioinitall()`, ensure the SDK's USB stack is enabled. You can test by
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] adding a simple `printf("hello\n"); sleep_ms(100);` in main and checking `dmesg` for USB descriptor
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] changes.
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] Let me know what `lsusb` and `dmesg` show – that will pinpoint the exact enumeration stage you're
2026-04-29 16:59:48 [deepseek-v4] stuck at.
