2026-04-30 12:48:44 [nitro@ryzen5] on alibaba ecs with extra storage what is difference betwee cloud and elastic?
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] On Alibaba Cloud, when you add extra storage to an ECS instance, you typically use Cloud
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] Disks (block storage volumes). The term "cloud" in this context refers to the product
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] category: persistent, network-attached block storage managed by the cloud platform. The term
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] "elastic" is a general characteristic of cloud resources, meaning you can scale them up or
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] down on demand.
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] There is no separate "Elastic Storage" product for ECS. Instead, Cloud Disks themselves
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] support elastic expansion – you can increase the size of a disk (or upgrade its performance
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] tier) without stopping the instance. So the difference is:
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] - Cloud Disk – the storage resource you provision and attach to an ECS instance.
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] - Elastic – the ability to adapt that storage (e.g., resize, change performance)
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] dynamically, which is a feature of Cloud Disks.
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] If you encountered a UI option that distinguishes "Cloud" vs "Elastic" (e.g., in some translation
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] or third-party tool), it likely refers to the same underlying Cloud Disk, with "Elastic"
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] emphasizing the scaling capability. For all practical purposes on Alibaba Cloud ECS, use Cloud
2026-04-30 12:49:08 [deepseek-v4] Disks for extra storage; elasticity is built in.
2026-04-30 12:49:09 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:50:10 [nitro@ryzen5] the tabs are "Cloud DisK" "Local Disk" and "Elastic Ephemeral Disk"
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] Thank you for the clarification. The tab labels you are seeing are from the Alibaba Cloud ECS
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] console when adding extra storage. My previous answer misinterpreted "elastic" as a general
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] characteristic; now I can give you the precise difference:
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] ╭──────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] │  Tab                         │  Type                                                               │  Persistence                                                                                         │  Attachment                                                    │  Elasticity                                                                               │  Use Cases                                                                                       │
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] ├──────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] │  **Cloud Disk**              │  Network-attached block storage (e.g., ESSD, SSD, Ultra)            │  **Persistent** – data survives instance stop/termination unless you explicitly release the disk.    │  Can be detached from one instance and reattached to another.  │  Supports online resize (increase capacity) and performance tier upgrade.                 │  OS data, databases, long-term storage, stateful applications.                                   │
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] │  **Local Disk**              │  Physically attached disk on the host machine                       │  **Ephemeral** – data is lost when the instance is stopped, released, or moved to a different host.  │  Cannot be detached; permanently tied to the instance.         │  **Fixed size** – no resize possible.                                                     │  High‑throughput temporary data, caches, swap, scratch space.                                    │
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] │  **Elastic Ephemeral Disk**  │  Also physically attached, but with **elastic scaling** capability  │  **Ephemeral** – data is still lost on instance stop/termination.                                    │  Cannot be detached.                                           │  **Supports online expansion** (increase capacity) and sometimes performance adjustment.  │  Similar to Local Disk but need the ability to enlarge storage without recreating the instance.  │
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] ╰──────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] • Key takeaway*
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] - Cloud Disks are the go‑to for persistent, flexible, sharable storage.
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] - Elastic Ephemeral Disks combine the low latency of local storage with the elastic
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] resizing feature of cloud disks, but remain ephemeral – they are best for high‑performance
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] temporary workloads where you might need to increase capacity later without replacing the entire
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] disk.
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] - Ordinary Local Disks lack any elasticity and are the simplest, cheapest option for
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] volatile data.
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] If your workload requires data that survives instance restarts, always choose Cloud Disk.
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] If you need maximum I/O for temporary data and might want to scale capacity later, Elastic
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] Ephemeral Disk is the appropriate choice.
2026-04-30 12:50:25 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:53:48 [scudmarks@discord] what ping tool to work out bottle necks in network connection with host
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] To help diagnose network bottlenecks between your machine and a host, the most effective
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] “ping‑based” tool is mtr (My Traceroute). It combines the functionality of `ping` and
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] `traceroute` into a single diagnostic tool, continuously sending packets to each hop along the path
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] and reporting:
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] - Latency (min, avg, max, standard deviation) per hop
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] - Packet loss per hop
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] - Jitter (latency variation)
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] This makes it far better than plain `ping` for identifying where a bottleneck occurs (e.g.,
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] a specific router dropping packets or adding high latency).
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] Using `mtr`
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] • Installation* (on most Linux distributions):
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] sudo apt install mtr          # Debian/Ubuntu
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] sudo yum install mtr          # RHEL/CentOS/Alibaba Cloud Linux
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] • Basic usage*:
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:54:05 [deepseek-v4] mtr <destination_host>
