2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] Passive bottleneck analysis during rsync
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] ╭───────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] │  Tool                             │  What it does                                                                                                │  How to use alongside rsync                                                                                                                                                               │
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] ├───────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] │  **`rsync --progress`**           │  Shows transfer speed per file. If speed drops at certain times, that indicates a bottleneck.                │  Add `--progress` or `-P` to your rsync command.                                                                                                                                          │
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] │  **`iftop`**                      │  Shows real‑time bandwidth usage per connection.                                                             │  Run `sudo iftop -i eth0` (replace interface) – see which IPs are using how much bandwidth.                                                                                               │
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] │  **`nload`**                      │  Simple live bandwidth graph per interface.                                                                  │  Run `nload eth0`                                                                                                                                                                         │
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] │  **`ss -i`** or **`netstat -s`**  │  Shows TCP statistics (retransmits, congestion window). High retransmits indicate packet loss / bottleneck.  │  While rsync is running, run `ss -i` and look for the rsync socket (or `ss -ti` for TCP info).                                                                                            │
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] │  **`iperf3` in reverse mode**     │  Tests throughput without interrupting rsync if run on a spare port/interface.                               │  On the destination host: `iperf3 -s` on an unused port. On source: `iperf3 -c <dest> -p <port>` – this adds traffic, but you can run it briefly before/after rsync to compare baseline.  │
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] ╰───────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] The most practical approach: run `mtr -c 100 <dest>` for 10–20 seconds while rsync is
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] transferring. It adds < 50 KB total traffic and will show if any hop is losing packets or has high
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] latency. If the bottleneck is within your own link or the remote host’s CPU/disk I/O, `mtr` won’t
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] show it—for that, check CPU/disk/network wait on both ends with `top`, `iostat`, or `dstat`.
2026-04-30 12:56:17 [deepseek-v4] 
