2026-04-30 13:09:01 [scudmarks@discord] rsync multiple files at once, recursive
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] The standard way to transfer multiple files recursively with rsync is to use the `-a` (archive)
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] flag, which implies `-r` (recursive) and preserves metadata. Here is a clear breakdown of how to
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] handle multiple files at once, including optional parallelisation for better throughput over
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] high‑latency links.
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 1. Basic recursive transfer of a directory
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] rsync -av /source/directory/ user@host:/target/directory/
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] - The trailing slash on the source means "copy the contents of the directory". Without it, the
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] directory itself is copied inside the target.
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] - The `-v` flag is for verbose output (you can omit it or use `--progress`).
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 2. Transfer multiple specific files or directories in one command
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] Rsync accepts multiple source paths:
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] rsync -av file1.txt dir1/ dir2/subdir user@host:/target/
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] This copies `file1.txt`, the entire `dir1/`, and `dir2/subdir` into `/target/`.
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] If you need to include/exclude patterns, use `--include` and `--exclude`:
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] rsync -av --include="*.txt" --exclude="*" /source/ user@host:/target/
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 3. Parallel rsync for faster transfers (advanced)
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] Because rsync is single‑threaded per connection, you can run multiple rsync processes in
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] parallel to saturate the link better, especially with high latency and multiple small files.
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] • Using GNU parallel* (install if needed):
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] find /source -maxdepth 1 -type d | parallel -j 4 rsync -av {} user@host:/target/
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] • Using xargs*:
2026-04-30 13:09:19 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] ls -d /source/*/ | xargs -P 4 -I {} rsync -av {} user@host:/target/
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] • Caution*: Parallel transfers can cause contention on the target filesystem. Use with care
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] and only if the bottleneck is bandwidth or latency rather than disk I/O.
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 4. Performance recap for your high‑latency link
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] If you have already applied the TCP buffer tuning from my previous message, the next step is to
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] ensure rsync uses compression (`-z`) and larger socket buffers:
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] rsync -az --progress --sockopts="SO_RCVBUF=134217728,SO_SNDBUF=134217728" /source/ user@43.106.16.226:/target/
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] For a large number of small files, consider `--no-whole-file` (default for local‑to‑local, but for
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] remote transfers the delta algorithm may help). Also try `--partial` to keep partial transfers on
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] interruptions.
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-04-30 13:09:20 [deepseek-v4] 
