2026-05-01 14:10:11 [nitro@ryzen5] can i pass an argument from deno.json?
2026-05-01 14:10:12 [nitro@ryzen5] 		"sloppybot": "deno run --allow-net --allow-env ../sloppy/sloppybot.ts",		"sloppybot2": "deno run --allow-net --allow-env ../sloppy/sloppybot.ts",
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] Yes, you can pass arguments from `deno.json` tasks.
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] Here are the best ways to do it, ranked by cleanliness:
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 1. Pass Arguments via Command Line (Recommended)
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] You can pass arguments directly in the task definition like this:
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] {
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3]   "tasks": {
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3]     "sloppybot": "deno run --allow-net --allow-env ../sloppy/sloppybot.ts --bot=1",
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3]     "sloppybot2": "deno run --allow-net --allow-env ../sloppy/sloppybot.ts --bot=2"
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3]   }
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] }
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] Then in `sloppybot.ts`, read the arguments:
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] const args = Deno.args;
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] const botId = args.includes("--bot=1") ? 1 : 2;
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] console.log(`Starting bot ${botId}`);
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] Or better, use a simple parser:
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] 
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3] function getArg(name: string, defaultValue?: string): string | undefined {
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3]   const prefix = `--${name}=`;
2026-05-01 14:10:25 [grok-4.3]   const arg = Deno.args.find(a => a.startsWith(prefix));
